5300:
** ὗς**, ὗν,
**A**_genitive_ ὑός [ῠ]; or σῦς, σῦν, _genitive_ σῠός, ὁ and ἡ: {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus prefers σῦς, and uses ὗς}} only for the sake of meter: in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus and _Attic dialect_ ὗς}} is the prevailing form, as also at Rhodes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12(1).905, Myconos, “SIG” 1024.16(3rd-2nd c.BC), etc., and ὑῶν ὄρος is an Argive place-name, [prev. work]56.25 (5th c.BC)}}; ὖς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 99 (assuming variant)}}; both forms in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}}, see below; ὗς in {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 462.7 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Lev.11.7, +others (σῦς}} only as see reading in {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Psa.79(80).14), and 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.29.4, 31.14.3, 34.8.8}} (συναγρειον _falsa lectio_ in{{Refs 5th c.BC+|8.26.10, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus -W. ii “Praef.” p.lxxvii)}}; but σῦς (_accusative_ σῦν) in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.34, +others (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}: _plural_, _nominative_ ὕες, σύε; _accusative_ ὕας, σύας, _Attic dialect_ ὗς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166c, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.4.5, 8, “GDI” 5633.9 (from Clazomenae) (σῦς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.107)}}; _genitive_ ὑῶν, συῶ; _dative_ ὑσί (συσί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.783, 7.257)}}, but _Epic dialect_ also ὕεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.410, σύεσσι (see. below)}}:—**the wild swine,** of the **boar,** σῦν ἄγριον ἀργιόδοντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.539, compare 8.338, +others; ἀργοτέρῳ συΐ καπρίῳ 11.293; ἀγροτέροισι σύεσσιν ἐοικότε 12.146; ἀργιόδοντος ὑός 10.264}}; also called σῦς κάπριος or κάπρος, (see entry).; compare also χλούνη; of the **sow,** συὸς ληϊβοτείρης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.29; ὗς ἄγριος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.192, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.28, etc.}}; ὕες (variant{ὗς}) ἄγριαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 578a25.}}
**A.2** of the domesticated animal, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.14}}; the hogs being eaten, ὕες θαλέθοντες ἀλοιφῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.32}}; they were fed on acorns, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.243}}; also on μῆλα πλατανίστινα, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.597; τοκὰς ὗς}}**sus foeta,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 6, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.16; ὗς ἐπίτεξ 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.73.}}
**A.3** proverbs, Βοιωτία ὗς, of stupidity (compare συοβοιωτοί), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.90, compare “fragment” 83}} (σύας); ὗς ποτ᾽ Ἀθαναίαν ἔριν ἤρισεν (or more shortly ἡ ὗς τὴν Ἀθηνᾶν, Latin _sus Minervam,_ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 11)}}, of dunces **setting themselves up** against wise men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.23; οὐκ ἂν πᾶσα ὗς γνοίη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196d}}; ὗς διὰ ῥόδων 'a bull in a china-shop', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 4}}; ὗς ἐκώμασε, of arrogant and insolent behaviour, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Theognostus Grammaticus “Can.” 24}}; ὗς ὑπὸ ῥόπαλον δραμεῖται, of one who runs wilfully into destruction, Dinoloch.14; παχὺς ὗς ἔκειτ᾽ ἐπὶ στόμα (compare βοῦς VIII) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 21}}; λύσω τὴν ἐμαυτῆς ὗν I will give my **rage** vent ('**go the whole hog**'), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 684.}}
**A.II** ={ὕαινα}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 68, 4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 22.1.}}
**A.III** see at {ὕσγη}. (Cf. Latin σῡς, O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **sú, sw-in:** perhaps I.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **sū-s** _feminine_ 'mother', cf. Sanskrit _sū-s_ 'mother', **sū-te** 'bring forth (young)'; change of meaning as in Polish **maciora** (1) 'mother', (2) 'sow', and in Sardinian **mardi** 'sow', from **mater**; Sanskrit _sū-s_ is also _masculine_, and σῦς is difficult.)

5302:
** ὑστερ-έω**,
_future_ **-ήσω**{{LXX|LXX.Psa.83(84).12, +others:}}_aorist_ ὑστέρησα (frequently with variant{ὑστέρισα}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.70, etc.}}: _perfect_ ὑστέρηκα {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.47, NT.Heb.4.1}}: _pluperfect_ ὑστερήκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.31}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ὑστερήθην {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.2Cor.11.9, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 15.6.7}}: (ὕστερος):—**to be behind** or **later, come late,** opposed to προτερέω or φθάνω, ὑστέρησαν οἱ ἄγοντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.70, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 976, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 447a: with _dative_ modi, ὑ. τῇ διώξει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.134; τῇ βοηθείᾳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.3}}: simply, **occur later,** of thunder after lightning, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.46U.}}
**II** with _genitive_ _of things_, **come later than, come too late for,** ὑστέρησαν (variant{ὑστέρισαν}) ἡμέρῃ μιῇ τῆς συγκειμένης **came** one day **after** the appointed day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.89; ὑ. τῆς μάχης ἡμέραις (sic to be read cum codex C 1, pro -ρας) πέντε}} **came too late for** the battle by five days, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.12; ὑστερήσαντες οὐ πολλῷ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.44; ὑ. δείπνου 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 39}}; ἐπειδὴ τῆς Μυτιλήνης ὑστερήκει **had come too late** to save M., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.31}}; ὑ. τῆς πατρίδος **fail to assist** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.1}}; τῶν λέμβων ὑ. **miss** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.101.4; τῶν καιρῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 175a26; τῆς ἐργασίας “PCair.Zen.” 25.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; ταύτης [τῆς ὥρας] {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.362; τῆς βοηθείας 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.110.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_ _person_, **come after** him, ὑ. εἰς Ἁλίαρτον τοῦ Λυσάνδρου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.25}}: also with _dative_, **come too late for** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.29.}}
**II.3** ὑ. ἔς τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.7.3.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **lag behind, be inferior to,** τῶν.. ἀποστόλων {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.2Cor.11.5; ἐμπειρίᾳ ὑ. τῶν ἄλλων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 539e}}; μηδ᾽ ἐν ἄλλῳ μηδενὶ μέρει ἀρετῆς ὑ.{{LXX|[prev. work] 484d; ἵνα γνῶ τί ὑστερῶ ἐγώ LXX.Psa.38(39).5.}}
**III.2** **fall below, fail to do justice to** a theme, ὑστερήσας οὐδὲν τῆς τέχνης {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 60.}}
**IV** **fail to obtain, lack,** τἀγαθοῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Clearchus Comicus 3.5; τοῦ δικαίου “PEnteux.” 86.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; ξύλων ἀκανθίνων οὐχ ὑστεροῦσι, ἀλλ᾽ ἢ ἔχουσιν ἱκανά {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 270.5 (3rd c.BC):}} —_middle_ (with _aorist_ _passive_), ὑστερεῖσθαί τινος {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.71, NT.Rom.3.23, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 15.6.7, “PMasp.” 2 iii 14 (6th c.AD)}}; ὑστερηθεὶς τῆς ὁράσεως **having lost** his sight, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PLond.” 5.1708.85 (6th c.AD)}}; δάνιον δανιεῖς αὐτῷ ὅσον ἂν ἐπιδέηται καὶ καθ᾽ ὅσον ὑστερεῖται {{LXX|LXX.Deu.15.8 (codex A)}}; in _future_ _middle_, παιδὸς ὑστερήσομαι (ἐστερήσομαι corr. Reiske) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1203.}}
**IV.2** _absolutely_, **fail, come to grief,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.50 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; **fall short of supplies,** ἵνα μηθὲν ὑστερῇ τὰ ἐλαιούργια {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.43.6 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_middle_, **to be in want,** {{NT|NT.Luke.15.14, NT.1Cor.8.8}}; _perfect_ _participle_ ὑστερημένοι those **who have failed,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1457.9.}}
**V** of things, **fail, be wanting,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.75.13, NT.John.2.3}}; ἕν σε (variant σοι) ὑστερεῖ {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Mark.10.21; ὡς μὴ ὑστερεῖν τι ὑμῖν τῶν ὑπαρχόντων δικαίων “BGU” 1074.7 (3rd c.AD).—Cf. ὑστερίζω throughout.}}

5306:
** ὕστερος**,
**ὕστᾰτος**, **latter, last,** _comparative_ and _superlative_ without any Posit. _adjective_ in use. (The Posit. must be looked for in Sanskrit _úd_ 'up'; with ὕστερος, ὕστατος cf. Sanskrit _comparative_ and _superlative_ **úttaras, uttamás** 'higher, (later)', 'highest, (latest)'; compare ὑστέρα.)
**A** **ὕστερος**, α, ον, **latter:**
**I** of Place, **coming after, behind,** ὑστέρῳ ποδί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1243, “HF” 1040}}; ὑστέρας ἔχων πώλους keeping them **behind,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 734; ὕ. λόχος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.21; ἐν τῷ ὑ. λόγῳ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.14, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).5, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 503c, etc.}}; τὰ ὕ. the **latter clauses,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.742d (assuming variant, δεύτερα Turnebus): with _genitive_, ὕστεροι ἡμῶν}} **behind** us, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 206e, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.103}}; οὐδὲν ὑστέρα νεώς not a whit **behind** (**slower than**) a ship, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 251.}}
**II** of Time, **next,** ὁ δ᾽ ὕστερος ὄρνυτο χαλκῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.17, 16.479}}; τῷ ὑ. ἔτει in the **next** year, X {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“HG” 7.2.10; τῇ ὑ. Ὀλυμπιάδι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.103}}; ὑ. χρόνῳ in **after** time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.130, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 702 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ἐν ὑ. χρόνοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 865a; ἐν ὑστέραισιν ἡμέραις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1666 (troch.)}}; δεκάτῃ ὑ. or ὑ. δεκάτῃ, on the 21st day, Decrees cited in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.10, compare 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor “Ars Rhetorica” p.192}} H.: with _genitive_, **later than, after,** σεῦ ὕστερος εἶμ᾽ ὑπὸ γαῖαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.333, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 859, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 87c, +others: ὑ. χρόνῳ τούτων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.166, 5.32, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.54.}}
**II.2** **later, too late,** ὕ. ἐλθών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.320; κἂν ὕ. ἔλθῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 691 (_anapaest meter_); μῶν ὕστεραι πάρεσμε; [prev. author] “Lys.” 69; ὑ. ἀφικνεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.90}}; ὕ. (i.e. ἐλθών) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 222, “Tr.” 92; Διονύσιος ὁ ὕ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}} the **second,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1312a4.}}
**II.3** with _genitive_ _of things_, **too late for,** ὕστεροι ἀπικόμενοι τῆς συμβολῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.120; ὕ. ἐλθεῖν τοῦ σημείου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 690 (_anapaest meter_); κακῶν ὕ. ἀφῖγμαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1174; ὕ. ἀφίκοντο τῆς μάχης μιᾷ ἡμέρᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 698e.}}
**II.4** as _substantive_, οἱ ὕ. **posterity,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1225}}; ἄνδρες ὕ., ὕ. βροτοί, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 13, 1245.}}
**III** of inferiority in Age, Worth, or Quality, γένει ὕ., i.e. younger, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.215}}; with _genitive_, οὐδενὸς ὕ. **second** to none, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 181 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.91; γυναικὸς ὕ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 746}}; μηδ᾽ ἔμπροσθεν τῶν νόμων, ἀλλ᾽ ὕ. πολιτεύου not putting yourself above the laws, but **below** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.23}}; σῶμα δεύτερον καὶ ὕ (i.e. ψυχῆς) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 896c}}; νομίσας πάντα ὕστερα εἶναι τἆλλα πρός τι that all things were **secondary** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.41.}}
**III.2** logically **posterior,** ὁ τόπος ὕ. τῆς ὕλης {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.4.12.}}
**IV** _adverb_ ὑστέρως is found only in Eccl. writers, the ascription to Plato by {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Ammonius Grammaticus “περὶ ὁμοίων καὶ διαφόρων λέξεων” p.115V., Thom.Mag. p.284}} R. being now corrected from {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus Ascal. p.405 H., where codices have δευτέρως: the _neuter_ ὕστερον}} was used, rarely of Place, **behind,** ὀπαδεῖν ὕ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 475; ὕ. τῶν ἱππέων γίγνεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.42.}}
**IV.2** of Time, **later, afterwards,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|parm. 8.10, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.91, etc.}}; also τὸ ὕ., opposed to τὸ παλαιόν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 61; ὕστερα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.319}}; frequently with other words, ὕ. αὖτις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.27; οὔποτ᾽ αὖθις ὕ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 858}}; ἔπειτα δ᾽ ὕ., after μέν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 270; εἶτα.. ὕ. [prev. author] 53.4}}; χρόνῳ ὕ. πολλῷ a long time **after,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.171}}; ὕ. χρόνῳ or χρόνῳ ὕ. some time **later,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8, 64; χρόνοις ὕ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.39; βραχεῖ χρόνῳ ὕ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.52; οὐ πολλαῖς ἡμέραις ὕ. [prev. author] “HG” 1.1.1}}; ὀλίγῳ or ὀλίγον ὕ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 327c, “Grg.” 471c; πολλῷ ὕ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58a; οἱ ἄνθρωποι οἱ ὕ.}}**posterity,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 415d}}; τὰ ὕ. γράμματα the **later** inscriptions, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Chrm.” 165a.}}
**IV.2.b** with _genitive_, ὕ. τούτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.113, etc.; ὕ. ἔτι τούτων [prev. author] 9.83}}; τῆς ἐμεωυτοῦ γνώμης ὕ. **after** my own opinion was formed, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.18}}; τοῦ δέοντος ὕ. **later than** ought to be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 57: with _dative_ et _genitive_, ἔτεσι πολλοῖσι ὕ. τούτων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.140, compare 1.91; πολλῷ ὕ. τῶν Τρωϊκῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.22: followed by ἤ, τεσσαρακοστῇ ἡμέρᾳ ὕ... ἢ ποτείδαια ἀπέστη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.60, compare 6.4.}}
**IV.3** in _adverb_ sense with Preps., ἐς ὕστερον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.126, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 351, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.41, 74, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1194, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 720, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 82b, etc.: ἐν ὑστέρῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.13, 8.27: ἐξ ὑστέρου 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.109, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.73; also ἐξ ὑστέρης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108, 5.106, 6.85.}}
**B** **ὕστᾰτος**, η, ον, **last:**
**B.I** of Place, ἅμα θ᾽ οἱ πρῶτοί τε καὶ ὕστατοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.281}}; εὐθυντὴρ ὕστατος νεώς **hindmost,** of a rudder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 717; ἡμῖν τοῖς ὑ. κατακειμενοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 177e.}}
**B.II** of Time, τίνα πρῶτον, τίνα δ᾽ ὕ. ἐξενάριξε; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.299, compare 5.703, E “HF” 485, etc.; ὁ δ᾽ ὕ. γε.. πρεσβεύεται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1300; ἡλίου.. πρὸς ὕ. φῶς [prev. work] 1324}}; τὸν ὕ. μέλψασα γόον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1445; τοὔπος ὕ. θροεῖ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 864}}; ἡ ὑστάτη (i.e. ἡμέρα) τῆς ὁρτῆς the **last day** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.151; ἐν τοῖσιν ὑ. φράσω 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 908}}; οὐκ ἐν ὑστάτοις not among **the last,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1115; οἱ ὕστατοι εἰπόντες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16, etc.}}; ὕστατος ἁλώσιος ἀντάσαις meeting with his downfall **at last,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).41.}}
**B.III** of Rank or Degree, οὐκ ἐν ὑστάτοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 315}}; τὰ ὕ. πάσχειν, like{τὰ ἔσχατα}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Phal.” 1.5.}}
**B.IV** for regular _adverb_ ὑστάτως (which occurs only in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Hippiatrica” 20), the _neuter_ _sing._ and _plural_ are used, πύματόν τε καὶ ὕστατον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.116; ὕστατα καὶ πύματα 4.685, 20.13; νῦν ὕστατα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.232, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.78; ὕστατα ὁρμηθέντες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.43; καὶ πρῶτον καὶ ὕ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 247a}}; ὕ. δή σε προσεροῦσι, τὸ ὕ. προσειπεῖν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 60a, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.30.}}
**B.IV.2** in _adverb_ sense with Preps., ἐν ὑστάτοις **at last,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 620c}}; εἰς τὸ ὕ. **extremely,** γέρων ἐς τὸ ὕ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 9.}}

5308:
** ὑψηλός**, ή, όν
(also -ός, όν Demetr.Troezen. 1 Diels): _comparative_ and _superlative_ -ότερος, -ότατος, and irregular **-έστατος**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.13.9}}: (ὕψι, ὕψος): —**high, lofty,** θάλαμος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.426; πύργος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.384, etc.}}; of a **highland** country, χώρη ὀρεινὴ.. καὶ ὑψηλή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.110; ὑψλὰ χωρία 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.97}}; and ὑψηλά alone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 732c}}; ἐφ᾽ ὑψηλοῦ εἶναι, καθῆσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.4, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 6; ἐν ὑψηλῷ τινι καταστάς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Eumenes” 17; ἀπὸ ὑψηλοῦ κρεμασθείς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175d; ἀφ᾽ ὑψηλοτέρου καθορῶντες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.29}}; ἐποικοδομήσαντες ὑψηλότερον [τὸ τεῖχος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.4}}. _adverb_, -λῶς καθήμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 64.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **high, lofty, stately, proud,** ὄλβος, ἀρεταί, κλέος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.22, 5.1, “P.” 3.111; τέχνη θεσπεσία τις καὶ ὑ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 289e; ὑ. καὶ χαύνη ἐλπίς [prev. author] “Ep.” 341e}}; ὑψηλὰ κομπεῖν talk **high** and boastfully, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1230.}}
**II.2** of persons, opposed to δυσδαίμων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 418; ἀφ᾽ ὑψηλῶν βραχὺν ᾤκισε [prev. author] “Heracl.” 613 (Lyric poetry); ἐπὶ τοῖς ἐμοῖς κακοῖς ὑ. εἶναι [prev. author] “Hipp.” 730; ἐπὶ τούτοις ὑ. ἐξαρεῖν αὑτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 494d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.7, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.174; [δαίμονα] ὑ. αἴρειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 555; τὸ νέον ἅπαν ὑ. καὶ θρασύ 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “fragment” 57; αὑτὸν παρέχειν -ότερον λημμάτων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 25; ὑ. τῷ ἤθει 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Dio” 4.}}
**II.3** **upraised,** i.e. **mighty,** ἐν βραχίονι -λῷ {{LXX|LXX.Exo.6.1, +others}}
**II.4** of poets, **sublime,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 40.2}}; τὰ -ότερα the **loftier, sublimer** thoughts or language, {{Refs|[prev. author] 43.3}}; ὑ. λέξις, λόγος, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 13, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 5}}. _adverb_ **-λῶς**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.12.}}

5310:
** ὕψιστος**, η, ον,
_superlative_ without any Posit. in use: (ὕψι, ὑψοῦ):— **highest, loftiest,** of places, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 720, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.1026, etc.}}; ἐν τοῖς ὑ., i. e. in heaven above, {{NT|NT.Matt.21.9, NT.Luke.2.14.}}
**2** of Zeus, **highest,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.60, 11.2, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 28; Ζηνὸς ὕψιστον σέβας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1289: one of the gates of Thebes was called Ὕψισται}} from his temple there, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.8.5: of Jahweh. ὁ θεὸς ὁ ὕ. LXX.Gen.14.18, cf. LXX.Deu.32.8, “OGI” 96.7 (Egypt, 2nd c.BC), “SIG” 1181 (Rhenea, 2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**3** of things, στέφανος, κέρδος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.100, “I.” 1.51; κακῶν ὕψιστα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 331, 807; ὕ. ἐν βροτοῖς φόβος [prev. author] “Supp.” 479.}}

5311:
** ὕψος**, εος, τό,
(ὕψι) **height,** ὕψος κρεῖσσον ἐκπηδήματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1376; εἰς ὕ. αἴρειν τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 404; κυπαρίττων ὕψη καὶ κάλλη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 625c}}; ὕ. ἔχειν, λαμβάνειν, rise some **height,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.91, 4.13, compare 2.75; ἀφ᾽ ὕψους [με] δισκοβόλησε “epigram Gr.” 336 (Alexandria Troas): _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 44d: _absolutely_ ὕψος}} **in height,** opposed to μῆκος, εὖρος, πλάτος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50, 178, “IG” 12.372.24, 22.1666 A 79, “PMich.Zen.” 38.12, +others (3rd c.BC)}}; so ἐς ὕψος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.13, 155.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **summit, crown,** ὕ. ἀμαθίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 351d; σεμνότητος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 398a12.}}
**II.2** **sublimity, grandeur,** τῶν λόγων {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.8, compare 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 1.1, +others: _plural_, [prev. author] 3.4, 7.4.}}

5312:
** ὑψόω**,
**lift high, raise up,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|Batrachomyomachia 81, “APl.” 4.41 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}:—_middle_, τάφον ὑψώσαντο {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.55 (7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus)}}:—_passive_, μάκελλος ἐκ θεμελίων ὑψοῦτο {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).268.45 (Mantinea, 1st c.BC).}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **elevate, exalt,** opposed to ταπεινόω, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Exo.15.2, +others, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.26.12, NT.Matt.23.12, +others}}; πονοῦντα τὸν ἴδιον ὑψῶσαι βίον [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus] cited in 2nd-3rd c.AD: Clemens Alexandrinus Theologus “Stromateis” 5.14.120}}:—_passive_, **to be exalted,** μὴ ὑψωθῇς τῇ καρδίᾳ {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Deu.8.14; τὰ χθαμαλὰ ὑψοῦται 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.103e; ὑ. κάλλεϊ “Anthologia Graeca” 5.91 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus)}}; ἐκ ποδὸς ὑψεύμενοι, of suddenly **exalted** persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 7.}}
**II.2** **represent in the 'grand manner',** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 14.1.}}
**II.3** _passive_, Astron., of planets, **mount** to the north of the ecliptic, Theo Sm.p.135 H.
**II.4** _passive_, Astrology texts, of planets, **attain exaltation,** i.e. maximum appotelesmatic efficacy, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 140.7.}}

5313:
** ὕψ-ωμα**, ατος, τό,
**elevation, height,** οὐ χθὼν οὐρανίοις ὑψώμασι [φθονέει] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 73; ὕ. τοῦ ἀέρος 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.408}}; τὸ ὕ. τῆς ῥινός the **bridge** of the nose, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).796,806.}}
**2** Astrology texts, **exaltation** of a heavenly body, opposed to ταπείνωμα, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.149a, 782d, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 5.33, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 37.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **exaltation,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 92.29.}}

5315:
** φᾰγεῖν**,
_infinitive_ of ἔφαγον, with no _present_ in use (except in late Gr., φαγεῖ {{Refs|Anonymus “in EN” 448.16}}; φαγέοις in _editions_ of {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 157 is see uncertain)}}, used as _aorist 2_ of ἐσθί; later _1st pers._ _plural_ ἐφάγαμεν {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.19.42 codex B, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐφάγοσανLXX.Gen.18.8: later}}_future_ is φάγομαι,{{LXX+NT|LXX.Sir.36.23, NT.Luke.14.15}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ φάγεσαι {{LXX+NT|LXX.Ruth.2.14, NT.Luke.17.8; φάγῃ LXX.Gen.3.14; also φαγοῦμαι [prev. work] 2 (variant)}}; _future_ φαγήσω is see uncertain in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 53.29}}:—**eat, devour,** both of men and beasts, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus; ἀζηχὲς φαγέμεν καὶ πιέμεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.3, compare 15.378, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 831e; reversely, πιόντα ἢ φαγόντα [prev. author] “Prt.” 314a, compare “Phd.” 81b, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 336 (uncertain reading): mostly with _accusative_, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.127, 24.411, etc.}}: with _genitive_, **eat of** a thing, {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.102, 15.373; ὄρνιθος ὄρνις πῶς ἂν ἁγνεύοι φαγώ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 226; ἀπό τινος LXX.Gen.2.16.}}
**II** **eat up, devour, squander,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.76, 4.33. (Cf. παματοφαγέω}} and Sanskrit _bhájati_ 'apportion, (_middle_) enjoy'.)

5316:
** φαίνω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.102, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ also **φαείνω** (which see):—_future_ φᾰνῶ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 304.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 827, etc. (φᾱνῶ _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 187.26, but φᾱνῶ, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 300, and ἀναφᾱνῶ, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 528, are uncertain)}}; _Ionic dialect_ φᾰνέω (ἀπο-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Steril.” 213}}, _optative_ φᾰνοίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 313 (codex recentia, rightly)}}: _aorist 1_ ἔφηνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.318, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ ἔφᾱνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).2, “IG” 42(1).123.28 (Epid., 4th c.BC), also later _Attic dialect_}}_subjunctive_, ἀπο-φάνῃ uncertain reading in {{Refs|“IG” 22.1631.379 (= 2.811c133)}}; φάνῃς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 233 (ἐκ-φάνῃς 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 418 = 4th-3rd c.BC: Chares Iambographus 4b20)}}; so in late Prose, (ἐξ-) {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 12.33, (ἐπι-) NT.Luke.1.79, (ἀνα-) NT.Act.21.3}}; _Epic dialect_ _iterative_ φάνεσκε (_intransitive_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.64, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 14.3}}: _perfect_ πέφαγκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 2.10, (ἀπο-) 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.15, +others: _intransitive_}}_perfect_ πέφηνα (see. below{{Refs|A 111.2)}}, _Doric dialect3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐκ-πεφάναντι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 83}}; _pluperfect_ ἐπεφήνειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 46.10}}:—_middle_, _future_ _infinitive_ φᾰνεῖσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.230}}, _Ionic dialect_ φᾰνέομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.35; _optative_ φανοῖσθε 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.10 (nisi to be read φανεῖσθε)}}; the forms φανῆσθον and _Doric dialect_ _imperative_ φάνευ are corrupt in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 399e, 3rd c.BC: Teles Philosophus p.58 H. (to be read φαίνευ):}}_aorist 1_ ἐφηνάμην (_transitive_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 944, (ἀπ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.52, etc.}}:—_passive_, _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ φαινέσκετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.194}}: _future_ φᾰνήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.108, Sicilian _Doric dialect_ (_infinitive_) φᾰνήσειν (perhaps -ησεῖν) 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “A ren.” 4.20; _Epic dialect_}}_future_ πεφήσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.155}}: _aorist 1_ ἐφάνθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 263 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 525, etc.:rare in Prose, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.11, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.13, (ἀπο-) “IG” 12.10.35, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.44}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ φαάνθη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.650}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ φάανθεν {{Refs|1.200:}}_aorist 2_ ἐφάνην [ᾰ], _Epic dialect_ φάνην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.477, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ φάνεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.68}}; _Epic dialect_ _subjunctive_ φανήῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.375}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ φανήμεναι {{Refs|9.240}}: _perfect_ πέφασμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1543}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ πέφανται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.122, 16.207, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.115, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 374 (Lyric poetry)}}; πέφᾰται in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 9.52, Perictione Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.28.19 belongs either to φαίνω in sense 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.5}}, or to φημ; _infinitive_ πεφάνθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 294a, etc.}}; _participle_ πεφασμένος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.127, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 227, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 843, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1122, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 245e, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _pluperfect_ ἐπέφαντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 166.}}
**A** _active_, **bring to light, cause to appear,** in physical sense, τέρας τινὶ φ. **make** a sign **appear** to one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.324, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.173, etc.; σήματα φαίνων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.353; γένυσι φ. ὀπώραν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.6; δύο μορφὰς φ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 304.5; τὸν αὐχένα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.132}}; ἔφην᾽ ἄφαντον φῶς, i.e. fire, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 297; λαμπάδας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1524 (_anapaest meter_); φ. θησαυρόν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 565}}; φ. μηρούς, ἐπιγουνίδα, **show by baring,** i.e. **uncover**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.67, 74; φαίνοισα πρόσωπον ἀλάθεια 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.17}}; **reflect** an image in water, τὰ δέ νιν καλὰ κύματα φαίνει {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.11:}} —_middle_, τὰ τόξα.. τοῖσιν Ἀργείοισι φήνασθαι θέλει **exhibit** them **as his own,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 944.}}
**b** **make known, reveal, disclose,** ἐς τὸ φῶς φανεῖ κακά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 1229; κακῶν ἔκλυσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 898 (Lyric poetry, probably); τὸν μιαρὸν τῷ χρόνῳ ἀποδόντες φῆναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.4.11; ὁδόν τινι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.334; τὰ ὀνείρατα καὶ τὸν πόρον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.13, compare “Cyr.” 6.4.13, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 725; τοῖς πολεμίοις σύνθημα 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.10, etc.}}; φανεῖ.. κωκύματα wailings **will show forth** [the truth of what I say], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1078: with a predicate added, ἡμᾶς σὺ δειλοὺς φανεῖς}} wilt **make** us **appear**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1362; τὸν Ααΐου φόνον φανεῖ δικαίως ὀρθόν [prev. author] “OT” 853.}}
**c** γόνον Ἑλένῃ φ. **show** her a child, i. e. **grant** her to bear one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.12}}; φ. παράκοιτίν τινι **show** (i.e. **give**) one a wife, {{Refs|15.26.}}
**2** of sound, **make** it **clear** to the ear, **make** it **ring clear,** ἀοιδὴν φαίνειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|8.499; σάλπιγξ.. ὑπέρτονον γήρυμα φαινέτω στρατῷ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 569.}}
**3** **show forth, display** in action, ἀρετήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.237; ἀεικείας 20.309; βίην 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 689; εὐμαχανίαν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).2; εὔνοιαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.36}}; ὕβριν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 127; ὀργάς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 326 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3.b** **set forth, expound,** νοήματα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.295; λόγον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116; τριφασίας λόγων ὁδούς [prev. author] 1.95}}; but τὰ λαμπρὰ.. φ. ἔπη **make** them **good,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 721.}}
**4** **inform against** one, **denounce,** φανῶ σε (σε φανῶ codices) τοῖς πρυτάνεσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 300 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ach.” 824, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 325}}: **denounce** a thing **as contraband,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 542, 819, +others; φαίνειν πλοῖον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.9}}; τὰ φανθέντα articles **denounced** as contraband, {{Refs|[prev. work] 13: _absolutely_}}**give information,** ὁ φήνας ἢ ὁ γραψάμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.45.3, compare 4.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.14, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.207 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, etc.}}
**5** φαίνειν φρουράν, **call up** a levy, at Sparta, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.23, +others}}; also φ. θυσίαν **proclaim, order** a sacrifice, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Philodamus Scarpheus Lyricus 112:}} —_passive_, πέφαται θνατοῖσι νίκας ὕστερον εὐφροσύνα has been **ordained,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 9.52.}}
**II** _absolutely_, **give light, shine,** φαίνοντες νύκτας.. δαιτυμόνεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.102, compare 19.25}}; of the sun, moon, etc., φ. τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 586 (troch.); εἰς ἅπαντα φ. τὸν οὐρανόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 39b; ἀλλά, σελάνα, φαῖνε καλόν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.11; οἱ λύχνοι φ. ἧττον 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 11}}; compare φάω: so ἦρι μὲν φαίνοντι in spring **when it shines forth,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 304.4 codices (to be read φανέντι)}}; of the Dioscuri **shining** in mid-air, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1234 (_anapaest meter_): _metaphorically_, ἀγανὴ φαίνουσ᾽ ἐλπίς}} soft **shining** hope, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 101 (_anapaest meter_, uncertain).}}
**II.b** Φαίνων, οντος, ὁ, the planet **Saturn,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 392a23, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 2.20.52, etc.; Φ. ὁ τοῦ Ἡλίου 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus “Ars astronomica” 5.19}}; _accusative_ **-ωνα** {{Refs|“Placita Philosophorum” 2.15.4.}}
**III** _Epic dialect_ _iterative_ φάνεσκε **appeared,** μετὰ πρώτοισι φάνεσκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.64; ὑπένερθε δὲ γαῖα φάνεσκε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.242, compare 11.587, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 14.3.}}
**III.2** _perfect_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2 πέφηνα is also used _intransitive_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 328, etc.; less frequently in Prose, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.120, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.22, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.13.8.}}
**B** _passive_, **come to light, appear,** φάνεν δέ οἱ εὐρέες ὦμοι, being stripped bare, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.68, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.324, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.39}}: frequently of fire, **shine brightly,** πυρὰ φαίνετο Ἰλιόθι πρό {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.561; ἕκαθεν δέ τε φαίνεται αὐγή 2.456; δεινὼ δέ οἱ ὄσσε φάανθεν}}**shone like fire,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.200}}: frequently of the **rising** of heavenly bodies, ἄστρα φαεινὴν ἀμφὶ σελήνην φαίνετ᾽ ἀριπρεπέα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.556, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 598}}; of the first **gleam** of daybreak, ἦμος δ᾽ ἠριγένεια φάνη ῥοδοδάκτυλος Ἠώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.477, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.1, +others}}; ἅμ᾽ ἠοῖ φαινομένηφι at **break** of day, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.618, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.407, +others; ἀκτὶς ἀελίου, τὸκάλλιστον.. φανὲν.. τῶν προτέρων φάος, ἐφάνθης ποτ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 100 (Lyric poetry)}}: of a **rising** wind, οὐδέ ποτ᾽ οὖροι πνείοντες φαίνονθ᾽ ἁλιαέες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.361}}; of a vapour, ἐκ νεφέων ἐρεβεννὴ φ. ἀήρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.864.}}
**B.2** of persons, οἴῳ φαινομένη **appearing** to him alone, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.198, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.517, etc.; ἐφάνη λὶς εἰς ὁδόν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.275}}; οὗπερ κἀφάνης where **thou didst first appear,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 77; χρόνιος φανείς [prev. author] “Ph.” 1446 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ὁδόν φανῆναι a pregnant expression for ἐλθεῖν ὁδὸν ὥστε φανῆναι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 1274 (Lyric poetry); κέλευθον φανείς “Aj.” 878 (Lyric poetry)}}; πόθεν φαίν; whence **come you?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 309a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.8.1}}; οὐδαμοῦ φ. **is** nowhere **to be seen,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 1.10.16.}}
**B.2.b** **come into being,** φανεὶς δύστηνος **born** to misery, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 974, compare 1225 (Lyric poetry)}}; **become,** ἐκ βασιλέως ἰδιώτην φανῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.28; δυοῖν ἐφάνη τριήραρχος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.104}}; **to be made out,** δοῦλος λόγοισιν.. φανείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1020, compare 1241.}}
**B.3** of events, **come about,** τέλος οὔ πώ τι πέφανται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.122; φάνη βιότοιο τελευτή 7.104}}; ἔργον, ἄεθλον, etc.,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|16.207, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.106, etc.; τὸ φανθέν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 743}}; of sayings, **to be set forth,** λόγος ἀρχαῖος φανείς{{Refs|[prev. work] 1, compare “OT” 474 (Lyric poetry), 848.}}
**B.II** **appear to be** so and so, with _infinitive_, δμῳάων ἥ τίς τοι ἀρίστη φαίνεται εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.25, compare 11.336; οὐ γάρ σφιν ἐφαίνετο κέρδιον εἶναι 14.355; τοῦτό μοι θειότατον φαίνεται γενέσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.137; εὖ σὺ λέγειν φαίνει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 403 (_anapaest meter_), compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 319, etc.}}: frequently with _infinitive_ omitted, οὗ καὶ πρόσθεν ἀρίστη φαίνετο βουλή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.94, compare 2.5; ὅς τις φαίνηται ἄριστος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.106; σμερδαλέος αὐτῇσι φάνη 6.137; ἕρμαιον ἂν ἐφάνη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 368d, etc.: but in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, etc.}} also with _participle_, **to be manifest**: thus, ἐμοὶ σὺ πλουτέειν μέγα φαίνεαι you **appear** to me to be very rich, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32}}; but εὔνοος ἐφαίνετο ἐών he was **manifestly** well-inclined, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.173, compare 175, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2}}; οὐκ ἄκαιρα φαίνεται λέγειν he **appears** to be speaking.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1036}}; but φανέονται λέγοντες οὐδέν it **will be manifest** that they talk nonsense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.35; φαίνομαι δύο καθορᾶν εἴδη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 235d; but οὐκ ἂν φανεῖμεν πήματ᾽ ἔρξαντες 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 786; πλαγκτὸς οὖσ᾽ ἐφαινόμην [prev. author] “Ag.” 593, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.89, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 343; ἐφάνησαν πεπονθότες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22c: with}}_participle_ omitted, πέφανται ἁρματηλάτας σοφός (i.e. ὤν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.115, compare “N.” 6.14; κρατηθεὶς ἡμερώτερος φανεῖ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1632}}; Κᾶρες ἐφάνησαν (i.e. ὄντες) **they were seen** to be Carians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8}}; τί φαίνομαι (i.e. ὤν) δῆτ; what **do I look like?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 925; ὡς ἀγαθοὶ.. ἐφάνησαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 408a: hence φαίνεσθαι, opposed to εἶναι, εἶναι μὲν ὅσπερ εἰμί, φαίνεσθαι δὲ μή 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 698 (cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 441); στρατηγὸς ἀγαθὸς μὴ ὢν φαίνεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.7.3; ὀλίγοι καὶ ὄντες καὶ φαινόμενοι [prev. author] “HG” 6.5.28.}}
**B.II.2** in Philosophy, φαίνομαι (_absolutely_) is sometimes used of what **appears** to the senses, φαίνεται δ᾽ οὐδὲν **is observed,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 204b35, compare “Cael.” 312b30; φ. κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν [prev. author] “GA” 716a31}}: sometimes of what **is** mentally **manifest,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1175a29}}; **to be evident,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “APr.” 24b24}}: especially **appear** to the imagination {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (φαντασία 2), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 264b; φ. καὶ μύουσιν ὁράματα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 428a16; φ. δέ τι.. οἷον τὰ ἐν τοῖς ὕπνοις [prev. work] a7: distinguished from αἰσθάνεσθαι and δοξάζειν, [prev. work] b1}}: especially in _participle_ φαινόμενος, η, ον:
**B.II.2.a** **appearing** in sense experience, τὰ φ. κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cael.” 303a22, +others; εἴτε τὰ δοκοῦντα πάντα ἐστὶν ἀληθῆ καὶ τὰ φ. [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1009a8, compare “de An.” 404a29 (_sing._); τὰ φ.}}**sense-data,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “PA” 639b8, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1pp.9,10 U., +others: Astron., τὰ φ.}} = celestial **phenomena,** title of a work by Eudoxus, versified by Aratus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.1.8, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 293b27; πρός τινας δόξας αὑτῶν τὰ φ. προσέλκοντες [prev. work] 293a26}}: generally, τὸ μὴ ἐκ φαινομένων τὸ βλεπόμενον γεγονέναι {{NT|NT.Heb.11.3.}}
**B.II.2.b** mentally **apparent,** opposed to ὄντα τῇ ἀληθείᾳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 596e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 100b24, “EN” 1113a24; τὰ οὖν ἐμοὶ φαινόμενα οὕτω φαίνεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 517b; [νοῦς] τῶν φ. θειότατον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1074b16}}; τὸ φ. εἰπεῖν to express one's **opinion,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.158c}}: hence, **specious, fallacious,** φ. ἐνθυμήματα, opposed to ὄντα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1402a28.}}
**B.II.2.c** τὰ φ. **what is to be seen, show,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 30.28.}}
**B.II.3** frequently in answers in Plato's dialogue, φαίνεται, **yes, Prt.**{{Refs|332e, “R.” 333c, +others; ὥς γέ μοι φ. “Prt.” 324d, compare “R.” 383a, +others}}: [τοῦτο] φῂς εἶνα; Answ. φαίνομαι (i.e. λέγειν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.20.}}
**B.II.3.b** later _impersonal_ with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **it seems good,** ἐάν σοι φαίνηται Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 304.11 (3rd c.BC),compare “PCair.Zen.” 44.7, 16 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; ὁπότε αὐτῷ φανείη στρατιὰν ἐξάγειν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.14, compare 4.85.}}
**B.II.4** joined with δοκέω, εἰ δὴ κακός τε φ. δοκῶ τέ σοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1071; δοκοῖμεν ἂν.. χείρους φαίνεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.122, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 269d, “Erx.” 399c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.22.}}
**B.II.5** οὐδαμοῦ φαίνεσθαι 'to be nowhere', _metaphorically_ from racing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 72c, compare “Grg.” 456b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.310.}}
**B.III** τὰ φανθέντα, see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.4.}}

5318:
** φᾰνερός**, ά, όν,
but ός, όν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 991 (Lyric poetry)}}: (φαίνω):— **visible, manifest,** ἡ στήλη ἔχει πάντα φ., i.e. all that is in it can be plainly seen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.24; φ. ὄμμασιν ἐμοῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 501; φ. τι δεῖξαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 608 (variant); θήσω φανέρ᾽ ἀθρό᾽ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.98; φ. ποιῆσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 630b, etc.; ἐς φ. ὄψιν βαίνειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1236 (_anapaest meter_); τοὔργον παρέσται φ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1291; φ. χαρακτὴρ ἀρετᾶς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 658 (Lyric poetry); φ. πηγαί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15; ἐσβολαὶ ἐς Αἴγυπτον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.5; φ. ἔχθραν κτήσασθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.42; διαφορὰ φ. ἐγένετο [prev. work] 102}}; φ. θάνατος, ὄλεθρος, opposed to ἀφανής, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.53; φ. ὑποψία εἰς ἐμὲ ἰοῦσα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.6; φ. γενόμενος}}**if detected,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.12:}} — Constr.: φανερός εἰμι with _participle_, ἀπικόμενοι φανεροί εἰσι they are **known** to have come, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.26; ἐπισπεύδων φανερὸς ἦν [prev. author] 7.18; ὁ μέν ἐστι φ. ἐκβὰς ἐκ τοῦ πλοίου καὶ οὐκ εἰσβὰς πάλιν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.23: followed by _conjunction_, φανεροὶ γιγνόμενοι ὅτι ποιοῦσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.12; φ. ἦν ὅπως ἐγίγνωσκεν [prev. author] “Mem.” 1.1.17: _impersonal_, φανερόν [ἐστιν] ὅτι.. [prev. work] 3.9.2}}; εἰ φανερὸν γίγνοιτο ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 70d.}}
**2** **shining, illustrious,** προεδρίη {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 2.7; ὁδός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.73}}; **conspicuous, remarkable,** φ. μηδὲν κατεργάζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.17.}}
**3** φ. οὐσία **real** property, opposed to personalty (ἀφανής), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.4, “fragment” 79, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.8.}}
**3.b** property **in possession** (opposed to **in action**), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.118, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.30, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.7.}}
**3.c** **in hand, in cash,** μηδὲν φανερὸν κεκτῆσθαι to have no **cash in hand,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.70; λαβὼν ἀργύριον φ. καὶ ὁμολογούμενον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.1; πόρος φ. [prev. author] 14.24; φ. οὐσία [prev. author] 27.57; φ. χρήματα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.83; φ. ποιεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.4}}; φανερόν τι **a certain sum of money,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 330, Scholia 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.102.}}
**4** of votes, φ. ψήφῳ by **open** vote, opposed to κρύβδην (ballot), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.82, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 68.2; ψῆφον φ. διενεγκεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.74; τὴν ψῆφον φ. φέρειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 767d; φ. ἡ ψῆφος τιθεμένη [prev. work] 855d.}}
**5** _adverb_ -ρῶς **openly, manifestly,** βουλόμενος φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.71; στείχειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1090 (_anapaest meter_); οἴχεσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 833 (Lyric poetry); ἀκοῦσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 291 (_anapaest meter_); ἀποδείκνυσθαι τὴν γνώμην 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.87}}; φ. ἐρᾶν, opposed to λάθρᾳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 182d; τὸ φ. ἐξεῖναι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.3}}: _comparative_, φανερώτερον ἐκπολεμεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.91}}; **-τέρως** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 657a2: but}}
**5.b** τὸ φ. frequently with Preps. in _adverb_ sense, ἐκ τοῦ φ. **openly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.96, 8.126}}; πολέμιος οὐκ ὢν ἐκ τοῦ φ. not **openly declared,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.79; ἐκ τοῦ φ. τὴν μάχην ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.16; ἐκ τοῦ φ. ἀποφεύγειν [prev. author] “Mem.” 3.11.8; ἀπὸ τοῦ φ. 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.4; also ἐν τῷ φ. σαυτὸν παρεῖχες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.55; ἀκοῦσαι ἐν τῷ φ. [prev. author] “An.” 1.3.21; βουλεύεσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.235 (rarely ἐν φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 5.7); ἐς τὸ φ. ἀποδῦναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6}}; αἱ ἐς τὸ φ. λεγόμεναι αἰτίαι, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.23}}; τὸν σῖτον φέρειν ἐς τὸ φ. into **public,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.27, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 480c, etc.; εἰπεῖν κατὰ τὸ φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 525 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐπὶ φανεροῖς ξυνελθεῖν on **public, acknowledged** terms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69.}}
**II** of persons, **manifest, conspicuous,** εἰ [Διόνυσος καὶ Πὰν] φ. ἐγένοντο ἐν τῇ Ἑλλάδι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.146; φανερὰ.. ἦλθε κόρα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 507 (Lyric poetry); Κύπρις.. φανερὰ τῶνδ ἐφάνη πράκτωρ [prev. author] “Tr.” 861 (Lyric poetry); πάντων -ώτατος Βρασίδας ἐγένετο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.11, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.58; οἱ -ώτεροι}}**persons of distinction,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 2.20.}}
**II.2** **open, frank,** opposed to ἐπίβουλος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1149b15.}}
**III** = τις, φ. χρέα **certain** debts, Mitteis {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Chr.” 71.3 (5th c.AD),compare “PMasp.” 167.10, 194.5 (6th c.AD), etc.}}

5319:
** φᾰνερ-όω**,
**make manifest,** σεαυτὸν τῷ κόσμῳ {{NT|NT.John.7.4, compare 2.11, NT.2Cor.5.10 (_passive_)}}; **reveal, make clear,** τινὶ τὴν ἀλήθειαν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“POxy.” 925.4 (5th c.AD)}}; ἀληθῆ πεφανέρωται **have been demonstrated,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus Alch.p.88 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**II** **make known** or **famous,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.37}}:—_passive_, **become so,** ἐφανερώθη ἐς τοὺς Ἕλληνας δαπάνῃσι μεγίστῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.122.}}

