5321:
** φᾰνέρ-ωσις**, εως, ἡ,
={σημασία}{{Refs 5th c.AD+|1, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (φ. διὰ σάλπιγγος 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus).}}
**2** **disclosure,** ἡ ἀπὸ τοῦ οἴνου φ. τοῦ λογισμοῦ {{Refs|Anonymus “Incred.” 17.}}
**II** **manifestation,** τοῦ πνεύματος {{NT|NT.1Cor.12.7, cf. NT.2Cor.4.2.}}
**II.2** Astron., **becoming visible,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Julianus Laodicensis Astrologus (?) in “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 5(1).189(_plural_).}}

5322:
** φᾱνός**, ὁ,
**torch,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 308; ὑπὸ φανοῦ πορεύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 5.7}}; ποῖος φ. τοιοῦτος οἷος ὁ ἥλιο; {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 87}}; distinguished from λύχνος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 48, compare 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 40}}; but probably ={λύχνος} in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 5.18, 6.15 (2nd c.BC), “Stud.Pal.” 10.251.2 (6th c.AD)}}; μετὰ φανῶν καὶ λαμπάδων {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.John.18.3, compare “PLond.” 3.1159.59 (2nd c.AD)}}: the form **πᾱνός** (probably a different word) is found in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 284, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 184, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 195 (Lyric poetry), “fragment” 90, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 6, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 62.}}
**II** φ. ὑελοῦς glass **cover,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Olymp.Alch. p.75 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**II.2** a form of **still,** Zos.Alch.p.224 {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}

5324:
** φαντάζω**,
(φαίνω) **make visible, present to the eye** or **mind,** τι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Problemata - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 2.18, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus: but, φ. τὴν αἴσθησιν}} **deceive,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Callistratus Sophista “Statuarum descriptiones” 14}}:—_middle_, with _aorist_ _passive_, **place before one's mind, picture** an object **to oneself, imagine,** τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 15.2, 8, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 10.28, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 33, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 3.20, Chor. p.16 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus; χωρίον [prev. author] “Milt.” 29 F.-R.; κύνα ἐν ὕδατι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 19 (“Hermes” 43.68); περί τῆς φύσεως 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 14.24}}; **fancy, imagine,** ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.5.6; ὄναρ ἐφαντάσθην 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 15}}; τοῖς μηδὲ φαντασθεῖσιν ὡς καλόν.. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.6.4}}; with _accusative_ and _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|Polem. Phgn. 36, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 2.10.}}
**II** in early writers only in _passive_, _future_ φαντασθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211a}}: _aorist_ ἐφαντάσθην {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 51a}}:— **become visible, appear,** σφι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.124; ὄνειρον φαντάζεταί μοι [prev. author] 7.15; μηδὲ φαντάζου δόμων πάροιθε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 876, compare “Ph.” 93; φ. ἄλλοτε ἐν ἄλλαις ἰδέαις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 380d}}; also, **to be heard,** μυκαὶ σηκοῖς ἔνι φ. {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1285; φοβερὸν ἦν τὸ -όμενον NT.Heb.12.21.}}
**II.b** **to be terrified by visions** or **phantasms,** {{Refs|PMag.Lond. 121.888.}}
**II.2** **make a show,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10. ε᾽.}}
**II.3** φαντάζεσθαί τινι **make oneself like** someone, **take** his **form,** φανταζόμενος.. γυναικί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1500 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.4** **appear** so and so, **to be imagined,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 300c; ἡδοναὶ φαντασθεῖσαι [prev. author] “Phlb.” 51a; τῷ νοηθῆναι ἢ φαντασθῆναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 433b12.}}
**II.5** com. for συκοφαντεῖσθαι, **to be informed against,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 823 (Megarian dialect φαντάδδομαι).}}

5325:
** φαντᾰσί-α**, ἡ,
verbal noun of φαντάζομαι and (in sense) of φαίνομαι, **appearing, appearance,** ={τὸ φαίνεσθαι}, πάντες ἐφίενται τοῦ φαινομένου ἀγαθοῦ, τῆς δὲ φ. οὐ κύριοι do not control the **appearing,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1114a32}}; usually with less verbal force, **appearance, presentation** to consciousness, whether immediate or in memory, whether true or illusory, φαίνεται μὲν ὁ ἥλιος ποδιαῖος, ἀντίφησι δὲ πολλάκις ἕτερόν τι πρὸς τὴν φ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Insomn.” 460b19}}; ἡ τοῦ γάλακτος φ. the **appearance** of the milky way, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mete.” 339a35; ἡ τοῦ προσώπου φ. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.50}} M.; especially of visual **images,** ἐπεὶ ἡ ὄψις μάλιστα αἴσθησίς ἐστι, καὶ τὸ ὄνομα ἀπὸ τοῦ φάους εἴληφεν [ἡ φ.] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 429a2}}; κατοπτρικὴ φ. **image** reflected in a mirror, {{Refs|“Placita Philosophorum” 3.1.2}}; also of other sense=perceptions, φ. καὶ αἴσθησις ταὐτὸν ἔν τε θερμοῖς καὶ πᾶσι τοῖς τοιούτοις **appearance** is the same as perception, whether we are talking of hot things or of anything else like them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152c, compare 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.21; ταῦτα ἔστι μέν τι, ἀλλ᾽ οὐχ ὧν ἐμποιεῖ τὴν φ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1024b24; ἡ φ. ἐστὶν αἴσθησίς τις ἀσθενής.. κἂν τῷ ἐλπίζοντι ἀκολουθοῖ ἂν φ. τις οὗ ἐλπίζει [prev. author] “Rh.” 1370a28; αἱ [αἰσθήσεις] ἀληθεῖς ἀεί, αἱ δὲ φ. γίνονται αἱ πλείους ψευδεῖς [prev. author] “de An.” 428a12; φ. ἀληθεῖς ἁπάσας 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 254; ἀπελθόντων τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἔνεισιν αἱ αἰσθήσεις καὶ φ. ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητηρίοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 425b25}}; διὰ τὸ ἐμμένειν [τὰς φ.] καὶ ὁμοίας εἶναι ταῖς αἰσθήσεσι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 429a5; τῆς αἰσθήσεως ἀλλοιουμένης ἐξ ἧς γίνεσθαι τὴν φ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 63; ἐλέγχειν τὰς ἀλλήλων φ. καὶ δόξας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161e}}; frequently in later Philos. especially in meaning psychic **image,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.12U., 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.152, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 4.24, +others; defined as τύπωσις ἐν ψυχῇ 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.23; φ. καταληπτική 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.17, etc.; [φ. κ.] ἢν κριτήριον εἶναι τῶν πραγμάτων φασί, τὴν γιγνομένην ἀπὸ ὑπάρχοντος κατ᾽ αὐτὸ τὸ ὑπάρχον ἐναπεσφραγισμένην “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.21, compare 26, +others}}; διανοητικαὶ φ. mental **images,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Familiares” 15.16.1; νυκτεριναὶ φ. 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus in “de An.” 486.13,compare “Geoponica” 12.17.15}}; **apparition,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 846a37.}}
**b** less scientifically, **appearance,** ἐμποιοῦντα τὴν φ. (i.e. τοῦ ἐλέγχειν) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “SE” 165b25; τὸ παράδοξον τῆς τῶν ζῴων φ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.53.8, compare 5.48.9, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.102, “Hann.” 15; κατὰ τὴν πρώτην φ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.27.7}}; συναύξειν τὴν φ. [τῆς νίκης] {{Refs|[prev. author] 16.8.3; δουλεύοντες τῇ τῶν ἐκτὸς φ. [prev. author] 30.19.4; φ. ποιεῖν καὶ προσδοκίαν [prev. author] 18.10.7, compare 14.2.4}}; ζῷα.. μέχρι φ. φαινόμενα (in a conjuring trick) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Celsus Medicus cited in 3rd c.AD: Origenes Theologus “contra Celsum” 1.68; κατὰ τὴν πρόχειρον οὑτωσὶ φ. 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.105, compare 15.17,115, 19.206; τῶν ἀπεπτούντων ἐνίοις φ... γίνονται [prev. author] 18(2).73, compare 71, +others}}
**2** **imagination,** i.e. the **re-presentation** of appearances or images, primarily derived from sensation {{Refs|compare (αἴσθησις 11), ὅταν μὴ καθ᾽ αὑτὸ ἀλλὰ δι᾽ αἰσθήσεως παρῇ τινι τὸ τοιοῦτον αὖ πάθος (i.e. δόξα)}} ἆρ᾽ οἷόν τε ὀρθῶς εἰπεῖν ἕτερόν τι πλὴν φ.;.. φαίνεται δὲ ὃ λέγομεν (i.e. φαντασία) σύμμειξις αἰσθήσεως καὶ δόξης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 264a, 264b; οὐδὲ δόξα μετ᾽ αἰσθήσεως οὐδὲ δι᾽ αἰσθήσεως οὐδὲ συμπλοκὴ δόξης καὶ αἰσθήσεως φ. ἂν εἴη 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 428a26}}; ἡ φ. καθ᾽ ἣν λέγομεν φάντασμά τι ἡμῖν γίγνεσθαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 428a1; ἔστι δὲ φ. ἡ ὑπὸ τῆς κατ᾽ ἐνέργειαν αἰσθήσεως γινομένη κίνησις [prev. author] “Insomn.” 459a17, compare “de An.” 429a1; εἰ ἔστι καὶ τοῦτο [τὸ νοεῖν] φ. τις ἢ μὴ ἄνευ φ. [prev. work] 403a8}}; with _genitive_, μέλλοντος κακοῦ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1382a21, compare 1370a30, b33, +others}}; αἰσχροῦ φ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 9.6.5; also περὶ ἀδοξίας φ. ἐστὶν ἡ αἰσχύνη 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1384a23}}; γίγνεται ἑκάστῳ φ. ὅτι τοιοῦτός [ἐστι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|] [prev. work] 1371a9; ἡ κατὰ τὴν σύλληψιν φ. τῆς γυναικός “Placita Philosophorum” 5.12.2, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.39 (_plural_)}}; τὰ πρὸς τὴν φ. χρώματα colours as judged by the φ., **apparent** colours, {{Refs|“Placita Philosophorum” 1.15.8; φωτίζεσθαι πρὸς τὴν φ. [prev. work]2.28.6.}}
**2.b** in Aristotle, **faculty of imagination,** both presentative and representative, opposed to αἴσθησις, [φ.] οὐκ ἔστιν αἴσθησις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 428a5; opposed to δόξα, because πίστις is absent, [prev. work] 22, 24; opposed to ἐπιστήμη, νοῦς, διάνοια, οὐδὲ [φ.] τῶν ἀεὶ ἀληθευόντων οὐδεμία ἔσται, οἷον ἐπιστήμη ἢ νοῦς [prev. work] 428a17}}; φ. ἕτερον καὶ αἰσθήσεως καὶ διανοίας· αὐτή τε οὐ γίγνεται ἄνευ αἰσθήσεως καὶ ἄνευ ταύτης οὐκ ἔστιν ὑπόληψις{{Refs|[prev. work] 427b14; φ. γίνεται ἢ διὰ νοήσεως ἢ δι᾽ αἰσθήσεως [prev. author] “MA” 702a19; ὀρεκτικὸν [τὸ ζῷον] οὐκ ἄνευ φ., φ. δὲ πᾶσα ἢ λογιστικὴ ἢ αἰσθητική [prev. author] “de An.” 433b28.}}
**2.c** **creative imagination,** φ. σοφωτέρα μιμήσεως δημιουργός {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.19.}}
**3** the **use of imagery** in literature, τεθορύβηται ταῖς φ. μᾶλλον ἢ δεδείνωται {{Refs 1st c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 3.1; ἡ ῥητορικὴ φ. [prev. author] 15.2; ἀπὸ τοῦ ἀποδεικτικοῦ περιελκόμεθα εἰς τὸ κατὰ φαντασίαν ἐκπληκτικόν [prev. author] 15.11; αἱ ποιητικαὶ φ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.759c; ἐς τὰς φ. τῶν λεγομένων τῷ σχήματι τοῦ σώματος συνεφέροντο 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Αιβυκή” 134, compare “Hisp.” 26, “Syr.” 40.}}
**4** **prestige, reputation,** μεγάλην ἐφείλκετο φ. ὡς μόνος εἰδὼς τί λέγει {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 22.9.12, compare 24.7.2, 24.11.5, “fragment” 233; ἐκ τοῦ τοὺς ἄλλους ἐλέγχειν φ. ἀπενέγκασθαι προαιρούμενος 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.1.6}}; **parade, ostentation,** ποιέειν μηδὲν περιέργως μηδὲ μετὰ φαντασίης {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 7, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.25.22, 16.21.1, 31.26.6, 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 36 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.83, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 38.26, +others; ἡ ἐφήμερος τῆς ἀρχῆς φ. 4th c.BC: Sopater Comicus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.5.55; μετὰ πολλῆς φ. NT.Act.25.23, compare 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 4.53.}}

5326:
** φάντᾰσ-μα**, ατος, τό,
={φάσμα}, **apparition, phantom,** ἐνύπνια φαντάσματα {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 710; νυκτέρων φ. ἔχουσι μορφάς [prev. author] “fragment” 312; φ. δαίμονος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Dio” 2, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 54, 94 (_anapaest meter_), 390, 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.22, NT.Matt.14.26; περὶ τὰ μνήματα.. ὤφθη ἄττα ψυχῶν σκιοειδῆ φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 81d}}; **vision, dream,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1102b10 (_plural_), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.30.}}
**b** _plural_, **phenomena,** τὰ ἐν ἀέρι φ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 395a29}}: _plural_, **portents,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.62.}}
**II** ={φαντασία}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e, “Tht.” 167b, “Prm.” 166a, “R.” 598b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 428a1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2pp.37,51}} U.; distinguished from εἰκών, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 236c.}}

5327:
** φάραγξ** [ᾰ], αγγος, ἡ,
**cleft, chasm,** especially in a mountain side, **ravine, gully,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 60.1 (_plural_); φ. πρὸς δυσχειμέρῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 15, compare 142 (_anapaest meter_), +others, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 277, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.13, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.5.2 (_plural_), “Schwyzer” 289.161 (Priene, 2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; πᾶσα φ. πληρωθήσεται {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Isa.40.4; πάντας.. ἐς φάραγγα ἐσέβαλον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.67; ἐν πύλαισι γὰρ σταθεὶς φάραγγος}}**of the cave,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 668}}; φάραγγα δακτύλου πιάσματι σύρει, of shaping a roll before it is baked, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 75.11:— _metaphorically_, of Cleon, φάραγγα καὶ Χάρυβδιν ἁρπαγῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 248 (troch.).}}
**II** _metaphorically_, of the anus, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Sotades Lyricus 2.2.}}

5331:
** φαρμᾰκ-είᾱ**, ἡ,
**use of drugs,** especially of **purgatives,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 1.24, 2.36 (both _plural_), “PCair.Zen.” 18.5 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.447, etc.}}; αἱ ἄνω φ., i. e. emetics, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 962a3}}; of abortifacients, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.59}}: generally, **the use of any kind of drugs, potions,** or **spells,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 933b: _plural_, [prev. author] “Prt.” 354a, “Ti.” 89b, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 535.9.}}
**2** **poisoning** or **witchcraft,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40 57, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.13.4, “POxy.” 486.21 (2nd c.AD)}}; αἱ περὶ τὰς φαρμακείας, ={αἱ φαρμακίδες}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 572a22.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **remedy,** παιδιὰς προσάγειν φαρμακείας χάριν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1337b41.}}

5332:
** φαρμᾰκ-εύς**, έως, ὁ,
**poisoner, sorcerer,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1140, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203d, etc.; γνήσιοι σοφισταὶ καὶ φ. 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.197d.}}
**II** **druggist, apothecary,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 2.12.}}

5334:
** φάσις** [ᾰ], εως,
h(: (φαίνω):—
**A** **denunciation, information laid,** γραφαὶ ἢ φάσεις ἢ ἐνδείξεις ἢ ἀπαγωγαί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.88, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 209 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator _Attic dialect_ 89.36, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.78, Legal cited in [same work] 35.51; ἡ περὶ τὸ πλοῖον φ. [prev. author] 58.5, compare “SIG” 695.83 (Magn.Mae., 2nd c.BC).}}
**A.II** (φαίνομαι) **appearance,** of stars, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 97 b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 342b34, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 122, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.10, etc.: special uses}}
**A.II.a** πρὸς τὸν ὁρίζοντα φ. **appearance** above the horizon, opposed to ὑπὸ τὸν ὁρίζοντα κρύψις, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 13.2.}}
**A.II.b** **heliacal rising,** opposed to κρύψις, φ. ἑῷαι, ἑσπέριαι, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 99, compare “Alm.” 8.6, +others}}
**A.II.c** including the previous _signification_ and κρύψεις, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Phas.” p.3 H., +others (_plural_), 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 241.30 (_plural_).}}
**A.II.d** _plural_, **phases** of the moon, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 8.11, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.904, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.491, 497.}}
**A.II.2** **an appearance, trace,** τινος [{{Refs|Anonymus cited in 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {φασί}.}}]

5335:
** φάσκω**,
used by _Epic dialect_ only in _imperfect_ ἔφασκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.100}}; _Epic dialect_ φάσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.75, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 209: frequently in Trag. and Comedy texts (but perhaps never in _present_ _indicative_, see below)}}: as _imperfect_ of φημί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 110, etc., also 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 742; also _imperative_ φάσκε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1077, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1429a6}}; _subjunctive_ φάσκω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 93, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 561 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.3, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.11, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 10.11, “PHal.” 1.134 (3rd c.BC)}}; _optative_ φάσκοιμι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1037, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.27}}; _infinitive_ φάσκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 9, “OT” 462, “Ph.” 1411 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 695 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.52, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.1}}; _participle_ φάσκων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.66.6, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1382}} (the only part of the Verb used in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus (3.70) and 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus (“R.” 337e, +others), except ἔφασκεν [prev. author] “Lg.” 901a)}}:—_passive_ is not found, ἐφάσκετ᾽ is for ἐφάσκετε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 114}}. Rare in _present_ _indicative_ [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|before 3rd c.BC: φάσκει 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.16 (φάσκοι Reiske, Wyse), “PMich.Zen.” 82.6 (3rd c.BC), “PSI” 8.921.7 (2nd c.AD), uncertain in 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.17 (variant{φάσκοι}), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.35, “Geoponica” 9.14.2, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; φάσκεις “PSI” 9.1011.10 (3rd c.BC)}}]; φάσκουσι (ν) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator “Epistulae” 11.11, “PCair.Zen.” 21.25, 244.5, +others (3rd c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 86, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.429b, “Geoponica” 5.2.9, etc.}}; φάσκομεν is uncertain conjecture (for πάσχομεν) in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 146.4}}:—**say, affirm, assert,** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.191, 8.565, +others}}; φάσκειν as _imperative_, in this _construction_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 462, “Ph.” 1411 (_anapaest meter_)}}; οὐ φασκόντων χρήσειν **saying** they **would** not.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.58; οὐ φάσκων ἀνεκτὸν εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.52}}; the _infinitive_ is frequently to be supplied, ἐν τῇδ᾽ ἔφασκε γῇ (i.e. εὑρεθήσεσθαι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 110}}; φησίν γε· φάσκων δ᾽ (i.e. ἥξειν) {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 319}}; τῶν φασκόντων γονέων (i.e. εἶναι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 538a, etc.}}; rarely φ. ὡς.., ὅτι.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.12, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.215f: with _accusative_, τοῦτο φ. τοὔπος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 93, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1382, etc.: _absolutely_, ὡς ἔφασκεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 114; φάσκουσα καὶ οὐ φάσκουσα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 190a.}}
**2** **think, deem, expect,** ὃ οὔ ποτ᾽ ἔγωγε τελευτήσεσθαι ἔφασκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.100; οὔ μ᾽ ἐφάσκεθ᾽.. οἴκαδ᾽ ἱκέσθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.35}}; φάσκειν.. ὁρᾶν **believe that** you see, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 9.}}
**3** **say, promise,** with _infinitive_ _future_, με.. ἔφασκε θήσειν ἀθάνατον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.256; φάσκων προσποιήσειν αὐτήν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.85, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 541e.}}

5336:
** φάτν-η**, ἡ,
**manger, crib,** [ἵππους] ἀτίταλλ᾽ ἐπὶ φάτνῃ {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.271; ἵππος ἀκοστήσας ἐπὶ φ. 6.506; ἵππους μὲν κατέδησαν.. φάτνῃ ἐφ᾽ ἱππείῃ 10.568; ἐϋξέστῃ ἐπὶ φ. 24.280; ἡ φ. τῶν ἵππων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.70, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 510 (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.27 (_plural_), NT.Luke.2.7, +others}}; φάτναι Ζηνὸς, of the **manger** of Pegasus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.92}}: also of oxen, ὥς τίς τε κατέκτανε βοῦν ἐπὶ φ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.535, 11.411.}}
**2** βοῦς ἐπὶ φ., _proverbial_ of ease and comfort, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.10; also πλουσίαν ἔχειν φ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 378; φάτναις ἀργυραῖς χρῆσθαι 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.2.14}}; ἡ ἐν τῇ φ. κύων 'the dog in the manger', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 14, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 12.236 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus)}}; θεραπεύειν τὴν φ. τινός to court one who feeds you, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 107; τοὺς ἐκ τῆς αὐτῆς οἱονεὶ φ. ἐδηδοκότας [prev. work] 39: τὴν αὐτὴν φ. ζητεῖν}} to return to their old **haunts,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 129.}}
**II** ={φάτνωμα}{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1, “IG” 11(2).161 A 46 (Delos, 3rd c.BC): _plural_, [prev. work]42(1).109 iii 85, +others (Epid., 3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.66.}}
**III** **socket of tooth,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.93.}}
**IV** the **Manger,** name of the nebula (star-cluster) between the ὄνοι in Cancer, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 23, +others, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 892, 898, 3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus “Καταστερισμοί” 11, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 23. (Later Gr. πάθνη _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus, but φάτνη}} in Attic and Delian Inscrr., {{Refs|“IG” 22.1487.37, 11(2).[same place], “Inscription Déios” 504 A 6, B 9}}: **bhndh,** cf. Sanskrit _badhnāti_ 'tie', Celt. **benn** 'wicker chariot'.)

5337:
** φαῦλος**, η, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 435, “fragment” 1083.9, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.21: (compare φλαῦρος)}}:— **cheap, easy, slight, paltry,** first found commonly in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, twicein 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, 126 (_comparative_, elsewhereφλαῦρος), six times in 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus, “fragment” 87, +others, twice in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, “Frr.” 41,771:}} _adverb_ φαύλως{{Refs 4th c.BC+|once in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus: 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 520.}}
**I** of things, **easy, slight,** φ. ἀθλήσας πόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 317}}; φαυλότατον ἔργον ''tis as easy as lying', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 213; φ. πρᾶγμα [prev. author] “Lys.” 14; τὸ ζήτημα οὐ φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 368c; φ. ἐρώτημα [prev. author] “Phlb.” 19a; φαῦλον αὐτοῖς προστάξομεν [prev. author] “R.” 423c: frequently with negative, οὐ φ., ἀλλὰ χαλεπὸν πιστεῦσαι [prev. work] 527d; μάχη οὐ φ. [prev. author] “Tht.” 179d; οὐ φ. τέχνη [prev. author] “Sph.” 223c}}; οὔτοι βασιλέα φαῦλόν [ἐστι] κτανεῖν 'tis no **slight matter** to kill a king, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 760}}; νυκτὸς γὰρ οὔτι φ. ἐμβαλεῖν στρατόν no **easy** matter, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Rh.” 285; οὐ φ. πληγαί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.13; φιλοῦσιν ἰατροὶ λέγειν τὰ φαῦλα μείζω 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 497}}; φαῦλα ἐπιφέρειν bring **paltry** charges, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26}}; τὰ φ. νικήσας ἔχω have gained **petty** victories, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 41 (wrongly glossed by μέγα in 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus, and “Etymologicum Magnum” 789.43, cf. Hsch)}}; σύμμαχον Τροίᾳ μολόντα Ῥῆσον οὐ φαύλῳ τρόπῳ, i. e. with no **trivial** force, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 599; παρὰ φαῦλον ποιεῖσθαί τι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 4.2, compare 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 14.26.}} _adverb_ -λως εὑρεῖν, τυχεῖν,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 404 (troch.), 509 (_anapaest meter_); φ. πάνυ [prev. author] “Lys.” 566 (_anapaest meter_)}}; φ. ἐκφυγεῖν to get off **easily,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ach.” 215 (Lyric poetry); φ. ἀποδράς [prev. author] “Th.” 711 (Lyric poetry); φαυλότατα καὶ ῥᾷστα [prev. author] “Nu.” 778}}; οὔτι φαύλως ἦλθε **with** no **trivial force,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 112; φ. βοηθήσειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.13; φαύλως καὶ γλίσχρως παρείχοντο χρήματα “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 14.2}}; τὰς ἐλπίδας φ. ἔχειν to be **slight,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.3.1.}}
**I.2** **simple, ordinary,** δίαιτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 36, “Art.” 49, Eur. “fragment” 213.4; σῖτα καὶ ποτὰ φαυλότατα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.2, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 3.68 (_comparative_)}}; but frequently with sense **poor, indifferent,** στρατιά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.21}}; ἀσπίδες, τείχισμα, παρασκευή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.9.115, 6.31; ἱμάτιον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus [prev. cited]}} _adverb_ -λως, διατρίβειν ἐν φιλοσοφίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173c; μὴ φ. μηδὲ ἰδιωτικῶς [prev. author] “Lg.” 966e.}}
**I.3** **mean, bad,** πρῆξις {{NT+5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 177; λόγοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 870, ψόγος [prev. author] “Ph.” 94 (perhaps both in signification 1.1 and in 1.3); οὐ φ. ὄψις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 519a; φ. δόξα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.205; τὰ πράγματ᾽ ἐστὶ φ. [prev. author] 19.30; φαῦλα διαπεπραγμένος 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 229; ὁ φαῦλα πράττων NT.John.3.20; μηδὲ πραξάντων τι ἀγαθὸν ἢ φ. NT.Rom.9.11; τὸ φ.}}**evil,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 390}}; τὰ φ., opposed to τὰ ἀγαθά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.47}}; τύχη φ., opposed to ἀγαθή, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 197a26, compare “Metaphorical” 1065a35; τὴν πόλιν μηθὲμ φ. παθεῖν “OGI” 765.35 (from Priene)}}; κομίσασθαι.. εἴτε ἀγαθὸν εἴτε φ., of rewards and punishments, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.2Cor.5.10; φ. μαίωσις 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.17, compare 1.91, +others}}
**II** of persons, **low** in rank, **mean, common,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 688}}; οἱ φαυλότατοι **the commonest sort** (of soldiers), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.77}}; [γάμος] ὁ ἐκ τῶν φαυλοτέρων, opposed to ἐκ μειζόνων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.27, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 475b}}; of outward looks, αἱ φαυλότεραι **the plainer ones,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 617, compare 626 (_comparative_, both _anapaest meter_).}}
**II.2** **inefficient, bad,** διδάσκαλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 771.3}}; τὸ φ. καὶ τὸ μέσον καὶ τὸ πάνυ ἀκριβές **the inefficient,** the middling, and the perfect, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18}}; φ. αὐλητής, opposed to ἀγαθός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327c; τοξότης [prev. author] “Tht.” 194a; οὐ δὲ φαύλων ἀνδρῶν οὐδὲ τῶν ἐπιτυχόντων [prev. author] “Cra.” 390d}}; opposed to σπουδαῖος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 757a, etc.}}; especially in point of education and accomplishments, opposed to σοφός, οἱ γὰρ ἐν σοφοῖς φαῦλοι παρ᾽ ὄχλῳ μουσικώτεροι λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 989, compare “Ph.” 496, “Ion” 834, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174c, Alcaeus1.129a; τὸ πλῆθος τὸ -ότερον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 431 (Lyric poetry)}}; οἱ -ότεροι, opposed to to οἱ ξυνετώτεροι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.37}}; οἱ φαυλότεροι γνώμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 83; τὰ γράμματα φαῦλοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 242c}} (so in _adverb_, φαυλοτέρως πεπαιδευμένοι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 876d)}}; generally, **inferior,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 483c}}: with _infinitive_, φαῦλοι μάχεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 305}}; φ. λέγειν, φ. διαλεχθῆναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 181b, “Prt.” 336c: of animals, φ. κύων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.22; φαυλότατοι ἵπποι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.1.3.}}
**II.3** **careless, thoughtless, indifferent,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 807:}} —especially in _adverb_, φαύλως ἐκρίνατε judged **lightly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 520; φ. εὕδειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 769; οὐχ ὧδε φ. [prev. author] “Ion” 1546}}; φ. παραινεῖν **off-hand,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “HF” 89}}; λόγισαι φαύλως μὴ ψήφοις ἀλλ᾽ ἀπὸ χειρός **off-hand, roughly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 656}} (_anapaest meter_); φ. εἰπεῖν **casually,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449c}}; φ. φέρειν to bear **lightly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 850, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 961.}}
**II.4** in good sense, **simple, unaffected,** φαῦλον, ἄκομψον, τὰ μέγιστ᾽ ἀγαθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 473 (_anapaest meter_), compare 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 3.63}}. _adverb_ -λως, παιδεύειν τινά **by a very simple method,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 13.4; φ. καὶ βραχέως ἀποκρίνασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 147c.}}
**II.5** of health, etc., φαύλως ἔχειν to be **ill,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.32}}; φ. πράττειν to be **in sorry plight,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 165; φ. ἔχει τὰ πράγματα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.3, +others}}

5338:
** φέγγος**, εος, τό,
**light, splendour, lustre,** τῆλε δὲ φ. ἀπὸ χροὸς ἀθανάτοιο λάμπε θεᾶς {{LXX+4th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 278; τὸ φ. τῆς χρόας 4th-3rd c.BC: Duris Epigrammaticus 14 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; τὸ φ. τῆς δόξης Κυρίου LXX.Eze.10.4}}; φ. ἀστραπῆς ὅπλων{{LXX|LXX.Hab.3.11}}: frequently like{φάος}, of **daylight,** either _absolutely_ or with some word added, φ. ἡλίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 377, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 606, etc.; τὸ φ. τοῦ θεοῦ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 722}}; without the _Article_, φ. εἰσορᾶν θεοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Or.” 1025, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 673}}; ὦ φέγγος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 859, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 866; ὦ φ. ἡμέρας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1577; δεκάτῳ φέγγει ἔτους in the tenth year, [prev. work] 504.}}
**b** **moonlight,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.4}}; νυκτερινὰ φέγγη, opposed to ἡμερινὸν φῶς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 508c; ἐὰν τὸ φ. ἐκλείψῃ “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 4.172, compare 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 38.255; φῶς ἡμέρας, φέγγος σελήνης 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}} see at {φέγγο; τὸ φ. [τοῦ γάλακτος}], of the milky way, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 346a26.}}
**c** of men, φ. ἰδεῖν, προσιδεῖν, to see **the light,** come into the world, ἐπεὶ πάμπρωτον εἶδον φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.111; ἀελίου προσιδεῖν φ. 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.162; λιπεῖν φ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 954; ὄλωλα, φ. οὐκέτ᾽ ἔστι μοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1144.}}
**d** **day,** τριταῖον ἤδη φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 32, compare 2nd c.BC: Sosiphanes Tragicus 3.1}}; μοιρίδιον φ. ={μ. ἦμαρ}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Eleg.” 2.}}
**2** **the light** of torches or fire, φ. λαμπάδων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 1022}}; πυρός{{Refs|[prev. work] 1029, “Ch.” 1037}}: **a light, torch,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 448, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.9}}; _plural_ φέγγη **torches** or **watch-fires,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 10.26 (conjecture), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 25, etc.}}
**3** **the light** of the eyes, φ. ὀμμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 368, 1035; ὄσσων 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.75}}; τυφλὸν φ, i.e. blindness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1068 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **light,** as a _metaphorically_ for delight, glory, pride, joy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.97, “N.” 3.64, 4.13; τί γὰρ γυναικὶ τούτου φ. ἥδιον δρακεῖν, ἀπὸ στρατείας ἀνδρὶ σώσαντος θεοῦ πύλας ἀνοῖξα; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 602}}; of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.42; μέγα βροτοῖσι φ. Ἀσκληπιόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 640 (Lyric poetry); Μουσέων φ. Ὅμηρον “Anthologia Graeca” 7.6 (Antip. Sid.); ὦ ταῖς ἱεραῖς φ. Ἀθήναις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1319 (_anapaest meter_); πλοῦτος.. ἀνδρὶ φ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.56}}; φ. ὀπώρας, of wine, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 153.}}
**II.2** **lustre,** δικαιοσύνης καὶ σωφροσύνης {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 250b; φ. ἐλέους LXX.3Mac.6.4; τῆς ψυχῆς τὸ γάνωμα καὶ τὸ φ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.792a, etc.}}

5339:
** φείδομαι**,
{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 101, etc.:}}_imperfect_ φείδοντο (without augment) even in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 716}} after a diphthong at the end of the preceding line: _future_ φείσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 312 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 31a, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ πεφῐδήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.215}}, later _future_ _passive_ in _middle_ sense φ[ε]ισθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PUniv.Giss.” 21.6 (2nd c.AD)}}: _aorist 1_ ἐφεισάμην {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 32.1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 412, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.11, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ φείσατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.236: _Epic dialect_ reduplicate}}_aorist 2_ πεφῐδόμην, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in _optative_ πεφῐδοίμην, πεφίδοιτο, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.277, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.464}}, _infinitive_ πεφιδέσθαι {{Refs|21.101}}: _perfect_ _participle_ πεφεισμένος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 59 (in middle sense, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 50.20)}}; _Epic dialect_ _imperative_ πεφίδησο {{Refs|“IG” 14.1363.16}}; _participle_ πεφιδημένος {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 12.392}}:—**spare**:
**I** **spare** persons and things, e.g. in war, i.e. **not destroy** them, with _genitive_, Τρώων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.101; ἀνδρός 24.158, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.277, 22.54, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 31a; Ἰλίου 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.215; Ἄρης οὐκ ἀγαθῶν φ. 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus [same place]; ἀπ᾽ ἀνδρῶν ὧν Ἄρης ἐφείσατο 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 412; γῆς πατρίδος 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus}} [same place]; μὴ φείσῃ βίου **spare** not my life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 749; μὴ φείδεσθε.. στρατοῦ [prev. author] “Aj.” 844; φ. μήτε ἰδίου μήτε δημοσίου οἰκοδομήματος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90, compare 3.74}}: _absolutely_, **spare, be merciful,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 59.}}
**II** **spare** persons and things in using them, **use sparingly,** ἵππων φειδόμενος, i. e. **taking care** of them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.202; πίθου μεσσόθι φ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 369}}; φ. ὃν εἶχε βίον (βίον by attraction to the _relative_) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 908; ἰδίᾳ μὲν τῶν [ὄντων] φείδομαι δημοσίᾳ δὲ λῃτουργῶν ἥδομαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.16; φείδεσθε τοὐλαίου σφόδρα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Comedy texts 190: in this sense, most frequently with a negative, οὐ φ.}} not **to spare,** i. e. **to use** or **give freely,** οὐδέ νυ τοῦ περ [δέπαος] φείσατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.236; μὴ φείδεο σίτου 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 604; θνῄσκωμεν ψυχέων μηκέτι φειδόμενοι 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 10.14; τᾶς ζωᾶς [prev. author] 15.5; σφετέρας οὐ φείσατο νευρᾶς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).33; φείδεο τῶν νεῶν, μηδὲ ναυμαχίην ποιέο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.68. ; τούτων φ. μηδενός [prev. author] 9.41, compare 39; φείδοντο κέντρων οὐδέν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 716; οὐδὲν φ. αὐτῶν οὔτ᾽ ἐν πόνοις κτλ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.1, compare 7.1.29; οὔτε τοῦ σώματος οὔτε τῶν ὄντων 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.11; οὐδενὸς ἂν ἐφείσατο τῶν ἑαυτοῦ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.24; οὔθ᾽ ἱερῶν κτεάνων οὔτε τι δημοσίων φ. 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.13; μήτε χρημάτων μήτε πόνων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a: later also with _accusative_, τῶν συμμάχων and τὰ τῶν συμμάχων both in 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 50.20.}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **to be sparing, live thriftily,** φείδεσθαι μὲν ἄμεινον {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 931; τοὺς φειδομένους καὶ τοὺς ἀκριβῶς διαιτῶντας 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.32; οἱ γεωργοῦντες καὶ φ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.172, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 53}}; frequently in _participle_ φειδόμενος, η, ον, **thrifty,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 247, 553 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.; ὄμμασι φειδομένοις with **shrinking, shy** eyes, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.21 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus), compare 5.215 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus), 268 ([prev. author])}}; αἱ μὴ φ. (i.e. μέλισσαι) the un**thrifty** ones, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 627a20: also ἔπαινοι πάνυ πεφεισμένοι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 59; πεφιδημένα δάκτυλα 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 12.392}}; compare πεφεισμένως, φειδομένως.
**III** **have consideration for,** τῆς τοῦ λόγου συμμετρίας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.114b}}: with _negative_, **pay** no **heed to,** οὔτ᾽ ἀνθρώπων φείδεται οὔτε θεῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.278 (6th c.AD: Paulus Silentiarius Epigrammaticus), compare 7.706 (4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus).}}
**IV** **draw back from, refrain from,** θαλάσσας {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 4.13 (probably); κελεύθου 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.20; κινδύνου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.18; τᾶς θήρας 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus “fragment” 10.12}}; τοῦ λέγειν, τοῦ ἀκολουθεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.19 (variant), “HG” 7.1.24; φείδου μηδὲν ὧνπερ ἐννοεῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 115, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 401, etc.; οὐδενὸς φεισάμενος οὔτε τῶν πρὸς τοὺς θεοὺς οὔτε τῶν πρὸς τοὺς πολίτας δικαίων “SIG” 708.36 (Istropolis, 2nd c.BC): (_absolutely_, μὴ φείδεσθε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1285; φείδου μηδέν [prev. author] “Hec.” 1044; μὴ φείδου, εἴ τι ἔχεις διδάσκειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.35)}}: with _infinitive_, **spare** to do, **forbear** from doing, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 393 (perhaps _absolutely_, post φείδου δ᾽ distinguendum)}}; also φ. μή τι δρᾶσαι τῶν τυραννικῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 574b; τί φειδόμεσθα τῶν λίθων.. μὴ οὐ καταξαίνειν τὸν ἄνδρ; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 319 (troch.).}}
**V** in {{Refs|LXX, with Preps., φ. ἐπί τινι}}**have mercy upon.., **{{LXX|LXX.Jer.15.5, 21.7; ἐπί τινα [prev. author] 28(51).3}}; φ. περί τινος **to keep one's hands off**.., {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.12.6 (but φ. περὶ κακώσεως}} **spare** to hurt, {{LXX|LXX.Sir.13.12); φ. ὑπὲρ τῆς κολοκύνθης LXX.Jonah.4.10; ἀπό τινος LXX.1Ki.15.3, LXX.Eze.24.21}}; φ. τι ἀπό τινος **keep** it **off, Jb.**{{Refs|30.10}}; φ. τῆς ψυχῆς ἀπὸ θανάτου{{LXX|[prev. work] 33.18, cf. LXX.Psa.18(19).14}}; φειδεύμενοι (from _contraction_ φειδέομαι) is conjecture for φιλεύμεναι in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Eusebius Myndius Philosophus 17.}}

5341:
** φαιλόνης**,
={φαινόλης}, {{NT+5th c.AD+|NT.2Tim.4.13 (wrongly explained by γλωσσόκομον, εἰλητάριον μεμβράϊνον, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}; written **φελόνης** in {{NT+2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 347 (2nd c.AD), variant in NT.2Tim.}} [same place]:—_diminutive_ **φαιλόνιον** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 933.30 (2nd c.AD)}}; written **φ[ε]λόνιον** in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PGen.” 80.14 (4th c.AD).}}
** φαινόλη**,
_Doric dialect_ **-όλα**, ἡ, = Latin _paenula_, thick upper garment, **cloak**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 7}}, compare **φαινόλα·** τὸ ὕφασμα, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; **φαίνουλα** {{Refs|“Edict.Diocl.” 19.51 (Megalopolis)}}, **παίνουλα** {{Refs|[prev. work]52}}**πένουλα** {{Refs|[prev. work]51 (Megarian dialect)}}:—but usually **φαινόλης**, ου, ὁ, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.8.34, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.97e, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 2.3, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.61, “POxy.” 736.4 (1st c.AD).}}

5342:
** φέρω**
(_Locrian dialect_ **φάρω** [ᾰ], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 9(1).334.5 (Oeanthea, 5th c.BC))}}, only _present_ and _imperfect_ (late 1 _aorist_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἤφεραν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 3.1379), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.458, etc.}}: _Epic dialect_ forms, _2nd pers._ _plural_ _imperative_ φέρτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.171}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ φέρῃσθα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 144}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ φέρῃσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.308, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.164, +others}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ φερέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.411, +others:}}_imperfect_ ἔφερον, _Epic dialect_ φέρον {{Refs|3.245}}; also φέρεσκε, φέρεσκον (_3rd.pers._ _plural_), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.429, 10.108.}}
**II** _future_ οἴσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.82, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ οἰσῶ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.11}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ οἰσεῦμες {{Refs|[prev. author] 15.133}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ηοίσοντι {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.150}}: the following _active_ forms are not _future_ in sense, _imperative_ οἶσε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.106, 481, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1099, 1101, 1122, “Ra.” 482; οἰσέτω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.173, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.255}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ οἰσόντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 15}}; _infinitive_ οἴσειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.102}}, _Epic dialect_ οἰσέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.429, οἰσέμεναι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.120, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.399, etc.}}: _aorist 1_ _infinitive_ οἶσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.611 codices (ἀν-οῖσαι is probably in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.157)}}:—_middle_, _future_ οἴσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.217, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 969, etc. (in _passive_ sense, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 440, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 18.6}}; so _Doric dialect_ οἰσεῖται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ τῶν ὀχουμένων” 1.7, +others)}}: _future_ _passive_ οἰσθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.45, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 205a13, 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ τῶν ὀχουμένων” 1.3, +others, (ἐξ-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 561}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ προοῖσται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 2}}; compare οἰστέον, οἰστός (ἀν-οιστός).
**III** from ἐνεγκ- (not found in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus or 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, except as variant in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.194, but in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.66, “I.” 8(7).21, (προσ-) [prev. author] “P.” 9.36, also 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 16.62}}, and normal in _Attic dialect_ and Trag., also in codices {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus, “Epid.” 1.1.2, +others) come}}_aorist 1_ ἤνεγκα, and _aorist 2_ ἤνεγκον:—Indic., _1st pers._ _singular_ ἤνεγκον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 521 (Lyric poetry), 964, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1299, “Th.” 742, “Lys.” 944, (δι-) 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.59, but ἤνεγκα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 13, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 38, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.4}}, and in _composition_ with Preps.; _2nd pers._ _singular_ always ἤνεγκας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 540 (Lyric poetry), (ἐξ-) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 741 (in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 742, δέκα μῆνας αὔτ᾽ ἐγὼ ἤνεγκον}} is answd. by ἤνεγκας σ;); _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἤνεγκε, common to both forms; dual δι-ηνεγκάτην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 723b}}; _plural_ always ἠνέγκαμεν, -ατε, -αν (_3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἀπ-ήνενκαν {{Refs|“IG” 22.1620.37, +others, once ἀπ-ήνεγκον [prev. work] 1414.2}}; δι-ηνέγκομεν is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.8)}}: _imperative_, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἔνεγκε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 699, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 110, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.9 (ἔνεγκον}} conjecture Pors. in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 8)}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐνεγκάτω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1149 (troch.), “Th.” 238, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116d, (προσ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 5.2; but ἐξ-ενεγκέτω “IG” 12.63.33, 76.61; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐνεγκόντω [prev. work]5 (1).26.16 (Amyclae, 2nd-1st c.BC)}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ ἐξ-ενέγκατε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 847:}}_subjunctive_ ἐνέγκω common to both forms: _optative_, _1st pers._ _singular_ ἐνέγκαιμι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 393, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43c: _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἐνέγκαι (codex A, but -κοι codex Laur.) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 774, but ἐνέγκοι [prev. author] “fragment” 84 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330a, (ξυν-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.20, etc.}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ ἐνέγκαιτε (ἐνέγκατε codices) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 751 (Lyric poetry)}}: _infinitive_ ἐνεγκεῖν {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 766, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1599, “IG” 22.40.18, etc., (προσ-) 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.36, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 15; Hellenistic ἐνέγκαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1349a27 (εἰσ-), “PAmh.” 2.30.35 (2nd c.BC), NT.Mark.2.4}} (προσ-), etc., found also in codices {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus, “Aff.” 3 (προσ-), “Nat.Mul.” 19}} (δι-): _participle_ ἐνεγκών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).21, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 692, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.56, etc., ἐνέγκας “IG” 22.1361.21 (εἰσ-), 333.4, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.51 (and later, 4th-3rd c.BC: Demetrius Comicus Novae Comoediae 1.10 (εἰσ-), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1351a14, etc.; in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus we find ἐξ-ενεγκόντες “Mem.” 1.2.53, and δι-ενεγκοῦσα, συν-ενεγκόντες, variants in [prev. work] 2.2.5, “An.” 6.5.6):}} — _middle_, only ἠνεγκάμην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 76}} (ἐξ-), etc. (except _imperative_ ἐνεγκοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 470)}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἠνέγκω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 583, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.13}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἠνέγκατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 462, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 406b, etc.}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ἠνεγκάμεθα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ion” 530b, (προ-) “Phlb.” 57a}}; _infinitive_ εἰσ-ενέγκασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.188}}: _participle_ ἐνεγκάμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.131, (ἀπ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 6.2.}}
**IV** from ἐνεικ- comes _aorist 1_ ἤνεικα, found mostly in _Ionic dialect_ (but not in codices {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus), _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry, also at Cos (see. below)}} and implied elsewhere in _passive_ forms (see. below see):—the endings are those of _aorist_ 1, except in _imperative_ ἔνεικε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.178}}, _infinitive_ ἐνεικέμεν (variant{ἐνεγκέμεν}) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.194}}, ἐνείκην (see. below), and _participle_ μετ-ενεικών, ἐξενικοῦσι (see. below), compare συνενείκομαι:—_1st pers._ _singular_ ἀν-ένεικα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.625}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἀπ-ένεικας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.255}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἤνεικε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.300, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.146}}, _Epic dialect_ ἔνεικε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.705, +others}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ἐνείκαμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.43}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἤνεικαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.30}}, _Epic dialect_ ἔνεικαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.306}}; _imperative_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἔνεικον {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 62.3}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_, ἐνείκατε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.393}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐνεικάντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 688 B 3 (Chios, 5th c.BC)}}; _infinitive_ ἐνεῖκαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.334, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.53, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32}}; ἐνεικέμεν (see. above); _Aeolic dialect_ ἐνείκην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Oxy.” 1788 “fragment” 15i i 20}}; _participle_ ἐνείκας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.39, (ἀν-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.23; μετ-ενεικών “Abh.Berl.Akad.” 1928(6).22 (Cos, 3rd c.BC)}}:—_middle_, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἀν-ενείκατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.314}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἠνείκαντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|9.127, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.57, (ἐσ-) 7.152}}; _participle_ ἐνεικάμενος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 35.4.}}
**IV.2** _aorist 1_ ἤνῐκα is found in the following dialect forms: _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἤνικε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.110 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; ἤνικεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 239 B i11 (Delph., 4th c.BC)}}; ἀν-ήνικε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 4.757 A 12, +others (Troezen, 2nd c.BC); ἀπ-ήνικε [prev. work]42(1).103.16, +others (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; but ἤνῑκε is probably written for ἤνεικε in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 4.801.3 (Troezen, 6th c.BC)}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ἀν-ηνίκαμες [ῐ] {{Refs|“GDI” 3591b21 (from Calymna)}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἤνικαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 239 B i 17 (Delph., 4th c.BC), “IG” 12(2).15.15 (Mytil., 3rd c.BC)}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ ἐνίκει {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.161 (from Cyrene)}}; ἐσ-ενίκη, and _infinitive_ ἐσ-ένικαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12(2).645b43,39 (Nesus, 4th c.BC)}}; _participle_ (_dative_ _plural_) ἐξ-ενικοῦσι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 4.823.49 (Troezen, 4th c.BC)}}; so in later Gr., εἰσ-ήνικα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Supp.epigram” 7.381,382 (Dura-Europos, 3rd c.AD); ἤνιγκα [prev. work]383 ([prev. passage], 3rd c.AD)}}:—_middle_, _participle_ ἐξ-ε[νικ]άμενος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12 (2).526a5 (Eresus, 4th c.BC).}}
**IV.2.b** _Boeotian dialect_ _aorist 1_ in _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἴνιξαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 7.2418.24 (Thebes, 4th c.BC)}}; _1st pers._ _singular_ ἤνειγξα Hdn.Gr.2.374.
**V** other tenses: _perfect_ ἐνήνοχα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.108, 22.62, (ἐξ-) 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pr.Im.” 15, 17, (μετ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 113a, (συν-) see reading in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.22}}:—_passive_, _future_ ἐνεχθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 205b12, 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ τῶν ὀχουμένων” 2.2, +others, (ἐπ-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.56, (κατ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 13.19}}: _aorist_ ἠνέχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.12}} and frequently in compounds; _Ionic dialect_ ἀπ-ηνείχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.66, etc.}}; (περι-) {{Refs|[prev. work] 84}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ written ἠνείχτθησαν in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 707 B 9 (Ephesus, 6th c.BC)}}; _Doric dialect_ _participle_ ἐξ-ενειχθείς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.115 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; Hellenistic ἐνεγχθείς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 327.42 (3rd c.BC), (συμπερι-) “IPE” 12.32 A 31,78, B 70 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC)}}; in dialects, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _indicative_ ἀπ-ηνίχθη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).103.111 (Epid., 4th c.BC); _3rd.pers._ _sing._}}_subjunctive_ ἐξενιχθῇ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]12(5).593 A 23 (Ceos, 5th c.BC), “Abh.Berl.Akad.” 1928(6).21 (Cos, 3rd c.BC)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ ἐν-ενιχθεῖ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 7.3172.150 (Thespiae, 3rd c.BC)}}; _participle_ (_neuter_) ἐπ-ενιχθέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Abh.Berl.Akad.” 1928(6).53 (Telos, 4th c.BC, unpublished)}}; _Attic dialect_ _perfect_ ἐνήνεγμαι, ἐνήνεκται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 584d, εἰσ-ενήνεκται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1340; ἀν-ενήνεγκται “IG” 12.91.4; ἐπαν-ενήνειγκται [prev. work]22.1607a7}} _Ionic dialect_ ἐξ-ενηνειγμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.37}}; _Attic dialect_ _pluperfect_ προσ-ενήνεκτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.3.20}}; _participle_ κατ-, μετ-ενηνεγμένος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.30.2, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.1.12. (With φέρω}} cf. Latin _fero,_ O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **beran,** Sanskrit _bhárati_ 'bear'; οἴσω is of uncertain origin; ἐνεγκ- is probably _reduplicate_ ἐγκ- (ἐνεκ- in _passive_ forms and in δουρηνεκής, etc.), _cognate_ with Sanskrit _náśati_ 'attain,' Latin _nanciscor,_ Lithuanian _nèšti_ 'carry, bear'; ἐνεικ- (ἐνῐκ-) is of uncertain origin; the glosses ἐνέεικαν· ἤνεγκαν, and ἐνεείκω· ἐνέγκω {{Refs 5th c.AD+|(5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus) are not corroborated.)}}
**A** _active_,
**A.I** **bear** or **carry** a load, ἐν ταλάροισι φέρον μελιηδέα καρπόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.568; μέγα ἔργον, ὃ οὐ δύο γ᾽ ἄνδρε φέροιεν 5.303; ἦγον μὲν μῆλα, φέρον δ᾽ εὐήνορα οἶνον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.622; χοάς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 15; φ. ἐπ᾽ ὤμοις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 564; χερσὶν φ. [prev. author] “Ant.” 429; φ. ὅπλα βραχίονι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 14}}; **bear** (as a device) on one's shield, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 559, etc.}}; γαστέρι κοῦρον φ., of a pregnant woman, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.59}}; φ. ὑπὸ ζώνην or ζώνης ὕπο, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1000(992), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 762: in Trag. stronger than ἔχω, ἁγνὰς αἵματος χεῖρας φ.}} **to have** hands clean from blood, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 316 (variant{φορεῖς}); ἀλαὸν ὄμμα φέρων [prev. author] “Ph.” 1531 (Lyric poetry); γλῶσσαν εὔφημον φ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 581, compare “Supp.” 994; καλὸν φ. στόμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 930 codices (nisi to be read φορῇ)}}]; ἄψοφον βάσιν φ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 967 (Lyric poetry).}}
**A.II** **bear, convey,** with collateral notion of **motion,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, πῇ δὴ.. τόξα φέρει; 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.362}}; πρόσω φ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 369; εἴσω φέρω σ᾽ ἐντεῦθεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1444, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 914b; πόδες φέρον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.514; πέδιλα τά μιν φέρον 24.341, etc.}}; of horses,{{Refs|2.838; ἵππω.. ἅρμα οἴσετον 5.232, etc.}}; of ships, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.323, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.306; τὰ σώματα τῶν ζῴων συνέστηκεν ἐκ τοῦ φέροντος καὶ τοῦ φερομένου Diocl. fragment 17.}}
**A.II.b** of persons, **bring to bear,** μένος or μένος χειρῶν ἰθύς τινος φέρειν **hurl** one's strength right upon or against him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.602, 5.506}}; φ. τὴν ὀργήν, τὴν αἰτίαν ἐπί τινα, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.31.8, 33.11.2.}}
**A.II.c** **lead, direct,** τὴν πόλιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 6.}}
**A.II.2** of wind, **bear along,** [πνοιὴ Ζεφύρου] φ. νῆάς τε καὶ αὐτούς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.26; [σχεδίην] ἄνεμοι φέρον ἔνθα καὶ ἔνθα 5.330, compare 4.516, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.378, etc.; ἐπέλασσε φέρων ἄνεμος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.300, 7.277, compare 5.111, etc.: _absolutely_, ὁ βορέας ἔξω τοῦ Πόντου εἰς τὴν Ἑλλάδα φέρει}} **is fair** for Greece, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.77: _metaphorically_, ὅπῃ ἂν ὁ λόγος ὥσπερ πνεῦμα φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 394d; φ. τινὰ φρένες δύσαρκτοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1023, compare “Th.” 687 (Lyric poetry)}}:—_passive_, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**A.III** **endure, suffer,** λυγρά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.135; ἄτην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32}}; χαλινόν, ζυγόν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1066, 1226}}; πημονάς, τύχας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 293, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1024; ξυμφοράς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60; τὰς οὐ προσηκούσας ἁμαρτίας 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.10}}; also of food, ἐσθίουσι πλείω ἢ δύνανται φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.21}}; of strong wine, **bear, admit,** καὶ τὰ τρία φέρων καλῶς, i.e. three parts of water, instead of ἴσον ἴσῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1188, compare “Ach.” 354}}; so τὰς ἐπιδείξεις.. φέρουσιν αὐτοῦ (i.e. Ἰσοκράτους) οἱ λόγοι, τοὺς δὲ ἐν ἐκκλησίαις.. ἀγῶνας οὐχ ὑπομένουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isocrate” 2: _metaphorically_, ᾗ φέρειν πέφυκε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 48a.}}
**A.III.2** frequently with modal words, πήματα κόσμῳ φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.82; σιγῇ κακά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 738; ὀργῇ τὸν πόλεμον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.31; θυμῷ φ. [prev. author] 5.80; χαρᾷ φ. τι 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 19.1.13}}: especially with an _adverb_, [ὕβριν] ῥηϊδίως φ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 215}}; δεινῶς, βαρέως, πικρῶς, χαλεπῶς φέρειν τι, **bear** a thing impatiently, **take** it ill or amiss, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.γ, 5.19, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 610, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330a, etc.}}; δυσπετῶς, βαρυστόνως φ., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 752, “Eu.” 794}}; προθύμως φέρειν τὸν πόλεμον **to be** zealous **about** the war, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.18, 40; προθύμως τὰ τοῦ πολέμου ἔφερον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.36; αἶσαν φέρειν ὡς ῥᾷστα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 104; συμφορὴν ὡς κουφότατα φ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.35; ῥᾳδίως φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 522d, +others; εὐπετῶς φ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 585, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.6}}; εὐπόρως (εὐφόρως Brunck) ἐνεγκεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 873}}; εὐμενῶς, εὐχερῶς φ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 474e}}; these phrases are used mostly with _accusative_ _of things_; also with _participle_, βαρέως ἤνεικε ἰδών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.155, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 385, etc.; φ. ἐλαφρῶς.. λαβόντα ζυγόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.93; ῥᾳδίως φέρεις ἡμᾶς ἀπολείπων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 63a: with _genitive_, τοῦ ἐνδεοῦς χαλεπώτερον φ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77, compare 2.62; ἐπί τινι, χαλεπῶς φ. ἐπὶ τῇ πολιορκίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.21, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.232; πράως ἐπὶ τοῖς γιγνομένοις φ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.55: with _dative_ only, βαρέως φέρειν τοῖς παροῦσι, τῇ ἀτιμίᾳ, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.3, “HG” 3.4.9, compare 5.1.29}}; later, χαλεπῶς φ. διά τι, πρός τι, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.111, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.17c codices}}
**A.IV** **bring, fetch,** εἰ.. θεὸς αὐτὸν ἐνείκαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.196; φ. ἄποινα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.502; ἄρνε 3, 120, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 95}}; ὕδωρ, οἶνον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 62.1; ἔντεα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.191; τόξα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.359; κνημῖδας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 675; δᾷδα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1490, etc.; γῆν τε καὶ ὕδωρ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.131}}:—_middle_, **carry** or **bring with** one, or **for one's own use,** ποδάνιπτρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.504; οἶνον 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 35, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.67, 7.50, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.14.1; φερνὰς δόμοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1282}}; **fetch,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.410; χοὰς ἐκ κρήνης 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 470.}}
**A.IV.2** **bring, offer, present,** δῶρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.428, etc.; μέλος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.3; χοάς τινι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 487; φ. πέπλον δώρημά τινι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 602; πρός τινα δῶρα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.31}}; χάριν τινὶ φ. **grant** any one a favour, **do** him a kindness, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.211, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.307, +others; ἐπὶ ἦρα φ. τινί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.572, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.164, etc.}}; φ. τισὶ εὐνοίας, ὄνησιν ἀστοῖς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 489, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 287; but after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, χάριν τινὶ φ.}} **show** gratitude to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).17}}; μῆνιν φ. τινί **cherish** wrath against.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Niob.” in “PSI” 11.1208.12.}}
**A.IV.2.b** ={ἄγω}{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|iv. 1, ἄχρι νῦν καθ᾽ ὥραν ἔτους λέγονται πένθος ἐπὶ Μελεάγρῳ φέρειν 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 2.7}}; Ἰάλεμος· ὁ ἐπὶ τοῖς ἀπολωλόσιν ἀνίαν φέρων, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}:—_middle_, τοῦ γονέως ἐφ᾽ ᾧ γε τὸ πένθος φέρεσθε {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 103.1.}}
**A.IV.3** **bring, produce, cause,** [ἀστὴρ] φέρει πυρετὸν βροτοῖσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.31; ὄσσαν.. ἥ τε φ. κλέος ἀνθρώποισι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.283, compare 3.204}}; φ. κακόν, πῆμα, ἄλγεα, etc., **work** one woe, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.541, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.231, 427, etc.}}; δηϊοτῆτα φ. **bring** war,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|6.203; ἐπ᾽ ἀλλήλοισι φ. Ἄρηα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.132, compare 8.516; πόλεμον 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 150; θάνατον φ. 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.134; τοῦτο εὐδοξίαν σοι οἴσει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 312c; τὸ σωθῆναι τὸ ψεῦδος φέρει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 109; τέχναι.. φόβον φέρουσιν μαθεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1135 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὥσπερ τὸ δίκαιον ἔφερε as justice **brought with it, brought about,** i.e. as was just, no more than just, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.58; ἀν᾽ ὄ κα φέρῃ ὁ λόγος ὁ ταμία Φιλοκλέος “IG” 42(1).77.13 (Epid., 2nd c.BC)}}; of a calculation, **yield** a result, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 349.27}}; **produce, adduce, bring forward,** παραδείγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.6, etc.; πάσας αἰτίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.22; ἁρμόττουσαν εἰκόνα [prev. author] 61.10}}:—_passive_, εἰς τὴν συνηγορίαν.. τοιαῦτά τινα φέρεται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.3.}}
**A.IV.3.b** **bring** or **carry with one, involve,** τὸ πᾶν ἡμῖν τοῦ πολέμου φέρουσιν αἱ νέες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.62; οὐ ξύλων ἀγὼν ὁ τὸ πᾶν φέρων ἐστὶ ἡμῖν, ἀλλ᾽ ἀνδρῶν [prev. work] 100.}}
**A.IV.4** μῦθον φ. τινί **bring** one word, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.288, 15.202}}; ἀγγελίην φ. **bring** a message,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 175, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.408; λόγον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.38; ἐπιστολὰς φ. τινί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 781, compare “Tr.” 493; ἐπιστολήν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 8.3}}: hence, **tell, announce,** πευθώ, φάτιν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 370, “Ag.” 9; σαφές τι πρᾶγος [prev. author] “Pers.” 248 (troch.), compare “Ag.” 639, etc.}}; **report,** ἀγήν (breakages) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 15r27 (3rd c.BC)}}; φ. κεχωνευκώς **reports** that he has.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 741.26, compare 147.4, 268.24 (all 3rd c.BC)}}; **enter, book** a payment made, {{Refs|“PBaden” 47.12}}:—_middle_, λόγους φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 583}}; but also ἀγγελίας ἔπος οἴσῃ thou **shalt have** it **brought** thee, **receive,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1546 (Lyric poetry); μαντήϊα.. φέρονται 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 134.9:}} —_passive_, θάνατον ἀνάγκη φέρεσθαι τοῦ διαθεμένου the death of the testator must be **announced, **{{NT|NT.Heb.9.16.}}
**A.IV.5** **pay** something **due** or **owing,** φόρον τέσσαρα τάλαντα φ. **pay** as a tax or tribute, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.57, compare “IG” 12.57.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 298a, “PCair.Zen.” 467.7 (3rd c.BC)}}; δασμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.10}}; σύνοδον φ. **subscribe to the expense** of a meeting, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 22.1012.14, 1326.6; χρήματα πᾶσι τάξαντες φ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.19; μισθὸν φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.12}} (but usually, **receive, draw,** pay, μισθὸν δύο δραχμὰς τῆς ἡμέρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 66; τέτταρας τῆς ἡμέρας ὀβολοὺς φέρων 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 357; αἱ νῆες μισθὸν ἔφερον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.21, “Oec.” 1.6); φ. ἐννέα ὀβολοὺς τῆς μνᾶς τόκους 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 1.2, compare 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 23}}; also of property, **bring in, yield** as rent, φ. μίσθωσιν τοῦ ἐνιαυτοῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.35.}}
**A.IV.6** **apply, refer,** τι ἐπί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 37e, “Chrm.” 163d, “R.” 478b, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.36.7, +others}}; φ. τὰ πράγματα ἐπί τινα **confer** powers upon, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.50.6.}}
**A.IV.7** ψῆφον φ. **give** one's vote, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 674, 680, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.2, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.18}}; ψῆφος καθ᾽ ἡμῶν οἴσεται (_passive_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 440; περὶ ταύτης ἡ ψῆφος οἰσθήσεται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.45; ὑπὲρ ἀγῶνος 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 7, compare 11: hence φ. τινά}} **appoint** or **nominate** to an office, φ. χορηγόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.130, 39.7, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 753d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1266a10:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; ὅπως φέρηται ἐν τῷ στρατιωτικῷ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 15.10 (2nd c.BC)}}; τῶν φερομένων ἐν Κλεοπάτρᾳ κληρούχων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PRein.” 10.13, +others (2nd c.BC)}}; φερομένου μου ἐν τῇ συνοχῇ since I **am enrolled** in prison, i.e. am in prison, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1821.21 (1st c.BC)}}:—_middle_, **choose, adopt,** ταύταν φ. βιοτάν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 785 (Lyric poetry).}}
**A.V** **bring forth, produce,** whether of the earth or of trees, φ. ἄρουρα φάρμακα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.229; ἄμπελοι φ. οἶνον 9.110}}; [νῆσος] φ. ὥρια πάντα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 131, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 117; [οὐ] γῆ καρπὸν ἔφερε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.139; γύαι φ. βίοτον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 196.5, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.41, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 593, etc.}}: _absolutely_, **bear fruit, be fruitful,** εὖτ᾽ ἂν τάδε πάντα φέρῃσι {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 91}}; ἡ γῆ ἔφερε (καρπόν add. codices quidam) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.82; αἱ ἄμπελοι φέρουσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.4}}; also of living beings, τόπος ἄνδρας φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 24c; ἤνεγκεν αὐτὸν Λαοδίκεια 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.25.1; ἡ ἐνεγκοῦσα}}**one's country,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.29, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 2.66, +others, Chor. p.81 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 3.26, uncertain in “Supp.epigram” 4.439 (Milet.) without _Article_ (also ἡ ἐνεγκαμένη 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Epistulae” 202)}}; or **Mother Earth,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 4.48}}: generally, **create, form,** Πηνειὸς Τέμπη φ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.25; [τὰ βρέφη] ἄρχεται φέρειν τοὺς ὀδόντας 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 4.9; φ. τοὺς κυνόδοντας “Geoponica” 16.1.14.}}
**A.VI** **carry off** or **away,** Κῆρες ἔβαν θανάτοιο φέρουσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.302; φ. τινὰ ἐκ πόνου 14.429, 17.718, etc.}}; of winds, [ἔπος] φέροιεν ἀναρπάξασαι ἄελλαι may the winds **sweep away** the word, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.409}}; of a river, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.189}}:—_middle_, **carry off with one,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.19.}}
**A.VI.2** **carry away as booty** or **prize,** ἔναρα, τεύχεα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.480, 17.70; αἶγα λέοντε φ. 13.199}}; δεῖπνον φ., of Harpies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 51; ἐνέχυρα βίᾳ φ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.11}}; in the phrase φέρειν καὶ ἄγειν {{Refs|compare (ἄγω 1.3), “IG” 12.69.19}}; φέροντα ἢ ἄγοντα Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.60; αἴ κα.. ἄγῃ ἢ φέρῃ “Leg.Gort.” 5.37; ἥρπαζον καὶ ἔφερον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.17; κείρων ἢ φέρων “IG” 12(9).90.10 (Tamynae, 4th c.BC)}}; αἴ τίς κα.. φέρει τι τῶν ἐν τᾷ ἱαρᾷ γᾷ {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.128}}; of a divorced wife, αἰ δέ τι ἄλλο φέροι τῶ ἀνδρός, πέντε στατῆρανς καταστασεῖ κὤτι κα φέρῃ αὐτόν {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 3.2}}; φέρειν alone, **rob, plunder,** θεῶν ἱερά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 804; ἀλλήλους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.7; _absolutely_, “SIG” 38.23 (Teos, 5th c.BC)}}:—_passive_, φερόμενοι Βακχῶν ὕπο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 759}}:—_middle_ in same sense, ἔναρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.245; πελέκεας οἶκόνδε φ. 23.856; ἀτερπέα δαῖτα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.124, compare 15.378.}}
**A.VI.3** **carry off, gain,** especially **by toil** or **trouble, win, achieve,** both _active_ and _middle_, ἤ κε φέρῃσι μέγα κράτος ἦ κε φεροίμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.308; φέρειν τρίποδα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 657; τἀπινίκια 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 692; τιμήν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1278}}; τἀριστεῖα, τὰ νικητήρια, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 468c, “Lg.” 657e; πέρα.. οὐδὲν φ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 651; ἐκ σοῦ πάντ᾽ ἄνευ φόβου φ. [prev. author] “OT” 590; τίς.. πλέον τᾶς εὐδαιμονίας φέρει ἤ..; [prev. work] 1190 (Lyric poetry),compare “El.” 1088 (Lyric poetry)}}; in bad sense, μείζω τὴν αἰσχύνην φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 671e}}: also, **receive one's due,** φ. χάριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 764; ὡς τοῦτό γ᾽ ἔρξας δύο φέρῃ δωρήματα [prev. author] “Ph.” 117; μισθὸν φέρειν (see. above iv. 5)}}; of a priest's perquisites, φέρει ὁ ἱαρεὺς γέρη σκέλη κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“BMus.Inscription” 968 A 9 (from Cos), compare “IG” 12.24.10, +others, “SIG” 56.35 (Argos, 5th c.BC):}} —_middle_ (see. ad [near the start]), **win for oneself,** κῦδος οἴσεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.217}}; δέπας, τεύχεα, **carry off as a prize,** {{Refs|23.663, 809, +others}}; ἀέθλια or ἄεθλον φ. **carry off, win** a prize, {{Refs|9.127, 23.413}}; τὰ πρῶτα φέρεσθαι (i.e. ἄεθλα) {{Refs|23.275, 538}}; οὐ σμικρὸν ἆθλον τῆς ἐρωτικῆς μανίας φέρονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 256d}}; of perquisites, τὸ.. σκέλος τοὶ ἱαρομνάμονες φερόσθω (i. e. φερούσθω from *φερόνσθω) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).40.13 (Epid., 5th-4th c.BC): hence οὐ τὰ δεύτερα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.104}}; πλέον φέρεσθαι **get** more or a larger share **for onself, gain** the advantage over any one, τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.211, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 500 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 308}}; ταῦτα ἐπὶ σμικρόν τι ἐφέροντο τοῦ πολέμου this **they received** as a small help towards the war, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.129; ἠνείκατο παρὰ Ἐγεσταίων τὰ οὐδεὶς ἄλλος 5.47; ἴδια κέρδεα προσδεκόμενοι παρὰ τοῦ Πέρσεω οἴσεσθαι 6.100; χάριν φέρεσθαι παρ᾽ ὑμῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.9; φ. τὴν ἀπέχθειαν αὐτῶν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.2; ὀνείδη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 762a; εὐσέβειαν ἐκ πατρὸς οἴσῃ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 969; δάκρυ πρὸς τῶν κλυόντων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 638; ἀπό τινος βοσκάν [prev. author] “Eu.” 266 (Lyric poetry); ἐξ ἀνανδρίας τοὔνομα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.131}}: generally, **get for one's own use and profit, take and carry away,** especially **to one's own home,** τοῦ.. πάμπρωτα παρ᾽ ἀγλαὰ δῶρα φέροιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.97: hence φέρειν or φέρεσθαι is often used pleonastic, see below xi.}}
**A.VII** _absolutely_, of roads or ways, **lead** to a place, ὁδὸν φέρουσαν ἐς ἱρόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.122, compare 138}}; τὴν φέρουσαν ἄνω (i.e. ὁδόν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.69; τῆς μὲν ἐς ἀριστερὴν ἐπὶ Καρίης φ., τῆς δὲ ἐς δεξιὴν ἐς Σάρδις [prev. author] 7.31; ἐπὶ Σοῦσα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.15; ἁπλῆ οἶμος εἰς Ἅιδου φέρει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 239; ἡ ἐς Θήβας φέρουσα ὁδός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24 (but ἡ ἐπ᾽ Ἀθηνῶν φέρουσα [prev. passage])}}; also ἡ θύρα ἡ εἰς τὸν κῆπον φ. the door **leading** to the garden, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.53}}; αἱ εἰς τὴν πόλιν φ. πύλαι, αἱ ἐπὶ τὸ τεῖχος φ. κλίμακες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.7, compare “PMich.Zen.” 38.27 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.12.3.}}
**A.VII.2** of a district or tract of country, **stretch, extend** to or towards, φέρειν ἐπί or ἐς θάλασσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.99}}; ἐς τὴν μεσόγαιαν{{Refs|[prev. work] 100; πρὸς νότον [prev. author] 7.201}}; ἡ ἀπὸ δυσμῶν αὐτῆς (i.e. τῆς Κιμβρικῆς) καὶ ἐπὶ τὸν Ἄλβιν φέρουσα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Geographia” 2.11.2, compare 3.}}
**A.VII.2.b** of time, τῇ νυκτὶ τῇ φερούσῃ εἰς τὴν β τοῦ Παχών {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.x (3rd c.BC),compare “PTeb.” 61 (b) 288 (2nd c.BC), “BGU” 1832.5 (1st c.BC), etc.}}
**A.VII.3** _metaphorically_, **lead** to or towards, **be conducive** to, ἐς αἰσχύνην φέρει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.10; τὰ ἐς ἄκεσιν φέροντα [prev. author] 4.90}}; ἐς βλάβην, ἐς φόβον φέρον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 517, 991; εἰς ὄκνον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 295}}: especially in good sense, **tend, conduce** to one's interest, ἐπ᾽ ἀμφότερά τοι φέρει (_impersonal_) ταῦτα ποιέειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3. 134; so τὰ πρὸς τὸ ὑγιαίνειν φέροντα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.31; τροφαὶ μέγα φ. εἰς ἀρετάν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 562 (Lyric poetry)}}; μέγα τι οἰόμεθα φέρειν (i.e. κοινωνίαν γυναικῶν τε καὶ παίδων) εἰς πολιτείαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449d}}; τὰ καλὰ ἐπιτηδεύματα εἰς ἀρετῆς κτῆσιν φ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 444e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.42}}; τοῦτο ἔφερεν αὐτῷ **was for** his **good,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 5.8.}}
**A.VII.3.b** **point** to, **refer** to a thing, ἐς τί ὑμῖν ταῦτα φαίνεται φέρει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120}}; φωνὴ φέρουσα πρός τινα **addressed** to him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.159; ἐς ἀρηΐους ἀγῶνας φέρον τὸ μαντήϊον [prev. author] 9.33, compare 6.19}}; [ὄψις] φέρει ἐπὶ πᾶσαν γῆν **refers** to.., **extends** over.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.19}}; τὰ ἴχνη τῆς ὑποψίας εἰς τοῦτον φ. **point** to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.10; πρός τινας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 538c; ταύτῃ [ὁ] νόος ἔφερε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.120}}; ἡ τοῦ δήμου φέρει γνώμη, ὡς.., the people's opinion **inclines** to this, that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.11; ἐπὶ τὸ αὐτὸ αἱ γνῶμαι ἔφερον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.79: with}}_infinitive_, τῶν ἡ γνώμη ἔφερε συμβάλλειν whose opinion **inclined to** giving battle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.110, compare 5.118}}; πλέον ἔφερέ οἱ ἡ γνώμη κατεργάσεσθαι his opinion **inclined** rather to the view.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.100, compare 3.77.}}
**A.VIII** **carry** or **have in the mouth,** i. e. **speak of,** πολύν τινα ἐν ταῖς διαβολαῖς φέρειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.223}}; **use** a word, οὐκ οἶδα καθ᾽ ὁποτέρου τούτων οἱ παλαιοὶ τὸ τῆς ζειᾶς ἔφερον ὄνομα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ λεπτυνούσης διαίτης - CMG” 6, compare 7.644, 15.753, 876}}; **record** an event, οἱ δευτέρῳ μετὰ τὴν ἔξοδον.. ἔτει φέροντες αὐτήν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.63: more frequently in}}_passive_, πονηρῶς, εὖ, φέρεσθαι, **to be** ill or well **spoken of,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.17, 2.1.6; ἀτίμως ὑπὸ τῶν ἀνθρώπων φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 328e}}; _absolutely_, φέρεται [the report] **is carried about,** i.e. **it is said,** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, τοιόνδε φέρεται πρῆγμα γίνεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.104 (variant)}}; ἐν χρόνοις φέρεται μνημονευομένοις **is recorded as occurring** within historical times, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.3.15; ὅτε καὶ Δημόκριτος φέρεται τελευτήσας 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “Vit.Hippocr.” 11; κρίνομεν.. τὰ γραφέντα ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν προστάγματα ἐν τοῖς ἱεροῖς νόμοις φέρεσθαι παρ᾽ ὑμῖν “OGI” 331.60 (Pergam., 2nd c.BC)}}; ὧν τὰ ὀνόματα φέρεται **are in use,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Geographia” 7.4.11}}; of literary works, **to be in circulation,** ἐπιστόλιον αὐτοῦ τοιοῦτον φέρεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.808a, compare 209e, 832d, 833c, +others, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.189b, “Geoponica” 2.35.8, 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.456 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}; πρόλογοι διττοὶ φέρονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Arg. Euripides Tragicus “Rh.”}}; ὁ στίχος οὗτος ἔν τισιν οὐ φέρεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 377, cf. Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.557.}}
**A.VIII.2** of words, φέρεσθαι ἐπί τι to **refer** to something, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 61.5, “Synt.” 21.14, +others}}
**A.IX** _imperative_ φέρε like{ἄγε}, as _adverb_, **come, now, well,**
**A.IX.1** before another _imperative_, φέρε γὰρ σήμαινε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 296 (_anapaest meter_); φέρ᾽ εἰπὲ δή μοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 534; φ. δή μοι τόδε εἰπέ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 385b; so φέρετε.. πειρᾶσθε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.127.}}
**A.IX.2** before _1st pers._ _singular_ or _plural_ of _subjunctive_ used imperatively, φέρε ἀκούσω, φέρε στήσωμεν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11, 97; φ. δὲ νῦν.. φράσω [prev. author] 2.14; φέρ᾽ ἴδω, τί δ᾽ ἥσθη; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 4; φέρε δὴ κατίδω [prev. author] “Pax” 361, compare 959}}; φ. δὴ ἴδωμεν, φ. δὴ σκεψώμεθα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 455a, “Prt.” 330b, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1281 (Lyric poetry), “Ph.” 276, etc.: less frequently before 2 _person_, φέρε.. μάθῃς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 300.}}
**A.IX.3** before a rhetorical question, φέρε.. τροπαῖα πῶς ἀναστήσει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 571; φ. δὴ νῦν.. τί γαμεῖθ᾽ ἡμᾶ; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 788 (_anapaest meter_), compare “Ach.” 541, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 348c}}; φ. μῶν οὐκ ἀνάγκη..; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 805d}}; φ. πρὸς θεῶν πῶς..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 514d; frequently in phrase φέρε γάρ, φέρε τίς γὰρ οὗτο; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 218; φ. γὰρ πρὸς τίνας χρὴ πολεμεῖ; 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.183, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.36; also φ. δή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 455a, +others}}: usually first in a sentence, but τὴν ἀνδρείαν δὲ φ. τί θῶμε; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 633c, etc.}}
**A.IX.4** φέρε δή, ἐάν πῃ διαλλαχθῶμεν.. **come** let us see if we can.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cra.” 430a.}}
**A.IX.5** φέρε with _infinitive_, **suppose, grant** that.. φ. λέγειν τινά {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.98b}}; φ. εἰπεῖν **let us** say, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.93, 163, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 3.3; οἷον φ. εἰ. 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “in Nicomachi arithmeticam introductionem” p.47 P., +others (οἷον φέρε alone, 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 11p.439M.).}}
**A.X** _participle_ _neuter_ τὸ φέρον, as _substantive_, **destiny, fate,** τὸ φ. ἐκ θεοῦ [καλῶς] φέρειν [χρή] ye must bear nobly **what** heaven **bears to you, awards you,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1693 (Lyric poetry, codices, sed secl. καλῶς, χρή); εἰ τὸ φερον σε φέρει, φέρε καὶ φέρου “Anthologia Graeca” 10.73 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus).}}
**A.X.2** _participle_ φέρων in all genders frequently joined with another Verb:
**A.X.2.a** to express a subsidiary action, φέρων ἔδωκε **he brought and** gave, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.146}}; δὸς τῷ ξείνῳ ταῦτα φέρων **take** this **and** give it him, {{Refs|17.345}}; ἔγχος ἔστησε φέρων **brought** the spear **and** placed it, {{Refs|1.127}}; σῖτον παρέθηκε φέρουσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 139, +others,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 622; τοῦτο ἐλθὼν οἴκαδε φέρων τῷ πατρὶ ἔδωκα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 282e, compare “R.” 345b; so ὁ μὲν Ἐπίχαρμον.. εἰς δέκα τόμους φέρων συνήγαγεν 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Vita Plotini” 24; ἑκάστῃ ἐννεάδι τὰ οἰκεῖα φέρων συνεφόρησα [prev. passage], etc.}} sometimes translatable by **with,** ᾤχοντο φέροντες τὰ γράμματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.8.}}
**A.X.2.b** _intransitive_, in _passive_ sense, to denote unrestrained action, νῦν σε μάλ᾽ οἴω.. φέροντα.. φιλητεύσειν {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 159}}; φέρουσα ἐνέβαλε νηΐ φιλίῃ she **went and** rammed, rammed **full tilt,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.87}}; ὅταν ἐπὶ θάτερ᾽ ὥσπερ εἰς τρυτάνην ἀργύριον προσενέγκῃς, οἴχεται φέρον **down** it sinks, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.12; τὰ μὲν ἄλλα μέρη τοῦ πολέμου παρῆκαν, φέροντες δὲ παντὶ τῷ στρατεύματι πρὸς αὐτὸν Ἀκράγαντα προσήρεισαν}}**hurling themselves,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.17.8; εἰς τοῦτο φέρων περιέστησε τὰ πράγματα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.82; ὑπέβαλεν ἑαυτὸν φέρων Θηβαίοις [prev. work]90, compare 1.175, 3.143,146}}; in the following passages φέρων accompanies a Verb of throwing, giving, entrusting, or dedicating, and expresses wholehearted action, whether wise or unwise; there is always an _accusative_, frequently of the _reflexive_ _pronoun_, governed by the principal Verb (or perhaps by φέρων): ἐπεὶ ἐς τοὺς κρατῆρας ἐμαυτὸν φέρων ἐνέβαλον (i.e. ὁ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς) when I **went** (or **took) and** threw myself.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 13, compare “Fug.” 1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp.Arist. Cat.” 1, “Fab.” 6, “Per.” 12, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.30.1, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 8.14, “Frr.” 10,69, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.4; τὴν κατασκευὴν.. φέρων ἐδωρήσατο τῇ μητρί 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 31.27, compare 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 1.7; σεαυτὸν.. φέρων ἀπημπόληκας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 24; τί παθόντες.. τοῖς ἀτέκνοις τῶν γερόντων ἐσποιεῖτε φέροντες αὑτού; 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 6.3, compare “Ind.” 19, “Laps.” 22}}; ταύτῃ (i.e. τῇ ὀργῇ) φέρων ὑπέθηκεν ἑαυτόν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 24, compare “Per.” 7; τούτῳ φέροντες ὑποβάλλουσι τοὺς υἱούς [prev. author] 2.4b, compare “Luc.” 6, “Pomp.” 27, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 6.1, 2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 1.2; προσέθετο φέρων ἑαυτὸν ἐκείνῳ 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.456 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, comparepp.461,465 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus cited in 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see {Σεβηριανό; ἀλλὰ σοὶ μὲν, ὦ θεῶν πάτερ, ἐμαυτὸν φέρων ἀναθήσω} 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.231b.}}]
**A.X.3** ἔκκρισις.. ἐκ μικρῶν φέρουσα διαστημάτων **occurring** at short intervals, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.45.}}
**A.XI** φέρειν, φέρεσθαι are frequently added epexegetically to δίδωμι and similar Verbs, δῶκεν.. τρίποδα φέρειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.513, compare 16.665, 17.131; τεύχεα.. δότω φέρεσθαι 11.798, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.349, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 419, 454 (troch.).}}
**B** _passive_ is used in most of the above senses:—special cases:
**B.I** **to be borne** or **carried** involuntarily, especially **to be borne along** by waves or winds, **to be swept away,** φέρεσθαι ἀνέμοισι, θυέλλῃ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.82, 10.54, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 276 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; πᾶν δ᾽ ἦμαρ φερόμην, of Hephaestus **falling** from Olympus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.592}}; ἧκε φέρεσθαι he sent him **flying,** {{Refs|21.120}}; ἧκα πόδας καὶ χεῖρε φέρεσθαι I let **go** my hands and feet, let them **swing free** [in the leap], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.442, compare 19.468}}; μέγα φέρεται πὰρ σέθεν, of a word uttered, **comes** with weight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.87; βίᾳ φέρεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254a; πνεῦμα φερόμενον [prev. author] “R.” 496d; τὸ πνεῦμα κατὰ τὰς ἀναπνοὰς εἴσω τε καὶ ἔξω φέρεται 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.520; ῥεῖν καὶ φέρεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 411c; φ. εἰς τὸν Τάρταρον [prev. author] “Phd.” 114b}}; simply, **move, go,** ποῖ γᾶς φέρομα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1309 (_anapaest meter_); οὐκ οἶσθ᾽ ὅποι γῆς οὐδ᾽ ὅποι γνώμης φέρῃ [prev. author] “El.” 922, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1076 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.; of the excreta, τὰ φερόμενα.. εἰ μὲν αὐτομάτως φέροιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 9.12; πρὸς κοιλίαν φερομένην 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 4.19: _metaphorically_, εἰς τὸ λοιδορεῖν φέρῃ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 729; πρὸς τὴν τοῦ κάλλους φύσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.4}}; ἐπὶ ταὐτὸ φέρονται **have** the same **tendency,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.42 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; ἀπὸ δογμάτων καὶ ἀπὸ θεωρημάτων φ. 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 238.30}}; of veins, **to be conveyed,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.531}}; also ἡ φερομένη οὐσία (the doctrine of) **universal motion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 177c}}; οἱ φερόμενοι θεοί the **moving** gods, i. e. the stars and planets, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.3.9.}}
**B.I.2** frequently in _participle_ with another Verb of motion, φερόμενοι ἐσέπιπτον ἐς τοὺς Αἰγινήτας they fell into their hands **with a rush, at full speed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.91; ἀπὸ.. ἐλπίδος ᾠχόμην φερόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 98b; ἧκε φερόμενος εἰς τὴν ἑαυτοῦ φύσιν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.89.}}
**B.I.3** of voluntary and impulsive motion, ἰθὺς φέρεται μένει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.172}}; ὁμόσε τινὶ φέρεσθαι **come** to blows with him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.21; δρόμῳ φ. πρός τινα [prev. author] “HG” 4.8.37; φυγῇ εἰς ἑαυτοὺς φ. [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.4.23; ἥξει ἐπ᾽ ἐκεῖνον τὸν λόγον φερόμενος 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 59; φερόμενος ὑπ᾽ ὀργῆς 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 18.}}
**B.II** _metaphorically_, καλῶς, κακῶς φέρεσθαι, of things, schemes, etc., **turn out, prosper** well or ill, **succeed** or **fail,** οὔτ᾽ ἂν.. νόμοι καλῶς φέροιντ᾽ ἄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1074; κακῶς φ. τὰ ἑαυτοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.25; εὖ φέρεται ἡ γεωργία [prev. author] “Oec.” 5.17}}; ὀλιγώρως ἔχειν καὶ ἐᾶν ταῦτα φέρεσθαι to neglect things and let them **take their course,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.67}}; less frequently of persons, **fare** well or ill, εὖ φερόμενος ἐν στρατηγίαις **being generally** successful.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.16, compare 15; καλῶς φερόμενος τὸ καθ᾽ ἑαυτόν [prev. author] 2.60; φ. ἐν προτιμήσει παρά τινι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 33.5; χεῖρον φερομένη παρὰ τἀδελφῷ 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 16.7.6}}; of euphonious writing, σύνθεσις καλῶς φερομένη {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.26.}}
**B.II.2** **behave,** ὑποκριτικῶς, ἀστάτως, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 38.20, 197.8, +others}}
**B.II.3** **have an opinion,** ὅπως ἠνέχθη περὶ τοῦ σφυγμοῦ what **his opinion was** about.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Marcellinus Medicus “de Pulsibus - Festschrift zur 49. Versammlung deutscher Philologen und Schulmänner” 233.}}
**C** _middle_: for its chief usages, see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus VI. 3.}}

5343:
** φεύγω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.472, etc.: _Epic dialect_}}_infinitive_ φευγέμεν, φευγέμεναι, {{Refs|10.147, 21.13}}; _imperfect_ ἔφευγον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|22.158, etc., poetical φεῦγον 9.478, 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 5.8, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.13: iterative φεύγεσκον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.461, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.43}}: _future_ φεύξομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.307, etc.}}; also φευξοῦμαι in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus and Comedy texts, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 341, 346, “Hel.” 500, 1041, “Ba.” 659, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 203 (codex R), 1129, “Pl.” 447, “Av.” 932 (ἀπο-), 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 283}} (but uncertain where found in _Attic dialect_ Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 635c, +others, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.19}}; φευξεῖται is uncertain reading in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IPE” 12.24.11 (Olbia, 4th c.BC)}}; _future_ _active_ ἐκ-φεύξω only late, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 349b, cf. Chambry ii p.479):}}_aorist_ ἔφῠγον, _Ionic dialect_ φύγεσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.316}}: _perfect_ πέφευγα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.154 codices (see. below 11.1a)}}; _optative_ πεφεύγοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.609 (ἐκ-πεφευγοίην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 840)}}, _participle_ πεφευγότες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.12}}; _participle_ _perfect_ _passive_ πεφυγμένος in _active_ sense, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.488, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.18, etc. (in _passive_ sense, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 423)}}; _Epic dialect_ πεφυζότες (compare φύζα) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.6, 528, 532, 22.1, later _sing._ πεφυζώς 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 128}}; _Aeolic dialect_ πεφύγγων, see at {φυγγάνω}:—_middle_, μὴ φεύγησθε Anon.Hist. in {{Refs|“PLit.Lond.” 115:}}_aorist 1_ δια-φεύξασθαι Decrees {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 25.}}
**I** _absolutely_, **flee, take flight,** opposed to διώκω, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.157, etc.; βῆ φεύγων ἐπὶ πόντον 2.665; πῇ φεύγει; 8.94; πόσε φεύγετ; 16.422; ποῖ φύγωμεν.. χθονό; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 777 (Lyric poetry); ποῖ τις οὖν φύγ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 403 (Lyric poetry); ἐνθένδε ἐκεῖσε φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 176b: with Preps., φ. ἀπό τινος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.120; φεύξονται ἀφ᾽ ἑαυτῶν εἰς φιλοσοφίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 168a, etc.}}; ἐκ πολέμοιο, ἐκ θανάτοιο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.118, 20.350; ἐκ κακῶν πεφευγέναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 437, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.65; ὑπὲκ κακοῦ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.700, compare 17.461 (rarely with _genitive_ only, πεφυγμένος ἦεν ἀέθλων (see. below 11) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.18; τῆς νόσου πεφευγέναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1044); φ. ἐς πατρίδα γαῖαν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.140, 159, +others}}; ἐπὶ Σάρδεων, ἐπὶ τὸν Ἑλικῶνα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.1, “Ages.” 2.11; πρὸς τὸ ὄρος [prev. author] “HG” 3.5.19; ὑπὸ γᾶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 175 (Lyric poetry); ὑπὸ δελφῖνος ἰχθύες φ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.23, compare 554 (compare below 111.2): with _accusative_ _cognate_, φύγε λαιψηρὸν δρόμον}} **ran** the course full swiftly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.121; τίνα φυγὴν φευξούμεθ; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1041}}; φ. τὴν παρὰ θάλασσαν (i.e. ὁδόν) **flee** by the shore route, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.12; compare below 111}}; for φυγῇ φεύγειν, see{{Refs|below 11.1, φυγή 1.1.}}
**I.2** _present_ and _imperfect_ tenses properly express only **the purpose** or **endeavour to get away**: hence _participle_ φεύγων is added to the _compound_ Verbs καταφεύγω, ἐκφεύγω, προφεύγω, to distinguish **the attempt** from **the accomplishment,** βέλτερον, ὃς φεύγων προφύγῃ κακὸν ἠὲ ἁλώῃ it is better that one should **flee and** escape than stay and be caught, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.81; φεύγων ἐκφεύγει 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.95, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 177; φ. καταφυγεῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23.}}
**I.3** φ. εἰς.. **have recourse** to.. **take refuge** in.., ἐς τοὺς ἀφώνους μάρτυρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1076.}}
**I.4** with _infinitive_, **shun** or **shrink from** doing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.76, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 26a}}; with _infinitive_ omitted, φεύγουσι γάρ τοι χοἱ θρασεῖς **shrink back,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 580.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **flee, avoid, escape,** Ἕκτορα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.327, etc.; φ. τινὰ ἐκ μάχης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.104; φ. ἐς τὴν Ἀσίην τοὺς Σκύθας [prev. author] 4.12; φ. θάνατον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.60; ἔνθ᾽ ἄλλοι μὲν πάντες, ὅσοι φύγον αἰπὺν ὄλεθρον, οἴκοι ἔσαν πόλεμόν τε πεφευγότες ἠδὲ θάλασσαν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.11}}; ἔφυγον κακόν, εὗρον ἄμεινον, formula used by μύσται, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.259}}; with modal _dative_, φ. ὄνειδος λόγοις, ἀμαχανίαν ἔργῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.90, “P.” 9.92}}; **avoid, shun,** χρὴ.. φεύγειν τὰ παχύνοντα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ λεπτυνούσης διαίτης - CMG” 12; τὴν ἀργίαν καὶ τὴν ἀκινησίαν τοῦ σώματος 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.93, compare 46, +others}}; φόνον φ. **flee the consequences of** the murder, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 796; αἷμα συγγενὲς φ. χθονός [prev. author] “Supp.” 148; τὰν Διὸς μῆτιν φ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 906 (Lyric poetry); ὀσμὴν.., μὴ βάλῃ, πεφευγότες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 412; φεύγων φυγῇ τὸ γῆρας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 195b; ἐς πόντον.. φύγε πέτρας νηῦς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.131}}; οὐδεμία [πόλις] πέφευγε (sed perhaps to be read ἀπέφυγε) δουλοσύνην πρὸς Ἱπποκράτεος at the hands of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.154}}: _participle_ _perfect_ _passive_ also retains the _accusative_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} in periphrastic phrases, μοῖραν δ᾽ οὔ τινά φημι πεφυγμένον ἔμμεναι ἀνδρῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.488; πεφυγμένον ἔμμεν ὄλεθρον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.455; οὔ οἱ νῦν ἔτι γ᾽ ἔστι πεφυγμένον ἄμμε γενέσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.219, compare “hymnus ad Venerem” 34:}} — but in _passive_ sense, τὸ πάραυτα πεφυγμένον κακόν Epicur. [prev. cited]
**II.b** **seek to avoid, shirk,** στρατείαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.162; εἰ τοῦτο φεύξονται καὶ μὴ 'θελήσουσι ποιεῖν [prev. author] 20.138}}; so in _aorist_, ἢν φύγῃ τις, ζημιοῦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 717.}}
**II.2** of things, ἡνίοχον φύγον ἡνία **escaped, slipped from his hands,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.465; Νέστορα δ᾽ ἐκ χειρῶν φύγον ἡνία 8.137, compare 11.128}}; τὸ φεῦγον the **part which slips,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 10.9, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 9, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).735: with double _accusative_, ποῖόν σε ἔπος φύγεν ἕρκος ὀδόντων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.350, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.64, etc.}}
**II.2.b** of wine, '**go off', turn sour,** {{Refs|Gp. 7.7.8.}}
**III** **flee one's country,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.478, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.259}}; οἱ φεύγοντες the **exiles,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 7.6; πατρίδα φ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.228, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.24; τὴν αὑτοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.26; ἅπασαν τὴν Ἀθηναίων ξυμμαχίδα “IG” 12.10.30; φ. ἐξ Ἄργεος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.224, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.85}}; ἐξ Ἀθηνέων, ἐκ τῆς πατρίδος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.103, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.3.}}
**III.2** φ. ὑπὸ Σκυθέων **to be expelled, driven out** by.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.125}}: but especially **to be exiled,** φ. ὑπὸ τοῦ δήμου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.27}}; φ. ἐξ Ἀρείου πάγου by their sentence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.44: also with _accusative_, φ. Πεισιστρατίδας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.62.}}
**III.3** _absolutely_, **go into exile, live in banishment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1668 (troch.), 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 242b; δύο ἔτη φευγέτω [prev. author] “Lg.” 867c}}; φ. ἀειφυγίαν **to be banished** for life, {{Refs|[prev. work] 871d, +others}}; φεόγειν Ἀμφίπολιν ἀειφυγίην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 194.3, compare 24 (Amphipolis, 4th c.BC)}}; but also ἐν ἀειφυγίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 877e; φεύγων ἀπ᾽ οἴκων ἃς ἐγὼ φεύγω φυγάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 976}}; φεύγοντες **being in exile,** opposed to φυγόντες **having gone into exile,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.33}}; with play on words, “μέχρι τίνος φεύξῃ, Ἀρκαδίω; καὶ ὅς, ἔς τ᾽ ἂν τοὺς ἀφίκωμαι οἳ οὐκ ἴσασι Φίλιππον” {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Duris Epigrammaticus 3 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**IV** as law-term (mostly in _present_ and _imperfect_, but{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.4 (see. below))}}, **to be accused** or **prosecuted at law**: ὁ φεύγων the **accused, defendant,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 893, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 405b, etc.; opposed to διώκω, οὔτε φεύγων ἁλοὺς οὔτε διώκων ἡττηθείς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.66}}; with _accusative_, φ. γραφάς, δίκην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 442 (Lyric poetry), “Nu.” 167; ὑπό τινος δίκας φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 19c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.1; οὐδενὶ πώποτε οὔτε ἡμεῖς οὔτε ἐκεῖνος δίκην οὔτε ἐδικασάμεθα οὔτε ἐφύγομεν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator [same place]; φ. ἀπολογίας 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.201}}; the crime being added in _genitive_, φόνου δίκην φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.9; γραφὰς φ. παρανόμων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.235}}; more frequently with _genitive_ only, φ. φόνου **to be charged with** murder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.31, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 133, etc.; φ. δειλίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1129; ξενίας [prev. author] “V.” 718 (_anapaest meter_)}}; with _genitive_ of the penalty, ἐὰν.. φεύγῃ δεσμῶν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 218.92 (Ilium, 3rd c.BC)}}; also περὶ θανάτου φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.95; φ. ἐπὶ μηνύσει τινός 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.18}}; ἀσεβείας φ. ὑπό τινος **is accused of** impiety by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 35d}}; rarely of things, τὸ φεῦγον ψήφισμα the decree **that is on its defence,** the decree **in question,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.58:—in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.214 αἰτίην φ.}} has the older sense, **flee from** a charge, **quit one's country** on account of a charge.
**IV.2** **plead in defence,** δεῖ τοί σε φεύγειν.. ὡς οὐκ ἔχουσι κῦρος [οἱ νόμοι] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 390}}; ἔφευγε μὴ εἰδέναι **pleaded** ignorance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 263}}, (Cf. Latin _fugio,_ Gothic _biugan_ 'bend', etc.)

5345:
** φήμη**, ἡ,
_Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ **φάμα**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20 a. 12, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.10}}; pseudo-_Doric dialect_ **φήμα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.194, +others, 3rd c.BC: Isyllus Lyricus 80}}: (φημί).
**I** **utterance prompted by the gods, significant** or **prophetic saying,** χαῖρε δὲ φήμῃ Ὀδυσσῆος φίλος υἱός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.35}}, ubi see {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia; in the prayer of Odysseus to Zeus, φήμην τίς μοι φάσθω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.100; followed by φήμην δ᾽ ἐξ οἴκοιο γυνὴ προέηκεν ἀλετρίς [prev. work] 105}}; φ. and κλεηδών are interchanged, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.72, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1109}} {φημί}; φ. about a τέρας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.153; εἴτε του θεῶν φήμην ἀκούσας εἴτ᾽ ἀπ᾽ ἀνδρὸς οἶσθα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 43, compare 86, 475 (Lyric poetry)}}; τοῦ ὀνείρου ἡ φ. the **message** of the dream, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.43; φ. μαντικαί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 723; φ. θεσφάτων [prev. author] “Tr.” 1150; μάντεων φῆμαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1056. compare “Ion” 180 (Lyric poetry); φήμη τις οἴκων ἐν μυχοῖς ἱδρυμένη [prev. author] “Hel.” 820; φήμας τε καὶ μαντείας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 111b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.21; φήμας καὶ ἐνύπνια καὶ οἰωνούς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.48, compare “Cyr.” 8.7.3, etc.; φήμης ἕνεκα}}**ominis causa,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 878a, compare 908a}}; τῇ πόλει (i.e. Aquileia) ἀετὸς οἴκιζομένῃ τὴν αὑτοῦ φ. χαρίζεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.72a; hence, comically, φήμη γ᾽ ὑμῖν ὄρνις ἐστί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 720}}; φ. ἀγαθὴν λέξομεν ={εὐφημίαν παρέξομεν}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 865 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**I.2** **report, rumour,** usually of uncertain and mysterious origin, φήμη οὔ τις πάμπαν ἀπόλλυται, ἥν τινα πολλοὶ λαοὶ φημίξωσι· θεός νύ τίς ἐστι καὶ αὐτή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 763, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.128 (citing φήμη δ᾽ ἐς στρατὸν ἦλθε as from 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”)}}; Φήμης βωμός Scholia (at [prev. work]), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.17.1}}; **common report,** opposed to συκοφαντία, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.145; φάμα δ᾽ ἦλθε κατὰ πτόλιν 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica [same place]; ἄμβροτε Φ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 158 (Lyric poetry); φ. ἐσέπτατο ἐς τὸ στρατόπεδον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.100; φ. δημόθρους 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 938; τίν᾽ ἔχων φ. ἀγαθὴν ἥκει; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1320 (_anapaest meter_); φ. ὑπορρεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 672b; φήμην κατασκεδάσαι [prev. author] “Ap.” 18c.}}
**I.3** **report** of a man's character, **repute,** δεινὴν δὲ βροτῶν ὑπαλεύεο φήμην· φ. γάρ τε κακὴ πέλεται, κούφη μὲν ἀεῖραι—ῥεῖα μάλ᾽, ἀργαλέη δὲ φέρειν, χαλεπὴ δ᾽ ἀποθέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 760; ὑποδεέστερα τῆς φ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.11; περὶ τὸν τῶν ἀνθρώπων βίον.. καὶ πράξεις ἀψευδής τις πλανᾶται φ. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.127; τοιαύτην φ. σαυτῇ περιφυομένην 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.78: _plural_, ἐπιφέρειν γυναικείους ἑαυτοῖς φήμας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 935a; ἐπώνυμος ἐν φήμαις βροτῶν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 105}}:—especially of **good report, fame,** περιχαρὴς τῇ φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31; κατὰ τὴν εὐλογίαν καὶ τοὺς ἐπαίνους καὶ τὴν φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.134, compare 4.186; ὁ δ᾽ ὄλβιος ὃν φᾶμαι κατέχοντ᾽ ἀγαθαί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.10: but also φ. πονηραί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1045}}; αἰσχρὰ φ., opposed to καλὴ δόξα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.43; ψευδῆ φ. ὑμνεῖν κατὰ θεῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 822c, compare “R.” 463d.}}
**I.4** φᾶμαι **songs of praise,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.16; φάμα φιλοφόρμιγξ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 697 (Lyric poetry), compare “Th.” 866 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II** **any voice** or **words, speech, saying,** λόγων φ. _poetry_ _periphrastic_ for λόγοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 846 (Lyric poetry)}}; especially **common report, tradition, legend,** ἀλλ᾽ ἔστι φήμη.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 760; πολιαὶ φῆμαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 701 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c, “Lg.” 713c; αἱ ἀρχαῖαι φ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.3.2}}; μνήμην παρὰ τῆς φήμης λαβών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.3.}}
**II.b** **common report** or **parlance,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.242}}; ὅσους ἡ κοινὴ φ. παραδέδωκεν [θεούς] {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 17.}}
**II.2** **message,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 741, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1155, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 158 (Lyric poetry).}}

5346:
** φημί**, φῄς, φηις
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 316.1 (3rd c.BC), “PSI” 7.846.7 (Papyrus of 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus (?), 2nd-3rd c.AD)}}, cf. Hdn.Gr.2.147, 419; φησί (_apocopation_ φή {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 40)}}; _plural_ φᾰμέν, φᾰτέ, φᾱσ; _Doric dialect_ **φᾱμί**, φᾱσί or φᾱτί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 771, Anonymus in “PSI” 9.1091.11, 18)}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ φαντ; _Aeolic dialect_ **φᾶμι** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 32}}, _2nd pers._ _singular_ φαῖσθα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 20.6, _3rd.pers._ _sing._ φαῖσι [prev. work]26.5, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 66}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ φαῖσι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 5.2:}}_aorist 2_ ἔφην, _Epic dialect_ φῆν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.326; ἔφησθα 1.397, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 132, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 438a, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.164, etc. (rarely ἔφης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.280, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.23)}}, _Epic dialect_ φῆσθα [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.186, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.149 (variant{φῇσθα}), φῆς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.473, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.239 (variant)}}]; ἔφη, _Epic dialect_ φῆ, _Doric dialect_ φᾶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.11}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ἔφᾰμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.26 (ἔφημεν 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 184.7, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.158, 3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 524.16, 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.341 H.)}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ ἔφᾰτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.25}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔφασαν, _Epic dialect_ φάσαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.278, also ἔφᾰν, φάν, 3.161, 6.108; _imperative_ φαθί (on the accent see Hdn.Gr. 1.431, +others, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 264.4}}; φάθι is found in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 475e, +others)}}; _subjunctive_ φῶ, φῇς, φῇ, _Epic dialect_ φῇσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.168, φήῃ 11.128, 23.275}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _subjunctive_ φᾶντι {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.116}}; _optative_ φαίην, _1st pers._ _plural_ φαῖμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.81, 24.222, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.87}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ φαῖεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68, etc.}}; _infinitive_ φάναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.27, etc., poetry φάμεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.19}}; _participle_ φάς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.35, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.63, 141, “SIG” 279.18 (Zeleia, 4th c.BC), φᾶσα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.135, _plural_ φάντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.44, 14.126, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.139b:}}_future_ φήσω, _Doric dialect_ φασῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 739, etc.}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ φασοῦμες {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.1.133}}: _aorist 1_ ἔφησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus in “PSI” 11.1212.9, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.153, “PCair.Zen.” 19.3 (3rd c.BC) (ἔφασεν is uncertain [prev. work] 140.7)}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ φᾶσε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.66}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ (_Doric dialect_) φάσῃς [ᾱ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 32 (φήσῃς etc. codices 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes, corr. Bgk.); _optative_ φήσειε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.69, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 503}}, _participle_ φήσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.239}}, _infinitive_ φῆσαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 2.7:}} —_middle_ (chiefly _poetry_ in early writers), _imperfect_ and _aorist 2_ ἐφάμην, ἔφατο (also {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 437.6 (Delph., 3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 343.8 (3rd c.BC), “PSI” 4.437.8 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 4.5, etc.)}}, _Epic dialect_ φάτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.262, φάσθε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.200, 10.562, ἔφαντο}} _Epic dialect_ φάντο {{Refs|24.460; _imperative_ φάο 16.168, 18.171}}, φάσθω, φάσθ; _infinitive_ φάσθα; _participle_ φάμενος (also {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “Spir. Prooem., Eratosth.Prooem.”, “SIG” 364.83 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 236.4 (3rd c.BC), “PHamb.” 4.14 (1st c.AD), 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.12.2, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.228, etc.): _Doric dialect_}}_future_ φάσομαι [ᾱ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.43}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ πέφαται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.500}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _imperative_ πεφάσθω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 72d}}; but _participle_ πεφασμένος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.127, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 843 should be referred to φαίνω:}}_aorist_ ἐφάθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(ἀπ-, κατ-) 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Interpretatione” 18b39. The}}_present_ _indicative_ φημί is _enclitic_, except in _2nd pers._ _singular_ _present_ φῄς: φαμέν is _1st pers._ _plural_ _present_, φάμεν _poetry_ _infinitive_: φαντί is _3rd.pers._ _plural_, φάντι _participle_
**II** φάσκω supplied all moods of _present_ except the _indicative_, also _imperfect_ ἔφασκο; compare ἠμί. [ᾰ, except in φᾱσι, and in _masculine_ and _feminine_ _participle_ φάς, φᾶσα: in _infinitive_ φάναι ᾰ always; φᾶναι is corrupt in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 119.11 codices 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus]}}:—**say, affirm, assert,** either _absolutely_, or followed by _infinitive_, e. g. Λυσίθευς Μικίωνα φιλῖν (={-εῖν}) φησι {{Refs|“IG” 12.924}}, compare 57.48, or _accusative_ et _infinitive_; the _infinitive_ is frequently omitted, σὲ κακὸν καὶ ἀνάλκιδα φήσει (i.e. εἶναι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.153}}; also Κορινθίους τί φῶμε; what **shall we say of** them? {{NT+8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.12; φ. πρός τινα, πρὸς ξεῖνον φάσθαι ἔπος ἠδ᾽ ἐπακοῦσαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.584: less frequently with _dative_, αὐτοῖς NT.Matt.13.28}}; κατά τινος φ. to **speak** against him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 25: sometimes followed by ὡς, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.19, variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.7}}; by ὅτι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 487d, 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 30}}; by an _interrogative_ clause, μή ποτε φάσῃς ὅ τι γίνεται αὔριον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus}} [same place]; by _participle_, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 481c (perhaps to be read θῶμεν)}}; also parenthetic, τίνες, φῄς, ἦσαν οἱ λόγο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59c.}}
**II.b** since what one says commonly expresses a belief or opinion, **think, deem, suppose,** φῆ γὰρ ὅ γ᾽ αἱρήσειν Πριάμου πόλιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.37}}; φαίης κε ζάκοτόν τέ τιν᾽ ἔμμεναι ἄφρονά τε **you would say, would think,** he was.., {{Refs|3.220}}; ἶσον ἐμοὶ φάσθαι **to say** he is (i. e. **fancy** himself) equal to me, {{Refs|1.187, 15.167}}; μὴ.. φαθὶ λεύσσειν **think** not that you see, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.56}}; τί φῄ; what **say you?** i.e. what **think you?** (see.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|below 11.5); λέγ᾽ ἀνύσας ὅ τι φῄς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 349:}} —so φ. δεῖν, φ. χρῆναι, **deem** it right, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.48.}}
**II.c** **say,** i.e. **write,** of an author, ὡς ἔφημεν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.158, etc.}} —The _middle_ has all these senses as well as the _active_
**II** Special Phrases:
**II.1** φασί, **they say, it is said,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.638, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.42, etc.; parenthetically, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1109a35, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 223, etc.: Prose writers use φησί when quoting, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.89, etc.}}; φησίν **saith** He, {{NT|NT.1Cor.6.16}}; especially of an opponent's objection, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.112c; even after a plural, ὅ τοίνυν μέγιστον ἔχειν οἴονται.. καὶ αὐτός, φησί, τῶν εἰσιόντων ἦσθα 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 52.39}}; “τὸν δὲ μετ᾽ εἰσενόησα, ἔφη Ὅμηρος as H. **said,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315b.}}
**II.2** joined with a synonymous Verb, ἔφη λέγων, ἔφησε λέγων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.156, 6.137, etc.; ἔλεγε φάς [prev. author] 1.122}}; λέγει οὐδέν, φαμένη.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.22; τί ἐροῦμεν ἢ τί φήσομε; 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.37, compare 25.100; τί φ; τί λέξ; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 483.}}
**II.3** in repeating dialogues the Verb commonly goes before its subject, ἔφην ἐγώ, ἔφη ὁ Σωκράτης, **said** I, **said** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, but the order is sometimes inverted, ἐγὼ ἔφην, ὁ Σωκράτης ἔφη}} I **said,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}} **said.**
**II.4** inserted parenthetically, though the sentence has been introduced by λέγει, εἶπεν, etc., ὁ Ἰσχόμαχος.. εἶπεν· ἀλλὰ παίζεις μὲν σύ γε, ἔφη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 17.10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 164e; ἡ κρίσις.. διαρρήδην λέγει διότι, φησίν, ἔδοξε τἀληθῆ εἰσαγγεῖλαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.50.}}
**II.5** τί φημ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1471, and τί φῄ; “Ph.” 804, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 706 (uncertain), are used extra metrum, as exclamations.}}
**II.6** κυριώτατα φάναι, in parenthesis, strictly **speaking,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.374}}; ὡς οὕτω φάναι, ={ὡς εἰπεῖν, ἅπασαι ὡς οὕτω φάναι} **practically** all, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ λεπτυνούσης διαίτης - CMG” 9}}; συνελόντα (variant{-όντι}) φάναι, ={συνελόντι εἰπεῖν}, {{Refs|[prev. author] 16.502.}}
**II.7** Οἰδίπουν.. ἂν μόνον φῶ if I only **mention** Oedipus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 191.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 411a, 435a.}}
**III** like{κατάφημι}, **say yes, affirm, assert,** καὶ τοὺς φάναι and they **said yes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.88; καί φημι κἀπόφημι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 317; ἔγωγέ φημι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 526c; φάναι τε καὶ ἀπαρνεῖσθαι [prev. author] “Tht.” 165a}}: with _infinitive_, φῂς ἢ καταρνεῖ μὴ δεδρακέναι τάδ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 442}}; but οὔ φημι means **say no, deny, refuse,** with _infinitive_, ἡ Πυθίη οὐκ ἔφη χρήσειν **said** she would **not**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.19, compare 8.2; οὐκ ἔφασαν ἐπιτρέψαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.15, 47 (to be read -τρέψειν)}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, οὔ φημ᾽ Ὀρέστην σ᾽ ἐνδίκως ἀνδρηλατεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 221, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.63: _absolutely_, κἂν μὲν μὴ φῇ}} if he **says no,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 555 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἢ φάθι ἢ μὴ ἃ ἐρωτῶ answer me **yes** or no, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 475e}}; so in answers, φημί or οὕτως φημί **yes,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 270c, +others, “Grg.” 500e}}; οὐκ ἔφη he **said no,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 118a.}} — In this sense _Attic dialect_ writers, besides _present_, mostly use _future_ φήσω and _aorist_ ἔφησα, but in _imperfect_, _infinitive_, and _participle_ _present_, to avoid ambiguity, they prefer ἔφασκον, φάσκειν, φάσκων (see. φάσκω): φάναι is distinguished from φάσκειν, e.g. ἔφη σπουδάζειν **he said** he was in haste, ἔφασκε σπουδάζειν **he alleged** he was in haste; but ἔφησθα is found in this sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.7.}}
**IV** **command, order,** ἔφην τῷ Ὀρθοβούλῳ ἐξαλεῖψαί με {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.13 (so ἔφασαν, see l. for ἔφρασαν in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.11).}}

5348:
** φθάνω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.506}}, _imperfect_ ἔφθανον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.30, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.272 (from Bianor)}}: _future_ φθήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.444, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 375c, etc.}}; but φθάσω [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 3.13 (assuming variant), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.38}}: _aorist_ ἔφθᾰσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.161, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 752 (troch.), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.49, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.19, etc.; _imperative_ φθάσον 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 6.11.7}}; _optative_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ φθάσειε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.120, _plural_ φθάσειαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.14}} (this tense prevails in later Gk., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.66.1, etc.)}}; _Doric dialect_ ἔφθασσα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.115: but the only _Epic dialect_}}_aorist_ is ἔφθην,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|not found in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus or 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}, but the more usual form in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus and 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus, less frequently in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; _plural_ ἔφθημεν, -ητε, -ησαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1468, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.7, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.5}}, _Epic dialect_ _plural_ 3 φθάν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.51}}_subjunctive_ φθῶ, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ φθήῃ, φθῇσιν, {{Refs|16.861, 23.805}}; _Epic dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ φθέωμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.383}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ φθέωσι {{Refs|24.437}}; _optative_ φθαίην, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ φθαίησι (παρα-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.346}}; _infinitive_ φθῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.115, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.4}}; _participle_ φθάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71}}; _Epic dialect_ _participle_ _middle_ φθάμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.119, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 554:}}_perfect_ ἔφθᾰκα{{LXX+4th c.BC+|Philipp. cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.39, LXX.2Ch.28.9, “IG” 12 9).906.26 (Chalcis, 3rd c.AD)}}; πέφθακα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 2.10 (see. l)}}: _pluperfect_ ἐφθάκει {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 17, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 6}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 395a18}}: _imperfect_ ἐφθάνετο {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.278 (from Bianor)}}; ἐφθάνοντο [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.2.4 (variant{ἐφονεύοντο})}}]: _aorist_ ἐφθάσθην {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.25, “epigram Gr.” 315 (from Smyrna), “IPE” 2.197 (Panticapaeum, 2nd c.AD), 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.12.4. 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.560}}. [φθᾰνω always in _Attic dialect_ (so also in {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.272 (from Bianor), “APl.” 4.382, 384)}}; φθᾱνω in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.506, 21.262 (where 4th-3rd c.BC: Zenodotus Grammaticus read φθανέει for φθάνει)}}]:—**come** or **do first** or **before** others:
**I** with _accusative_ _person_, **to be beforehand with, overtake, outstrip,** in running or otherwise, φθάνει δέ τε καὶ τὸν ἄγοντα {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.262; φθῆ σε τελος θανάτοιο 11.451, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 554, 570, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.161, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 120, “IT” 669, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.42, etc.; οὐ μὴ φθάσωμεν τοὺς κοιμηθέντας NT.1Thes.4.15}}; so ἔφθησαν τὸν χειμῶνα they **anticipated** the storm, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.188; φθάσας τὸν λογισμόν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.38}}:—_passive_, **to be overtaken,** ὑπό τινος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 395a18, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.278 (from Bianor)}}; ἐφθάσθην (see. above).
**II** _absolutely_, **come** or **act first,** opposed to ὑστερέω or ὑστερίζω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 975, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.18, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.121}}; τοῦ φθάσαντος ἁρπαγή the prey of **the first comer,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 752 (troch.), compare “fragment” 23 (Lyric poetry); πρὶν ἐλθεῖν αὐτοὺς φθάσαι βουλόμενοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.36; μὴ φθάσῃ ἐς τὸν Ἑλλήσποντον ἐσπλεύσας [prev. author] 8, 100; φθάσαι πρὶν ἀδικηθῆναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1302b23, compare “Rh.” 1373a23}}; in later writers, τὰ φθάσαντα the things **before mentioned,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 1.34, 4th c.BC: Arg. Demosthenes Orator 46}}; _participle_ φθάνων, φθάσας **previous,** τῶν φθασάντων δυεῖν βιβλίων {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 3.1}}; ἐν τοῖς φθάνουσιν ἔργοις Dex.Hist.{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“fragment” 26 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; τοῖς φθάνουσι κατορθώμασι [prev. author] “fragment” 6 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; οἱ φθάσαντες πόνοι 6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus 5.16; τὸ φθάνον}}**previous time,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 14.6}}; τὸ φθάσαν, τὰ φθάσαντα, the **past,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus 3.2, +others, 5th-6th c.AD: Procopius Gazaeus Rhetor “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 32; ὁ φθάσας χρόνος 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus Prot. p.127 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**II.2** with Preps., **come** or **arrive first,** ἕως τῶν οὐρανῶν {{LXX+NT|LXX.2Ch.28.9; ἔφθασεν ἐφ᾽ ὑμᾶς NT.Matt.12.28, NT.Luke.11.20, cf. NT.1Thes.2.16: φ. εἰς}}.., simply, **arriveat, attain to,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Rom.9.31, NT.Phil.3.16, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.338a; φθάσομεν εἰς Πηλούσι[ον] “PPar.” 18.14 (2nd c.AD)}}: _absolutely_, of Time, **arrive,** καιρὸς τῆς τομῆς ἔφθακε (variant{ἔφθασεν}) {{LXX|LXX.Cant.2.12}}; ἔφθασεν ὁ μὴν ὁ ἕβδομος{{LXX|LXX.2Esd.3.1.}}
**II.2.b** **extend,** μέχρι γῆς {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.27; εἰς βορρᾶν “PFlor.” 50.87 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.2.c** **reach,** αἰθέρα {{Refs|“APl.” 4.384.}}
**II.2.d** Grammars, **to be applied** or **applicable,** ἐπ᾽ ἀμφοτέρας τὰς διαθέσεις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 211.22, compare 217.23, +others}}
**III** the action in which one **is beforehand** is expressed by the _participle_ agreeing with the subject, [Ἄτη] πολλὸν ὑπεκπροθέει, φθάνει δέ τε πᾶσαν ἐπ᾽ αἶαν βλάπτουσ᾽ ἀνθρώπους and **is beforehand** in doing men mischief, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.506}}; ἀλλ᾽ ἄρα μιν φθῆ Τηλέμαχος κατόπισθε βαλών Telemachus **was beforehand** with him **in** striking, i.e. struck **first,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.91, compare 16.383, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.368; ἔφθασέν με προαπελθὼν Χάρμος “PCair.Zen.” 16.3 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἔφθησαν ἀπικόμενοι arrived **first,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.136, compare 6.115}}; so φ. εὐεργετῶν **to be the first** to show a kindness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.14; ὅπως φθάσειαν βοηθήσαντες [prev. author] “HG” 7.2.14; ἔφθασαν προκαταλαβόντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.112; φθάνουσιν αὐτοὺς προκαταφυγοῦσαι [prev. author] 2.91; ἢν φθάσωσιν πρότερον διαφθείραντες τὸ στράτευμα [prev. author] 7.25; φ. γόνασι προσπεσὼν πατρός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 986, etc:}}_participle_ _passive_ is also used, ἦ κε πολὺ φθαίη πόλις ἁλοῦσα, i.e. it would be taken **first,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.815}}; εἴ κε φθήῃ τυπείς shall be wounded **first,** {{Refs|16.861}}; φθαίητε γὰρ ἂν.. ἐξανδραποδισθέντες ἣ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.108; μὴ φθάσωσι προεπιβουλευόμενοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.83; ἔφθη κατακωλυθείς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.17}}; φθάνειν δεῖ πεφραγμένους τοὶς πόρους they must **first** be blocked up, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.4.25: these clauses, being comparable in sense, are followed by a _genitive_, φθὰν δὲ μέγ᾽ ἱππήων.. κοσμηθέντες}} were drawn up **before** the drivers, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.51}}; more frequently by πρὶν.. or ἢ.., ἔφθη ὀρεξάμενος, πρὶν οὐτάσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|16.322, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.4; φθήσονται τούτοισι πόδες καὶ γοῦνα καμόντα ἢ ὑμῖν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.444; ἔφθης πεζὸς ἰὼν ἢ ἐγὼ σὺν νηΐ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.58}}; ἔφθησαν ἀναβάντες πρὶν ἢ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.70}}; ἔφθησαν ἐκπεσόντες πρότερον ἢ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.91.}}
**III.b** in later Gr., with _participle_ to express **previous** action or happening, φθάνω ὑμῖν πρότερον γεγραφηκώς I have **already** written to you, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1666.3 (3rd c.AD), compare 237 vi30 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.; ἔφθασα εἰρηκώς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 29; ὡς ἔφθην εἰπών [prev. author] “Par.” 3; compare 111.2b.}}
**III.2** in the same sense, _participle_ φθάς or φθάσας, _Epic dialect_ φθάμενος, is used like an _adverb_ with a principal Verb, ὅς μ᾽ ἔβαλε φθάμενος, for ὅς μ᾽ ἔφθη βαλών, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.119, compare 13.387, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.449}}; οὐκ ἄλλος φθὰς ἐμεῦ κατήγορος ἔσται no other shall be an accuser **before** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71}}; ἀνέῳξάς με φθάσας you opened the door **before** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1102; φθάσας προσπεσοῦμαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.9, compare 2.91, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.3, etc.}}; even with a _participle_, φθάσας.. ἁρπάσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.65}}; rarely _participle_ _present_, φθάνοντες δῃοῦμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.18.}}
**III.2.b** in _signification_ {{Refs|111.1b, φθάσαντες ἐπληρώσαμεν αὐτούς we had}}**already** paid them, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1103.6 (4th c.AD)}}; but ὡσεὶ καὶ ὁμογενῆ φθάσας εἶπον as if I **had** said (not **had already** said) ὁ., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.502.}}
**III.3** rarely with _infinitive_, ὁ φθάσας θαρσῆσαι he that **first** gains confidence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82}}; σπεύδειν ὅπως.. φθαίης ἔτ᾽ εἰς ἐκκλησίαν ἐλθεῖν (variant{ἐλθών}) hurry **to be in time** to get to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 935 (Lyric poetry), compare “Nu.” 1384 (see. infr. IV. 1)}}; μόλις φθάνει θρόνοισιν ἐμπεσοῦσα μὴ χαμαὶ πεσεῖν hardly **manages** by falling **first** on the seat not to fall on the ground, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1169}}; more frequently in later writers, of actions which one **manages** to do, **does before** or **has done first** or **already,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1189, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.59, 61, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.111, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.2,93, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 13.2, “Harm.” 2; ἐὰν φθάσω πρὸ τῆς τρύγης ἀνελθεῖν “PSI” 8.971.10 (3rd-4th c.AD)}}; ἐὰν ὁ ἰατρὸς αὐτὸ φθάσῃ κενῶσαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.499}}; φθάνοντος ἤδη πυρέττειν ἐκ τεττάρων ἡμερῶν τοῦ νοσοῦντος **having already begun,** {{Refs|[prev. work]498}}; μὴ φθάνων προσηκόντως τρέφεσθαι if he **is** not **first** suitably nourished, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18(2).36, compare 84,103}}; συμβαίνει φθάνειν ἀποθνῄσκειν τοὺς νεωτέρους the young die **first,** {{Refs|[prev. work]222}}; εἰ φθάσαιμεν παλαιοὺς πίθους ἔχειν μεγάλους if we **already** have.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Geoponica” 6.3.11, compare 10.22.2, +others, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 90.1; ἔφθακεν οὖν ταῦτα ἐψηφίσθαι καὶ τῇ βουλῇ “IG” 12(9).906.26 (Chalcis, 3rd c.AD).}}
**IV** with negatives,
**IV.1** with οὐ and _participle_ (_infinitive_ is see reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1384), followed by καί or καὶ εὐθύς, of two actions following close on each other, οὐ φθάνειν χρὴ συσκιάζοντας γένυν καὶ.. ὁρμᾶν}} you must **no sooner** get your beard than you march, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1219}}; οὐ φθάνει ἐξαγόμενος καὶ εὐθὺς ὅμοιός ἐστι τοῖς ἀκαθάρτοις **no sooner** is he brought out than he becomes unclean, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 5.10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1384}}; οὐκ ἔφθημεν εἰς Τροιζῆν᾽ ἐλθόντες καὶ τοιαύταις νόσοις ἐλήφθημεν ἐξ ὧν.. **no sooner** had we come to Troezen than.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.22, compare 5.53, 8.98, 9.53}}; οὐκ ἔφθη μοι συμβᾶσα ἡ ἀτυχία καὶ εὐθὺς ἐπεχείρησαν διαφορῆσαι τἄνδοθεν **scarcely** or **no sooner** had misfortune befallen me when.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.65, compare 43.69, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.86.}}
**IV.2** οὐκ ἂν φθάνοις, οὐκ ἂν φθάνοιτε, with _participle_ _present_, express a strong exhortation or urgent command, οὐκ ἂν φθάνοιτε τὴν ταχίστην ὀπίσω ἀπαλλασσόμενοι you could not **be too quick** in departing, i.e. **make haste and** be off, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.162}}; οὐ φθάνοιτ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἄν θνῄσκοντες **make haste and** die, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 936, compare 941, Alcaeus 662, “Heracl.” 721, “Tr.” 456 (troch.), “IT” 245; οὐκ ἂν φθάνοιτον τοῦτο πράττοντε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 485; ἀποτρέχων οὐκ ἂν φθάνοις [prev. work] 1133}}; εἰς ἀγορὰν ἰὼν ταχέως οὐκ ἂν φθάνοις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 874, compare “Ec.” 118; οὐκ ἂν φθάνοις λέγων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 185e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.11}}; these phrases are not to be treated as questions, compare οὐκ ἂν φθάνοιμι (i.e. λέγων) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 214e, compare “Phd.” 100c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.40, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Fug.” 26, “Symp.” 2, “Anach.” 14}}: with _participle_ _aorist_, once in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista, “Vit.Auct.” 26.}}
**IV.2.b** in {{Refs|1, 2, or 3 _person_, to express immediate futurity, οὐκ ἂν φθάνοις ἀκούων you}}**shall** hear **in a moment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272d}}; οὐκ ἂν φθάνοι τὸ πλῆθος τούτοις τοῖς θηρίοις δουλεῦον **will soon** (or **inevitably**) be enslaved to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.143}}; also to express what is logically inevitable, οὐκ ἂν φθάνοιεν αὐτοὺς προσκυνοῦντες they **will soon be** (or **cannot logically help**) worshipping them, Aristeas 137; τοῦτο μὲν οὐκ ἂν φθάνοις καὶ Ἐμπεδοκλεῖ πρὸ αὐτοῦ ἐγκαλῶν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Fug.” 2; οὐκ ἂν φθάνοι κἀμὲ μάντιν λέγων [prev. author] “Hes.” 8; οὐκ ἂν φθάνοι τις ἁπάσας ἀναιρῶν τὰς τοιαύτας προστασίας [prev. author] “Apol.” 11}}: with _participle_ _aorist_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tox.” 2.}}

5350:
** φθέγγομαι**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.228, etc.}}: _future_ φθέγξομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.341:}}_aorist_ ἐφθεγξάμην, _Epic dialect_ and _poetry_ φθεγξάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|18.218, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.14:}}_perfect_ ἔφθεγμαι, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἔφθεγξαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 830c}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ **ἔφθεγκται** (_transitive_) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 77a2, (_passive_) [prev. author] “Cael.” 279a23}}: —**utter a sound** or **voice,** especially **speak loud and clear,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, φθεγξάμενος παρὰ νηός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.603, compare 10.67, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 336b}} (properly of all animals that have lungs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 535a30):}}
**I** of the human voice, ἀνθρωπηΐῃ φωνῇ φ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.57; ἀπὸ γλώσσας 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus [same place]; διὰ τοῦ στόματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 238b; [ψυχῆς] φθεγξαμένης ἀΐων 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 7.5; φθεγξαμένου τευ ἢ αὐδήσαντος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.497}}; with a _participle_ expressing the kind of cry, φθέγξομ᾽ ἐγὼν ἰάχουσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.341; τοὶ δ᾽ ἐφθέγγοντο καλεῦντες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.229, compare 12.249; so σφοδρῷ τῷ πνεύματι φ. 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1; φ. μετὰ βοῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 791e, etc.; μέγιστον ἁπάντων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.206; καλὸν καὶ μέγα [prev. work]216, compare 337; ἐλεύθερον καὶ μέγα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 485e}}; also of weak, small voice, φθεγξάμενος ὀλίγῃ ὀπί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.492; τυτθὸν φθεγξαμένη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.170}}; of the battle-cry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.18}}; of the recitative of the chorus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Oec.” 8.3; οὐκέτι πόρρω διθυράμβων φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 238d}}; οὐδ᾽ ἂν φθέγξασθαι δυνηθείη would not be able to **utter a syllable,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.192, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 368c; opposed to silence, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.6}}; εἶτα σὺ φθέγγει..; **open your mouth.. ?** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.283}}; of children just born, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 587a27}}:—Constr.:—with _accusative_ _cognate_, **utter,** ἔπος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.106; ἀγέλαστα 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 92; ὀδυρμοὺς καὶ γόους ἀνωφελεῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 34; καίρια 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 862 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἀράς, λόγους, βλασφημίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 475, “Med.” 1307, “Ion” 1189; ῥῆμα μοχθηρόν “SIG” 1175.19 (Piraeus, Tab. Defix., 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; τἀληθῆ {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 49b; ὑπέρογκα ματαιότητος φ. NT.2Pet.2.18}}: the _person_ addressed added with a _preposition_, φ. εἰς ἡμᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ph.” [same place]; πρός τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 534d}}; later τισί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 27; φ. τι περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.13}}; τὸ φθεγγόμενον, _absolutely_, that **which uttered the sound,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.65.}}
**I.2** of animals, as a horse, **neigh, whinny,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.84, 85}}; of an eagle, **scream,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.23}}; of a raven, **croak,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 16}}; of a fawn, **cry,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 13.62}}; of birds, opposed to ἄφωνοί εἰσι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 618a5; ἐν τῷ θέρει ᾄδει [κόττυφος], τοῦ χειμῶνος.. φ. θορυβῶδες [prev. work] 632b17}}; of worms, φ. οἷον τριγμόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.10.5}}; of certain fish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 300, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.135.}}
**I.3** of inanimate things, of a door, **creak,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1099}}; of thunder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.3}}; of trumpets, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 4.2.7, 5.2.14}}; of the flute, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 6.3, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 532}}; of the lyre, φόρμιγξ φ. ἱρὸν μέλος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 761, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1019b15}}; of an earthen pot, εἴτε ὑγιὲς εἴτε σαθρὸν φ. whether it **rings** sound or cracked, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 179d}}; φ. παλάμῃσι **to clap** with the hands, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 5.106, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 9.505.17 (uncertain).}}
**II** ={ὀνομάζω}, **to name, call by name,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 527a, “Phlb.” 25c, 34a}}; τῷ πλέγματι τούτῳ τὸ ὄνομα ἐφθεγξάμεθα λόγον **gave** it the name of λόγος, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 262d; φ. εἴδωλον ἐπὶ πᾶσιν ὡς ἓν ὄν [prev. work] 240a}}; φ. γιγνόμενα **speak of** things as coming into existence, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 157b}}; καὶ τὸν κύλλαστιν φθέγγου **use the word** κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 257}}; also τῇ δυνάμει ταὐτὸν φ. **have** the same **meaning,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 394c.}}
**III** with _accusative_ _person_, **sing,** or **celebrate** one **aloud,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.36}}; also, **tell of, recount,** θεῶν ἔργα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 12.1.}}

5351:
** φθείρω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **φθέρρω** Hdn.Gr. 2.303, +others; {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} **φθήρω** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).6.17 (Tegea, 4th c.BC): _Ionic dialect_}}_imperfect_ φθείρεσκε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(δια-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36}}: _future_ φθερῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.11, (δια-) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1266, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ φθερέω (δια-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.51}}; _Epic dialect_ φθέρσω (δια-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.625}}: _aorist 1_ ἔφθειρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 244 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.4; poetry ἔφθερσα 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1402; 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus _3rd.pers._ _sing._ _optative_ (?) φθέραι “IG” 5(2).6.8 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}: _perfect_ ἔφθαρκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.64, (δι-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 226; 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} _participle_ ἐφθορκώς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).6.10 (Tegea, 4th c.BC):}} —_middle_, _future_ φθεροῦμαι (in _passive_ sense) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 272, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 708, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.48}}; _Ionic dialect_ φθερέομαι (δια-) [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.108 (variant{δια-φθαρέεται}), 9.42 (variants δια-φθαρέονται, δια-φθορεῦνται)}}]; later φθαροῦμαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.23.5}}:— _passive_, _future_ φθᾰρήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 13, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1066b30, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.7U., (δια-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 802, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ **-ησοῦμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 94d:}}_aorist_ ἐφθάρην [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1502, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 708c}}; _poetry_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔφθαρεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.36}}: also _participle_ κατα-φθερείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 35.13}}: _perfect_ ἔφθαρμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 765}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐφθάραται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.13}}; _infinitive_ ἐφθάρθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1021b27, (δι-) 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.37}}, _Aeolic dialect_ ἔφθορθαι {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 790.8}}: _pluperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐφθάρατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 3.15, (δι-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.90. The _compound_ διαφθείρω}} is much more frequently than the simple Verb:—**destroy** things, μῆλα κακοὶ φθείρουσι νομῆες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.246}}; φ. τῶν Συρίων τοὺς κλήρους **waste** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.76, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.11, “An.” 4.7.20; τοὺς θεῶν νόμους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1344}}; τὰς ναῦς see reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.91; τὴν πόλιν καὶ νόμους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 958c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.3; εὐδαιμονίαν 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator [same place]; ἔμβρυα 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.163; τὸ συλληφθέν 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.60}} (also _absolutely_, **miscarry,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 59)}}; τὸν κοινὸν οἶκον Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Chr.” 284.11 (2nd c.BC)}}: —_passive_, **to be destroyed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 25, etc.; ἐκ τῶν αὐτῶν καὶ διὰ τῶν αὐτῶν καὶ γίνεται πᾶσα ἀρετὴ καὶ φθείρεται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1103b8; εἰς τὸ μὴ ὂν φ.}}**pass away, cease to be,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.5U.; δυὰς προσθέσει μονάδος εἰς τριάδα φθείρεται μηκέτι μένουσα δυάς 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.509}}; of animals, **perish,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PStrassb. 24.15 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** of persons, μαψαῦραι... ναύτας φ. **destroy** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 876}} (but perhaps only _active_ of _signification_{{Refs 4th c.BC+|11.4); στρατόν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 244 (troch.), “Ag.” 652}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 272, 283 (Lyric poetry); γειτόνων πολλοὶ ἁμᾷ.. ἔφθαρεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.36; νόσῳ ἐφθάραται Ἀθηναῖοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.13, compare 7.48; πρόρριζον ἔφθαρται γένος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 765}}; ἔφθαρμαι I **am undone!** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἥρως” 13; μὴφθαρῶσιν “PMich.Zen.” 80.4 (3rd c.BC).}}
**3** **corrupt, bribe,** τινα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.73}}; **lure, entice, trap,** κημοῖσι πλεκτοῖς πορφύρας φθείρει γένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 504 (assuming variant); φθείρει γὰρ ἡ πρόνοια τὴν ἀβουλίαν}}**entices to its ruin, entraps,** {{Refs|Trag.Adesp. 484 (assuming variant)}}; **pervert,** φθείρουσιν ἤθη χρήσθ᾽ ὁμιλίαι κακαί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1024:}} —_passive_, see below {{Refs|11.3.}}
**3.b** **seduce** a woman, ὑπὸ τῆς θυγατρὸς ἀδικούμενον καὶ Διονυσίου τοῦ φθείραντος αὐτὴν κιναίδου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 26.11 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 485, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 11.153}} (but not _Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 53, Moer.p.103 P.), 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 5.17.}}
**4** **ruin, spoil,** ποσὶν φθείροντα πλοῦτον ἀργυρωνήτους θ᾽ ὑφάς, of one who treads on rich carpets, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 949}}; βαφὰς φθείρουσα τοῦ ποικίλματος, of blood, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 1013}}; of a poison, ὧνπερ ἂν θίγῃ, φθείρει τὰ πάντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 716; φαρμάκων φθείρειν πεφυκότων τὰ σώματα 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.541}}; δούλην (wet-nurse) μὴ φθείρουσαν τὸ γάλα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1058.29 (1st c.BC),compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.88; τοῦ σώματος (i.e. τῶν νοσούντων) φθείροντος τὸ θρέψαι δυνάμενον [prev. work] 90, compare 63, +others}}
**5** τὰ μιγνύμενα τῶν χρωμάτων οἱ βαφεῖς φθείρεσθαι καὶ φθοράν τὴν μῖξιν ὀνομάζουσιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.393c (where μιαίνω 1 is compared).}}
**II** _passive_ (compare above 1.1, 2),
**II.1** φθείρεσθε (as a curse) **may you perish! ruin take you!** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.128, 5th c.BC: Sannyrio Comicus 10}}; φθείρου as an imprecation, **go to the devil! be off!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 460, “Pl.” 598, 610 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 610; ἐκποδὼν ἡμῖν φθείρεσθε 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.16:with _genitive_, φθείρεσθε τῆσδε}} **off from** her! unhand her, let her go, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 715 (so in _future_ _indicative_, εἰ μὴ φθερῇ τῆσδ᾽ ὡς τάχιστ᾽ ἀπὸ στέγης}} if thou **dost** not **depart**... {{Refs|[prev. work] 708).}}
**II.1.b** with a _preposition_, φθείρεσθαι πρὸς τοὺς πλουσίους, of hangers-on and flatterers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.139, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 21, “Eum.” 14, “Ant.” 24; εἰς ἡδονὰς ἀπὸ.. πόνων Anonymus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.31.84; ἀκούω σε λυρῳδοῦ γυναικὸς ἐρᾶν καὶ εἰς ἐκείνης φθειρόμενον πᾶσαν τὴν ἐφήμερον ἄγραν κατατίθεσθαι 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.18.}}
**II.2** in Medicine texts, ἡ κοιλίη φθαρήσεται **will be deranged, disordered,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 13.}}
**II.3** **to be morally corrupted,** ἐφθάρη ἡ γῆ ἐναντίον τοῦ θεοῦ {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Gen.6.11, cf. LXX.Hos.9.9, +others; ἔστι ἐν Ἀλεξανδρείᾳ σκηνῶν ἐν τοῖς Ἀριστοβούλου φθειρόμενος “PCair.Zen.” 37.7 (3rd c.BC); φθαρεὶς Εὔτυχος ὑπὸ τῆς Ἀρσινόης [prev. work] 620.7 (3rd c.BC)}}; but ἐν Σικυωνίαι ἐφθαρμένους is _falsa lectio_ for{ἐν Σικυῶνι διεφθ}. (conjecture Sintenis) in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 40.}}
**II.4** of seafarers, **wander, drift** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare(above 1.2, πολύφθορος 11.2, φθορά 8), πόσον χρόνον πόντου 'πὶ νώτοις ἅλιον ἐφθείρου πλάνο; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 774}}; ναυτίλους ἐφθαρμένους sailors **driven out of their course,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “IT” 276; ἱκέτας δέχεσθαι ποντίους ἐφθαρμένους [prev. author] “Cyc.” 300}}; of shipwrecked persons, νεῶν (ἐκ νεῶν Elmsl.) φθαρέντες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 451}}; also of travellers or wanderers by land, οὐχ ἕνα νομίζων φθείρεται πόλεως νόμον (variant{τόπον}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 234; ὁ Μενέλαος χρόνον πολὺν ἐφθείρετο πανταχόσε τῆς Ἐλλάδος 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 7.95}}; οὐδὲν δεῖ φθείρεσθαι περιόντα (={περιιόντα}) τὴν ἀρχὴν ἅπασαν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 26(14).33; ἄνω κάτω διαθέοντας τὴν Ἑλλάδα καὶ φθειρομένους [prev. author] 1.420 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; τῶν μετοίκων τῶν ἐξ Ἑρμιόνης οὐκ οἶδ᾽ ὅπως εἰς Πειραιᾶ φθαρέντων 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.13}}; μὴ περιΐδῃς ἀγαθοὺς γείτονας εἰς στενὸν τοῦ καιροῦ φθειρομένους{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 24; [Ἀλέξανδρον] ὑπὲρ τὸν Ἰνδὸν κτλ. φθειρόμενον 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.2}}; φθαρῆναι εἰς βάρβαρα ἔθνη (ἐν βαρβάροις ἔθνεσι or ἔθεσι codices) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 49}}; φθαρέντων ἐς ἀλλήλους **falling foul** of one another, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Praefatio” 10 (assuming variant).}}
**II.5** of women, χέρσους φθαρῆναι **pine away in** barrenness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1502, compare “El.” 1181}} (unless **wander,** compare above {{Refs|11.4). (Cf. Sanskrit}}**ksárati** 'flow', later 'wane, perish', Avest. γζ?~Xαρ- and ζ?~Xγαρ- 'flow'.)

5353:
** φθόγγ-ος**, ὁ
(both poetical and Prose), **any clear, distinct sound,** especially **voice** of men, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.234, etc.}}; of the Sirens, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.41, 159; φθόγγῳ ἐπερχόμεναι 18.199; φ. ἀραῖον οἴκοις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 237 (Lyric poetry); γόων οὐκ ἀσήμονες φ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1669}}; φ. οἰκείου κακοῦ **voice, telling** of.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 1187; τὸν Αἵμονος φ. [prev. work] 1218, compare 1214}}; of birds, ἀλεκτρυόνων φ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 864; ἀγνῶτα.. φ. ὀρνίθων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1001, compare 424; φθόγγος οὔτ᾽ ὀρνίθων οὔτε θαλάσσης 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 9 (_anapaest meter_); κυνῶν καὶ προβάτων καὶ ὀρνέων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 397a.}}
**2** **speech,** Ἑλλάδος φθόγγον χέουσαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 73}}; φ. ἔμμετρος, opposed to πεζά, poetical **speech,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.13}}; **utterance, saying,** {{Refs|Trag.Adesp. 417.}}
**II** generally, **sound,** ἀνέμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 37.11; δαίμονος πεδαρσίου.. πτερωτὸς φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1198 (= “Tragica Adespota” 47)}}; φωνῆς μὲν οὔ, φθόγγου δὲ μετέχοντά τινος, of semi-vowels, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 18c, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 801b2, 804b9; ἄνευ φθόγγου καὶ ἠχῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 37b, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.32U.; εἰς τοὺς φ. καὶ τὰς συλλαβάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 389d, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 27, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.6.}}
**II.2** of musical **sounds,** λωτὸς φθόγγον κελάδει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 716 (Lyric poetry); λύρας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 812d, etc., compare φθόγγους ἀλύρους θρηνοῦμεν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 162.6 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.2.b** _plural_, **notes** on a musical instrument; **strings** of lyre, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 10.19}}; **stops** of flute, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.21; compare 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 14.}}

5354:
** φθον-έω**,
_aorist_ ἐφθόνησα, later ἐφθόνεσα {{LXX+3rd c.AD+|LXX.Tob.4.7, “JHS” 46.45 (Athens, 3rd-4th c.AD), “Anthologia Graeca” 5.303, 7.607 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus), 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 3.159}}:—_middle_, _future_ in _passive_ sense φθονήσομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.70}}:—_passive_, fut φθονηθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 11.15}}: _aorist_ ἐφθονήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 30, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.33, etc.}}: _perfect_ _participle_ ἐφθονημένος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 6.11.10, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 330.2}}: (φθόνος):—**bear ill-will** or **malice, grudge, be envious** or **jealous,**
**I** _absolutely_, εἴ περ γὰρ φθονέω τε καὶ οὐκ εἰῶ διαπέρσαι, οὐκ ἀνύω φθονέουσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.55, 56}}; κρείττων δόξα τῶν φθονούντων too high for **envy,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.24}}; εἰ πέφυκε φθονεῖν τὸ θεῖον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (φθονερός 1.2) 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 982b32: with _accusative_ et}}_infinitive_, οὔτε τινὰ φθονέω δόμεναι I do not **grudge** that any should give thee, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.16; οὐ φθονῶ σ᾽ ὑπεκφυγεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 553; τὸ μὲν σὸν οὐ φθονῶ καλῶς ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 312; ἐφθόνησαν [οἱ θεοὶ] ἄνδρα ἕνα βασιλεῦσαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.109}}; ἔφη (i.e. ὁ Σωκράτης) φθονεῖν τοῦς ἐπὶ ταῖς φίλων εὐπραξίαις ἀνιωμένους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.9.8; ὁ φθονῶν ἐπὶ κακοῖς τοῖς τῶν πέλας ἡδόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 48b.}}
**I.2** with _dative_ _person_, πτωχὸς πτωχῷ φθονέει καὶ ἀοιδὸς ἀοιδῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 26; οὐ φ. ἀγαθοῖς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.71; φ. φασὶ μητρυιὰς τέκνοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1025; τισὶ φ. καὶ δυσμενῶς ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.241, compare 8.13}}; frequently with _participle_ added, φ. τινὶ εὖ πρήσσοντι **to envy** him for his good fortune, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.236, 237; παιδικοῖς φ. οὐσίαν κεκτημένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 240a, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 27.11}}; without a Noun expressed, καλῶς πράττουσι, πλουτοῦντι φ., [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.26, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.15, etc.: with _dative_ _of things_, φ. τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.10 (variant{ἐπὶ τοῖς ἀγ}.,compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.26; ἐφ᾽ οἷς ἕτεροι ποιήσαντες ἐτιμήθησαν φ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.151): with _genitive_ _of things_, τοῦ εὐτυχέειν φθονέουσι καὶ τὸ κρέσσον στυγέουσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.236}}]; οὐδέ τί σε χρὴ ἀλλοτρίων φθονέειν **to be envious** because of other men's goods {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.18}}: with _dative_ _person_ et _genitive_ _of things_, **bear a grudge** against a person on account of a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1309.}}
**I.3** **resent,** with _genitive_, τῆς δοκήσεως τῶν κερδῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.43}}: with _dative_ _of things_, **feel righteous indignation at,** ταῖς εὐπραγίαις τινῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.124; also with _dative_ _person_, [prev. author] 4.184, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.18.}}
**I.3.b** φ. τινὶ followed by ει.., or ἐάν.. **take it ill** or **amiss** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.146, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.29}}; μή μοι φθονήσητ᾽, ει.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 496: _absolutely_, φ. ἐάν τις.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.9}}; φθονεῖς ἄπαις οὖσ᾽, εἰ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1302}}; also φ. τινὶ ὅτι.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.39}}; φ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.3, uncertain reading in 18.16.}}
**II** **refuse from feelings of envy** or **ill-will, grudge,** with _infinitive_, οὐκ ἂν φθονέοιμι ἀγορεῦσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.381; μὴ φθόνει κιρνάμεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).24; φράσαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 63; σαυτὸν ἐπιδοῦναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 249}}; μὴ φθονήσῃς is frequently in dialogue, **do not refuse** to do a thing, μὴ φ. διδάξαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 338a, compare “Hp.Mi.” 372e, “Smp.” 223a; also μὴ φθόνει μοι ἀποκρίνασθαι [prev. author] “Grg.” 489a}}; μὴ φθονήσῃς alone, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 320c; δῆλον ὅτι οὐ φθονήσει Ἱππίας ἀποκρίνεσθαι [prev. author] “Hp.Mi.” 363c; οὐδενὶ πώποτε ἐφθόνησα [prev. author] “Ap.” 33a}}: with _participle_, μηδέ μοι φθόνει λέγων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 480 (nisi to be read λόγων)}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, τί φθονέεις.. ἀοιδὸν τέρπει; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.346}}: with _dative_ et _infinitive_, τῇ δ᾽ οὐκ ἂν φθονέοιμι.. ἅψασθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|19.348; οὔτοι φθονῶ σοι δαιμόνων τιμᾶν γένος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 236.}}
**II.2** **grudge, refuse to grant** a thing, φθονήσας μήτ᾽ ἀπ᾽ οἰωνῶν φάτιν, μήτ᾽ εἴ τινα.. μαντικῆς ἔχεις ὁδόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 310: with _dative_ _person_ et _genitive_ _of things_, οὔ τοι ἡμιόνων φθονέω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.68; μηδέ μοι φθονήσῃς εὐγμάτων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 583 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 238; μή μοι φθονήσῃς τοῦ μαθήματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 297b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.16; φ. τοῖς ἑαλωκόσι τῆς σωτηρίας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.58.5}}: with _genitive_ _of things_ only, **to be grudging of** a thing, πέπλων, καρποῦ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 333, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 238a}}; μηδ᾽ ὀλίγης φθονέσῃς γαίης {{Refs|“JHS” [prev. cited]}}
**III** _passive_, **to be envied** or **begrudged,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 188, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 30; διὰ σοφίαν φ. ὑπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.33; ἐπ᾽ ἐσθλοῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 814 (Lyric poetry); φθονηθέντα ὑπὸ Μοίρης “JRS” 18.30 (from Phrygia)}}: with _genitive_, **to be grudged** a thing, φ. τοῦ γάμου ὑπὸ δαιμονίου τινός {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.772b.}}

5355:
** φθόνος**, ὁ,
**ill-will** or **malice,** especially **envy** or **jealousy** of the good fortune of others {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 416b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1387b22), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.55, etc.; φθόνῳ}}**through envy,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.30, 9.71}}; opposed to εὔνοια, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 635b}}; opposed to ἔπαινος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.1}}; ἴσχει ὄλβος φθόνον incurs **envy,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.29, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.68; φθόνον πρὸς ἀστῶν ἀλφάνουσι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 297; φθόνῳ χρῆσθαι πρὸς τὰ παιδικά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 253b}}; κρέσσων οἰκτιρμοῦ φθόνος better **to be envied** than pitied ! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.85, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.6; πρὸς γὰρ τὸν ἔχονθ᾽ ὁ φ. ἕρπει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 157 (_anapaest meter_), compare “OT” 382; ἐς τἀπίσημα δ᾽ ὁ φ. πηδᾶν φιλεῖ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 294}}; φ. συνεστιώμενος, of wealth, {{NT+5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 9; φ. [ἐστὶ] τοῖς ζῶσι πρὸς τὸ ἀντίπαλον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.45; κατὰ φθόνον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 686, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 457d; σὺν φθόνῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 780 (Lyric poetry); διὰ φθόνον NT.Phil.1.15}}: with _genitive_ objecti, **envy for, jealousy of,** τῶν πεπραγμένων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.48}}; φθόνον δὲ σωμάτων ἕξει θεός i.e. **will grudge, deny,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 859}}: with _genitive_ _subjunctive_, **envy** or **jealousy felt by** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 282a; also φ. ἐφ᾽ ἑτέροις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.39e, etc.; εἴς τινα “Anthologia Graeca” 6.257 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus); πρός τινα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 22}}: _plural_, **envyings, jealousies, heartburnings,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.163, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 679c, 801e, etc.}}
**b** a **cause for indignation,** a **reproach,** ἀποκτείνειν φθόνος [ἐστὶ] γυναῖκας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 288.}}
**2** especially **jealousy** of the gods {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (φθονερός 1.2), θεῶν φ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 362, “Ag.” 947; φ. μὴ γένοιτό τις θεῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1135: hence _absolutely_, τὸν φ. δὲ πρόσκυσον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 776; εὐλαβούμενος φθόνον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.305.}}
**II** **refusal from feelings of ill-will** or **envy, grudging,** φθόνος μὲν οὐδεὶς.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 628}}; οὐδεὶς φ. or φ. οὐδείς, with _infinitive_, said in granting a request willingly, ἃ τυγχάνω ἀκηκοώς, φ. οὐδεὶς λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61d; οὐδεὶς.. φ. αὐτῷ διελθεῖν αὐτά [prev. author] “Sph.” 217a, compare b. “Lg.” 641d, 664a.}}

5356:
** φθορ-ά**,
_Ionic dialect_ **φθορή**, ἡ, (φθείρω) **destruction, ruin,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.161, 7.18, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.5, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 406 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; of persons, **death,** especially by some general visitation, as pestilence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.47, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 677a (_plural_), “GDI” 5104c11 (Crete, _plural_); ἀνδροθνῆτας Ἰλίου φθοράς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 814.}}
**b** of animals, **loss by death,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PStrassb. 24.26,31 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** Philos., **passing out of existence, ceasing to be,** γενομένῳ παντὶ φ. ἐστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 546a; περὶ γενέσεως καὶ φθορᾶς [prev. author] “Phd.” 95e, title of work by 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 55a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 229b13, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.6}}; ἡ φ. μεταβολή τίς ἐστι τῶν φθειρομένων εἰς τοὐναντίον ἑκάστῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.948f: _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 96b, “R.” 490e, +others: with _dative_ (instrumental), ἡ μεγίστηφθορὰ ὕδασιν [prev. author] “Ti.” 23c, compare 22d.}}
**3** **deterioration,** εἰς καρπογονίαν in respect of.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.8.2.}}
**3.b** **loss by deterioration,** ἐκφορίου.. ἀνυπολόγου πάο ης φθορᾶς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 105.3, 18 (2nd c.BC)}}; **damage,** ἐκτεῖσαι τὴν γεγονυῖαν ὑπ᾽ αὐτῶν τοῦ χόρτου.. φ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1824.29 (1st c.BC); misspelt φθαρά [prev. work] 1866.3 (1st c.BC).}}
**4** **seduction,** ἐλευθέρων Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.12}}; παρθένων, γυναικῶν, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.712c (_plural_), 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 2.37 (_plural_), compare 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 35.3, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.25}}; **rape,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.1.6.}}
**5** **abortion** or **miscarriage,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1365.22, 1366.7, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.56, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).800; τοῦ ἐμβρύου 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.59.}}
**6** **gradation of colours** in painting, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.346a; τὰς μίξεις τῶν χρωμάτων οἱ ζωγράφοι φθορὰς ὀνομάζουσι [prev. work]725c, compare 393c.}}
**7** ={φθόη}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 7.80.}}
**8** **storm-tossings** or **shipwrecks,** τί τοι λέγοιμ᾽ ἂν τὰς ἐν Αἰγαίῳ φθορά; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 766; compare φθείρω 11.4.}}

5357:
** φῐάλη** [ᾰ], ἡ,
**bowl** or **pan** used as a saucepan for boiling liquids, ἀμφίθετος φ. ἀπύρωτος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.270}}; also used as a **cinerary urn,** [ὀστέα] ἐν χρυσεῃ φ. καὶ δίπλακι δημῷ θείομεν {{Refs|[prev. work] 243, compare 253.}}
**2** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **broad, fiat bowl** or **saucer** for drinking or pouring libations, φιάλας τε καὶ ἄλλα ἐκπώματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.80, compare 2.151, 7.54; δωροφοροῦσιν.. φιάλας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 677 (_anapaest meter_); οἰνοδόκον φ. χρυσῷ πεφρικυῖαν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6 (5). 40; ἀργυρωθέντες σὺν οἰνηραῖς φ. [prev. author] “N.” 10.43}}; of gold, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.151, 7.54, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 120a (_plural_), “PCair.Zen.” 21.16 (3rd c.BC, assuming variant), NT.Rev.5.8, etc.; ἔλαβε σύμβολον παρὰ βασιλέως τοῦ μεγάλου φ. χρυσῆν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.25}}; of silver, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.51, “IG” 12.313.15, +others, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.11, “PCair.Zen.” 327.5, +others (3rd c.BC)}}; ἀργυρηλάτους χρυσέας τε φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1182; φ. λυκιουργεῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.31}}; as a votive offering, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50, “PTeb.” 6.27 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; πίνειν ἐκ φ. μεγάλης ἐπὶ δεξιά [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 223c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.23; φ. καρυωτή, see at {καρυωτός} 11.}}]
**2.b** used for unguents, ἄλλος δ᾽ εὐῶδες μύρον ἐν φ. παρατείνει {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 1.3}}; for administering medicines, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).122.125 (Epid., 4th c.BC).}}
**2.c** τὸ ἐκ φιάλης revenue, perhaps from a **collecting-bowl,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 11(2).161 A 116 (Delos, 3rd c.BC),compare “Inscription Delos” 442 A 156 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II** from its broad flat shape, φιάλη Ἄρεως _metaphorically_ for ἀσπίς, **shield,** cited from {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Timaeus Grammaticus (“fragment” 22) by 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 112, compare 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 80, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1412b35.}}
**III** **ornament used in a coffered ceiling,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 102}}.—The form **φιέλη** was less _Attic dialect_, {{Refs|Moer. p.389 P.}}

5361:
** φῐλᾰδελφ-ος**, ον,
**loving one's brother** or **sister, brotherly, sisterly,** φ. δάκρυα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 527 (_anapaest meter_)}}; of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.17, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 27}}: _superlative_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Luc.” 43}}; frequently as a title of kings, as of Ptolemy Il and Arsinoe, {{Refs|Wilcken “Chr.” 106, etc.}}; of Ptolemy XIII, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 185, etc., and of Attalus Il; [prev. work]329.38 (Aegina, 2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; τῆς Φ. Κύπριδος, of Arsinoe, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.318d}}; of Antoninus and Verus, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 22.3405, +others; τὸ φ. τῆς ψυχῆς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.34.}} _adverb_ -φως Scholia S. [prev. cited]
**2** in **NT, loving the brethren,** {{NT|NT.1Pet.3.8.}}
**II** φιλάδελφον, τό, **mock orange, Philadelphus coronarius,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 15.682c.}}
**III** φιλάδελφοι, οἱ, name of fabulous **stones,** Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fluv.” 11.4.}}

5362:
** φίλ-ανδρος**, ον,
**loving men;** of a country, **loving its men,** πέδον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 902 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **loving masculine habits,** of Atalanta, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1111; Ἀμαζόνες 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 26.}}
**II.2** of women, **lewd,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 191e.}}
**II.3** **loving one's husband,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Tit.2.4, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.431, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 8, Iamb.post Polem. p.48 Hinck, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Halc.” 8; frequently in epitaphs, “epigram Gr.” 387.12 (from Apamea), “IG” 14.1976; ψυχῇ φιλανδροτάτῃ “IG” 14. 607f (from Caralis).}} _adverb_ -ρως, ἔζησε{{Refs|[prev. work]12(3).280 (from Anaphe).}}

5363:
** φῐλανθρωπ-ία**, ἡ,
**humanity, benevolence, kind-heartedness, humane feeling,** or, in a weaker sense, **kindliness, courtesy,**
**I** of men, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 6, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.1, NT.Act.28.2, etc.}}; opposed to σεμνότης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.133}}; opposed to φθόνος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.165; opposed to ὠμότης, [prev. work]109; joined with εὔνοια, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.114, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.5}}; with πραότης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.116}}; with χρηστότης, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.5.76; φ. λόγων}}**courtesy,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.298; τῆς παρὰ τουτωνὶ τιμῆς καὶ φ. [prev. work]209; φ. διὰ τῶν λόγων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.17.11; φ. προσάγειν τινί [prev. author] 1.81.8; φ. εἰς or πρὸς τοὺς αἰχμαλώτους, [prev. work] 79.8, 11}}; ὑπὸ φιλανθρωπίας Pl.[same place]; μετὰ φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15}} [same place]; **clemency,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.73; so φιλανθρωπίᾳ [prev. author] “Ages.” 1.22}}; **the intercourse** of lovers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.171}}: _plural_, **acts of kindness, courtesies,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.70, 25.86, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 36.17.13, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.160 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, etc.}}
**I.b** ἡ σὴ φ. as a form of address, your **Clemency,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PRyl. 296 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**I.2** of God, _love to man, _{{NT|NT.Tit.3.4, +others}}
**II** of things, ἡ τοῦ ὀνόματος (i. e. νόμος) φ. its **mildness,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.156}}; ἡ φ. τῆς τέχνης, of agriculture, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 15.4, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.15}}; χώρα πάσης φ. ἐστερημένη, of a desert country, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.50}}; in disease, **mild symptoms,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.219.}}
**III** **concession, privilege**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 162 vii 21 (2nd c.BC), “OGI” 139.20 (Egypt, 2nd c.BC), “Rev.Phil.” 10 (1936).253 (from Ilium)}}; θεία φ. imperial **grant**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“SIG” 888.102 (Scaptopara, 3rd c.AD).}}

5364:
Included with: ** φῐλάνθρωπ-ος**, ον,
**loving mankind, humane, benevolent, tender-hearted,** and, in weaker sense, **kind, courteous,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [274]; φ. καὶ φιλαθήναιος καὶ φιλόσοφος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 5.2; φ. καὶ φιλόπολις [prev. author] 2.15; δημοτικὸς καὶ φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.60; ψυχὴν φιλανθρωπότατος [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.2.1}}; φ. δὲ παύεσθαι τρόπου, of Prometheus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 11, compare 28}}; of animals that attach themselves to men, as of dogs, **gentle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.25}}; of horses, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 2.3}}; τὸ φιλάνθρωπον, ={φιλανθρωπία}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 3, etc.; τὸ φ. καὶ μεταδοτικόν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.54J.}}; τὰ φιλάνθρωπα **humane treatment,** τῶν φ. τυχεῖν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 638.13 (3rd c.BC)}}; **kindnesses,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.38.3, 12.5.3, etc.}}
**2** of the gods, **loving men,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 189c (_superlative_), “Lg.” 713d, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 4.}}
**II** of things, **humane, humanizing,** χάρις δικαία καὶ φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 953.41; γεωργία 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.17; ψηφίσματα [prev. author] “Vect.” 3.6; λόγοι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.4}}; τρόπος, in Music, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1135c, etc.}}; of wines, **generous,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cleom.” 13 (_comparative_), compare 2.680b.}}
**II.2** **appealing to human feeling,** of situations, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1452b38, +others}} (less probably, **satisfying the sense of poetic justice**).
**II.3** in Medicine texts, of diet, **generous,** τροφὴ -οτέρα, opposed to ὀλίγη, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.211}}; but of a medical treatise, **popular,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 15.551 (_comparative_).}}
**III** φιλάνθρωπα, τά, **concessions, grants, privileges, immunities**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 221.14 (Ilium, 3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 37.11 (3rd c.BC), “SIG” 548.3 (Delph., 3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.26.8, “UPZ” 162v 22, +others (2nd c.BC), “OGI” 331.42 (Pergam., 2nd c.BC)}}, Epist.Jul.Caes. cited in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 14.10.2, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 32.4, etc.}}; Thess. **φιλάνθρουπα** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).517.16 (Larissa, 3rd c.BC).}}
**III.b** _singular_, **benefaction, endowment, gratuity**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1202.10 (1st c.BC), “Mon.Anc.Gr.” 9.10, etc.}}
**III.c** _singular_, **letter expressing friendly feelings**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.439.32, “PCair.Zen.” 56.10 (both 3rd c.BC).}}
**IV** _adverb_, -ως τινὶ κεχρῆσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.225; φ. διακεῖσθαι πρός τινα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.68.13; φ. καὶ δημοτικῶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.24; θεοφιλῶς καὶ φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.43, compare 15.132, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.14, etc.}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.191, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 69.2.}}
**V** **φῐλάνθρωπ-ον**, τό, ={ἀπαρίνη}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.90, 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 24.176}}; **φιλανθρώπειος** βοτάνη in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.574.}}

5368:
** φῐλέω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **φίλημμι** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 79, cf. Ead. “Oxy.” 1787 “fragment” 1 + 2.24}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ φίλησθα Ead.22; late _3rd.pers._ _plural_ φίλεισι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“epigram Gr.” 990.12 (2nd c.AD: Balbilla Lyrica)}}: _Boeotian dialect_ **φίλειμι** Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|2.930: _Epic dialect_}}_infinitive_ φιλήμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.265}}: _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ φιλέεσκε {{Refs|3.388, +others:}}_future_ φιλήσω, _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ φιλησέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.171}}: _aorist 1_ ἐφίλησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.16}}, etc.: _perfect_ πεφίληκα{{Refs|[prev. work] 1.13}}:—_middle_, poetical {{Refs|1}} _aorist_ ἐφῑλάμη; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐφίλατο, φίλατο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.61, 20.304, Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.Oxy.” 2080.55}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ φίλαντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 274; _imperative_ φῖλαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.117, 10.280}}; _subjunctive_ φίλωνται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 117, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 97}}; but φίλατο as _passive_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.66}}; also _participle_ φιλάμενος {{Refs|“IG” 14.1549 (from Rome):}} —_passive_, _future_ _middle_ φιλήσομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.123, 15.281, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.19}}: _future_ 3 πεφιλήσομαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 270}}: _aorist_ ἐφιλήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1000, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 253c}}: _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐφίληθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.668}}: _perfect_ πεφίλημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.28}}; _Doric dialect_ _participle_ πεφιλᾱμένος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.3}}. [ῐ exceptin the forms ἐφίλατο, φῑλατο, etc.]: (φίλος):— **love, regard with affection,** opposed to μισεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 334c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1380b34; φιλήσω τὸν δῆμον τὸν Ἀθηναίων “IG” 12.15.36}}; (on its relation to **sexual love** see below 3); of the love of gods for men, φ. δέ ἑ μητίετα Ζεύς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.197}}; πέρι γάρ μ᾽ ἐφίλει (of the love of the master for his swineherd) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.146; (also ὃν περὶ κῆρι φ. Ζεὺς.. παντοίην φιλότητα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.245, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.117); μάλα τούς γε φ. ἑκάεργος Ἀπόλλων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.94; εἰ.. Ἕκτορά περ φιλέεις καὶ κήδεαι αὐτοῦ 7.204, etc.}}; of love for a child reared, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.370; αἰ δὲ μὴ φίλει, ταχέως φιλήσει κωὐκὶ θέλοισα 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.23; λόγοις φιλοῦσαν οὐ στέργω φίλην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 543; φιλέων φιλέοντα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.66; ὃν δ᾽ ἐχρῆν φιλεῖν στυγεῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 907; μάλιστά σ᾽.. ἤχθηρα κἀφίλησ᾽ ἐν ἡμέρᾳ μιᾷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1363; ὃν οἱ θεοὶ φιλοῦσιν ἀποθνῄσκει νέος 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 125}}; ὅσα θεοὶ ἀνθρώποις οὓς φιλοῦσιν [διδόασιν] {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“SIG” 985.48 (Philadelphia, 1st c.BC)}}; οἱ φιλοῦντές τινα his **friends,** frequently in messages and letters, {{NT+1st c.BC+|“OGI” 184.10 (Philae, 1st c.BC), NT.Tit.3.15, “PSI” 8.971.30 (3rd-4th c.AD)}}, etc.; φιλεῖν ἐμαυτήν, αὑτόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 999, “Med.” 86, etc.}}:—_passive_, **to be beloved** by one, ἐκ Διός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.668; παρ᾽ αὐτῇ 13.627, etc.}}; τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1000.}}
**2** **treat affectionately** or **kindly,** especially **welcome, entertain** a guest, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.29, 5.135, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.207, etc.; φίλος δ᾽ ἦν ἀνθρώποισιν, πάντας γὰρ φιλέεσκεν ὁδῷ ἔπι οἰκία ναίων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.15; ξεῖνον ἐνὶ μεγάροισι φ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.42; ξεῖνον ἄγων ἐν δώμασι.. φιλέειν καὶ τιέμεν 15.543, compare 14.322}}; θεὸς (i. e. Calypso) ἥ με.. ἐφίλει τε καὶ ἔτρεφεν {{Refs|7.256}}; τίς ἂν φιλέοντι μάχοιτ; who would quarrel with **a kind host?** {{Refs|8.208}}; etc.:—_passive_, παρ᾽ ἄμμι φιλήσεαι **welcome shalt thou be** in our house, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.123, compare 15.281.}}
**3** opposed to ἐρᾶν, τούτους μάλιστά φασι φιλεῖν ὧν ἂν ἐρῶσι **regard with affection** those for whom they have a passion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 231c; ὥστε οὐ μόνον φιλοῖο ἄν, ἀλλὰ καὶ ἐρῷο ὑπ᾽ ἀνθρώπων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 11.11, compare “Smp.” 8.21}}; εἰκὸς τὸ φιλεῖν τοὺς ἐρωμένους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 70a6}}; but φ. is used of lovers, ἥ γ᾽ Εὐρυμάχῳ μισγέσκετο καὶ φιλέεσκεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.325; Λυσίθεος Μικίωνα φιλῖν φησι μάλισστα τῶν ἐν τῇ πόλει “IG” 12.924; οὐκ ἔστ᾽ ἐραστὴς ὅστις οὐκ ἀεὶ φιλεῖ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1051, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.176 (_passive_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 905}}; of the **love** of man for wife, ὅς τις ἀνὴρ ἀγαθὸς.. τὴν αὐτοῦ φιλέει (**cherishes** her) καὶ κήδεται ὡς καὶ ἐγὼ τὴν ἐκ θυμοῦ φίλεον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.343, compare 486}}; τὴν αὐτὸς φιλέεσκεν **loved and cherished** as his wife, {{Refs|[prev. work] 450}}; but ἐμὲ.. ἀτιμάζει, φιλέει δ᾽ ἀΐδηλον Ἄρηα (Hephaestus speaks of Aphrodite) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.309: Comedy texts, ὦ Δῆμ᾽, ἐραστής εἰμι σὸς φιλῶ τέ σε καὶ κήδομαί σου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1341.}}
**3.b** of sexual intercourse, [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {βαίνειν}.}}]
**4** **show outward signs of love,** especially **kiss** (not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus), φ. τοῖσι στόμασι}} **kiss on** the mouth, opposed to τὰς παρειὰς φιλέονται, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.134, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.27, “Smp.” 9.5; κατὰ τὸ στόμα “Anthologia Graeca” 5.284 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus); φιλήσω.. τὸ σὸν κάρα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1131; πατέρα.. περὶ χεῖρε βαλοῦσα φιλήσει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1559 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 671, 674, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 255e, NT.Mark.14.44, etc.: with double _accusative_, τὸ φίλαμα, τὸ.. τὸν Ἄδωνιν.. ἀποθνάσκοντα φίλασεν}} the kiss wherewith **she kissed** him, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.69:}} —_middle_, τὰς παρειάς **kiss each other's** cheeks, Hdt. [prev. cited]
**5** of things as objects of love, **like, approve,** σχέτλια ἔργα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.83; ἀοιδάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.7; οὔθ᾽ ἱστῶν ἐφίλησεν ὁδοὺς οὔτε δείπνων.. τέρψιας “P.” 9.18, etc.; αἰσχροκέρδειαν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1056, compare 312}}; τὰς λευκοτάτας [μάζας] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 1.6 (_anapaest meter_); Πράμνιον οἶνον 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 28.}}
**6** of things as the subject, ἡσυχία δὲ φιλεῖ συμπόσιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.48; ἢ [μίτρη] μαστοὺς ἐφίλησε 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 39.}}
**7** in making a request, οἶσθ᾽ ὁτιὴ φιλῶ σ᾽ ἐγώ, κἀμοὶ πιθόμενος ὑπαποκίνει τῆς ὁδοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1010}}; so τί πράσσει Φηλικίων ὁ ἀγαθό; φιλῶ σε **pray,** how goes it with the worthy Felicio? {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.19.20; so perhaps in 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.66, πείσθητί μευ, φιλέω σε}} (but rather 'I **speak as a true friend'**).
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, with _infinitive_, **love** to do, **be fond** of doing, and so **to be wont** or **used to** do, φιλέει ὁ θεὸς τὰ ὑπερέχοντα κολούειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10. ; ἢν ἁμάρτωσι τοῦ πατρικοῦ τύπου.. φιλέουσι διαφθείρεσθαι 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 228; Μοῖσα μεμνᾶσθαι φ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.12, compare “P.” 3.18; φιλεῖ δὲ τίκτειν ὕβρις.. ὕβριν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 763 (Lyric poetry); τοῖς θανοῦσί τοι φιλοῦσι πάντες κειμένοις ἐπεγγελᾶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 989, etc.}}; rarely with _participle_ for _infinitive_, φιλεῖς δὲ δρῶσ᾽ αὐτὸ σφόδρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 645.}}
**II.2** of things, events, etc., αὔρη ἀπὸ ψυχροῦ τινος φιλέει πνέειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.27; φιλεῖ ὠδῖνα τίκτειν νύξ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 769; ἐμπόρων ἔπη φ. πλανᾶσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 304; φιλεῖ γάρ πως τὰ τοιαῦθ᾽ ἑτέρᾳ τρέπεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 813 (Lyric poetry); φιλεῖ μεγάλα στρατόπεδα ἐκπλήγνυσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.125; ὃ δὴ φ. ὁ ἔρως ἐμποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 182c: especially with γίγνεσθαι}} of what **usually** happens, ἀπὸ πείρης πάντα ἀνθρώποισι φιλέει γίνεσθαι everything comes to man by experience, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9.γ, compare 7.10.ζ, 7.50, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.104, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 494c, +others; οἷα φ. γίγνεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.79, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.128}}; without γίγνεσθαι, οἷα δὴ φιλεῖ as **is wont,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 467b; ὁποῖα φ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 9.}}
**II.3** _impersonal_, φιλέει δέ κως προσημαίνειν (i.e. ὁ θεός), εὖτ᾽ ἂν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.27}}; ὡς δὴ φιλεῖ.. λόγον ἔχειν ἀνθρώπους as **it is usual** for.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 73.}}

5369:
** φῐλήδ-ονος**, ον,
(ἡδονή) **fond of pleasure,** [{{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 39.1.10, NT.2Tim.3.4, 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Gnomologium” 46, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 1, +others, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 16, 2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 4.2, etc.: τὸ φ., ={φιληδονία}, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1094a.}}]
**2** **wont to bring delight,** Βάκχοιο νᾶμα {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 10.118.}}

5370:
** φίλημ-α** [ῐ], ατος, τό,
_Doric dialect_ **φίλᾱμα** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 1.5}}:— **kiss,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 135, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.12, etc.; φ. δοῦναι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 679, 1238; φ. παρὰ γένυν τιθέντα σόν [prev. author] “Supp.” 1154; πατρὶ.. διὰ φιλημάτων ἰών [prev. author] “Andr.” 416}}; of kisses as prizes in a game, παίζωμεν περὶ φιλημάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 46, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 537, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 3.4}}; as a symbol of Christian love, {{NT|NT.1Cor.16.20.}}
**II** _plural_, **cosmetics,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 2.38 (perhaps φαρμάκων).}}

5372:
** φῐλ-ητός**, ή, όν,
**to be loved, worthy of love,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author = 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus] “EN” 1168a15}}; τὰ φ. **objects of love,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 1156a8.}}
**II** _adverb_ **-τῶς** **in a friendly spirit,** {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1490.47.}}

5373:
** φῐλί-α**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, (φιλέω) **affectionate regard, friendship** (not in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus or 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus), usually between equals, ἄνδρεσσι κακοῖς συνθέμενοι φ. 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 306, “IG” 12.1037; ἐπαγγέλλεσθαι φ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.130; εἰς ἀλλήλους φιλίας ἀνακίρνασθαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 254 (_anapaest meter_), compare 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 109, +others, Hippias 17, 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 64 (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 179c, etc.}}; φιλίας, _inscription_ on a bowl (perhaps **loving**-cup), {{Refs|“BSA” 32.194 (Haliartus, Hellenistic)}}, etc.; ἡ τῆς ψυχῆς φ. διὰ τὸ ἁγνὴ εἶναι, opposed to ἔρως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.15, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 255e}}; opposed to ἔχθρα, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.33, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.2.2; ἡ φ. τοῦ κόσμου ἔχθρα τοῦ θεοῦ ἐστιν NT.Jam.4.4}}; opposed to μῖσος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.122; φ. θεῶν καὶ ἀνθρώπων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 188d}}; of family **affection,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 3.7 (_plural_)}}; ἐν ταῖς φ. in the **family circle,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1453b19}}; of the **regard** of dependents towards their superiors, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.3; φ. ἡ πρὸς τὸν δῆμον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.28}}; of **friendship** between States, ἐχρημάτισε περὶ φιλίας τοῖς Ἀθηναίοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.5; φ. καὶ ξυμμαχία [prev. author] 6.34}}; τῆς φ. ἀφέσθαι, τὴν φ. διαλύσασθαι, of communities, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.11, 14.33: various εἴδη}} distinguished by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1156a7}} ff.; by the Stoics, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.24, 27, 181: phrases, φ. πρός τινας ποιήσασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.29; παρά τινων φ. λαβεῖν [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.1.28; τισὶ διὰ φιλίας ἰέναι [prev. author] “An.” 3.2.8}}; εἰς φ. ἰτέον, ἔρχεται, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 237c, “Ly.” 214d; ἔστιν ἡμῖν ἐν φ. “PMich.Zen.” 33.3 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἀνανεούμενος τὴν φ. καὶ ζενίαν τὴν πρότερον ὑπάρχουσαν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 7.13; προλιπόνθ᾽ ἡμετέρην φ. 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1102; ἔλιπε φ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 930 (Lyric poetry); τῆς φ. ἐξίστασθαί τινι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 8.18: with Preps., διὰ φιλίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 304e; μετὰ φιλίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.10}}; διὰ φιλίαν, see below; κατὰ φιλίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 823b:—the person is commonly expressed by πρός τινα, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.32; πρὸς ἀλλήλους [prev. author] 9.57, etc.; less frequently εἰς ἀλλήλους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hipp.” (see. above)}}; also by object. _genitive_, διὰ φιλίαν αὐτοῦ through **friendship for** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.91}}; φ. ξυνετοῦ **friendship with** a wise man, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 98}}; so ἡμετέρη φ. **friendship with** us, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 600, 1102 (see. above), 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.11 (see. above); φιλία ἡ σή 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.29, E “Or.” 138, etc.: _plural_, φ. ἰσχυραί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.82, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 182c.}}
**2** **friendliness, amiability,** φ. ἄνευ τοῦ στέργειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1126b22, compare 1108a28.}}
**3** later, of lovers, **fondness,** {{LXX+6th c.AD+|LXX.Pro.5.19, “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 1.8, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.266 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}
**4** with _genitive_, of things, **fondness, liking for,** κέρδους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 581a}}; [τῶν ἀρχῶν] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 306a12.}}
**5** **the natural force which unites discordant elements and movements,** opposed to νεῖκος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 18, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.268.}}
**II** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **three,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 16}} (not for **six,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “In Nic.” p.34 P.).}}

5376:
** φῐλιππ-ος**, ον,
**fond of horses, horse-loving,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.32, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Frr.” 582,859 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 212d, 1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 4.14, etc.}}; φ. ἄστυ Θήβας _restored_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Pae.” 1.7, etc.}}:— _superlative_ φιλιππότατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.5.}}
**II** as _masculine_ _proper name_, {{Refs|“Philip.”}}

5378:
** φῐλόλογ-ος**, ον,
**fond of words, talkative,** οἶνος φιλολόγους ποιεῖ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 284}}; φ. καὶ πολύλογος, opposed to βραχύλογος, of Athens, opposed to Sparta, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 641e}}; **fond of speaking,** of Socrates, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 236e.}}
**II** **fond of dialectic, fond of philosophical argument,** opposed to μισόλογος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “La.” 188c; φ. γ᾽ εἶ καὶ χρηστός [prev. author] “Tht.” 161a; ὁ φιλόσοφός τε καὶ ὁ φ. [prev. author] “R.” 582e, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 74, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.48 O.}}
**II.2** **fond of learning and literature, literary,** Λακεδαιμόνιοι.. ἥκιστα φ. ὄντες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1398b14; φύσει Ἀθηναῖοι φ. 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.3.7: opposed to λογόφιλος(lover of reason), 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.67; φιλολόγῳ ὑποκατακλίνεσθαι φιλομαθῆ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.618e, compare 419d}}; opposed to ἀπαίδευτος, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.22.107: opposed to πολιτικός, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 42.}}
**II.3** **student, scholar,** first used by Eratosthenes of himself, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Suetonius Grammaticus et Historicus Latinus “Grammars” 10, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.5.15, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 25, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.4.1, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “Libr.Propr.Prooem.”: but φιλόλογος ὁ φιλῶν λόγους καὶ σπουδάζων περὶ παιδείαν· οἱ δὲ νῦν ἐπὶ τοῦ ἐμπείρου τιθέασιν, οὐκ ὀρθῶς 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 371.}}
**II.4** of books, **learned,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 13.12.3 (_comparative_)}}: **suitable for a literary man, connected with learning,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 15.15.2.}} _adverb_ **-λόγως** **learnedly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.11, argument 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ra.”}}
**II.5** φ. **multa,** much **learned conversation,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 13.52.2.}}
**III** **studious of words,** opposed to φιλόσοφος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Vita Plotini” 14, etap. 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Timaeum commentarii” 1.86 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc. (Freq. written _paroxytone_ φιλολόγος}} in codices, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161a (codex B), “Etymologicum Magnum” 406.10: but φιλόλογος Hdn.Gr. 1.233 (from λόγος, not λέγω).)}}

5379:
** φῐλονῑκ-ία**, ἡ,
**love of victory, rivalry, contentiousness,** mostly in bad sense, φ. ἕνεκα τῆς αὐτίκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.41, compare 3.82; φ. ἀνόητος 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 237; φ. ἢ φιλοτιμίας ἕνεκα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 860d, cf. Alcaeus1.122c; ἐκ μέθης καὶ φιλονικίας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.43; διὰ στάσιν καὶ τὴν πρὸς ἀλλήλους φ. [prev. author] 33.4; εἰς πόλεμον καταστᾶσαι πρὸς ἀλλήλας καὶ φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.158; ἡ πρὸς ἀλλήλους ἔρις καὶ φ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.14, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 88a; ἀλλά τίς με φ. εἴληφεν πρὸς τὰ εἰρημένα [prev. author] “La.” 194a; ὑπὸ τῆς πρὸς τἀμὰ ἔργα φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.12; οὐ φιλονικίᾳ γε ἐρωτῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 515b; ἐάν τις φιλονικίᾳ κριθῇ.. δρᾶν, τεθνάτω [prev. author] “Lg.” 938c}}; εἰς τοσοῦτον φιλονικίας ἐλθεῖν πρὸς τὴν πόλιν, ὥστε.. {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mx.” 243b; ἐγένετο φ. ἐν αὐτοῖς τὸ τίς αὐτῶν δοκεῖ εἶναι μείζων NT.Luke.22.24: _plural_, φ. καὶ φιλοτιμίαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 548c, compare “Ti.” 90b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.246; περὶ ὁπόσων ἂν ἐγγένωνται ἀνθρώποις φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.22; αἱ περὶ τὰς χορηγίας φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.53; φ. καὶ στάσεις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1308a31.}}
**2** in good sense, **competition, emulation, emulous eagerness,** ἔστω τούτων.. κατὰ νόμον ἅμιλλά τε καὶ φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 834c; φ. αὐτοῖς ἔμβαλλε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.18}}; φιλονικίας ἐμποιεῖν περὶ τῶν καλῶν ἔργων{{Refs|[prev. work] 8.2.26; φ. ἐνέβαλε πρὸς ἀλλήλους τισί [prev. author] “Ages.” 2.8}}; especially in the games, πολλὴ φ. ἐγίγνετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.27, compare “Lac.” 4.2; διὰ φιλονικίαν [prev. author] “Hier.” 9.6; τῶν ἐργατῶν φ. πρὸς ἀλλήλους καὶ φιλοτιμία [prev. author] “Oec.” 21.10, compare “SIG” 685.12,36 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 2nd c.BC, found in Crete).}} —On the form φιλονεικία, see at {φιλόνικος} near the end

5380:
** φῐλόνῑκ-ος**, ον,
**fond of victory, contentious.**
**1** in bad sense, οὔτε δύσηρις ἐὼν οὔτ᾽ ὦν φ. ἄγαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.19 (-νεικ- codices vett.)}}; φ. ἐστι πρὸς ὃ ἂν ὁρμήσῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 336e}}; coupled with φιλότιμος, [{{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 545a, 582e (variant{-νεικ-}), compare 550b}}]; ἐπίπονον καὶ φ. καὶ φιλότιμον.. καταστήσας τὸν βίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.16.}}
**2** in good sense, of spirited horses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.8 (_superlative_)}}: of persons, φ. πρὸς τὸ μὴ ἐλλείπεσθαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 2.6.5, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 2 (_superlative_)}}; τὸ φ., ={φιλονικία, ἔσῳζον τὸ φ. ἐν ταῖς ψυχαῖς} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.64.}} _adverb_ -κως **in eager rivalry,** παραθεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyn.” 6.16; φ. ἔχειν πρὸς ἀλλήλους [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.3.57, 8.4.4; φ. ἔχειν πρὸς τὸ εἰδέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 505e}}; opposed to ἀνθρωπίνως, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.41. (In codices the forms φιλόνικος, -νικέω, -νικία and φιλόνεικος, -νεικέω, -νεικία}} occur, without any distinction of meaning, e.g. in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator we find περὶ τῶν καλλίστων ἐφιλονίκησαν 4.85, but τὰς θεὰς περὶ τοῦ κάλλους φιλονεικούσας 10.48; μὴ δύσερις ὢν.., μηδὲ πρὸς πάντας φιλόνικος 1.31; τῆς πρὸς ἡμᾶς φιλονικίας 4.19, but φιλονεικία in the same sense, 12.158}}; φιλόνῑκος is implied by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1389a12 (where -νεικ-, though found in good codices, as also in 1363b1, 1368b21, 1370b33, “Phgn.” 809b35, must be _falsa lectio_), καὶ φιλότιμοι μέν εἰσι [οἱ νέοι], μᾶλλον δὲ φιλόνικοι· ὑπεροχῆς γὰρ ἐπιθυμεῖ ἡ νεότης· ἡ δὲ νίκη ὑπεροχή τις,compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.178, “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 315}}; the _compound_ of φιλο- and νεῖκος would be Φιλονεική; the sense
**A** **contentious** arises naturally from **fond of victory;** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“SIG” 685 (see. φιλονικία entry near the end) we have φιλονικίαν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12, 36, and φιλονικίᾳ in “OGI” 335.7 (Pergam., decree of Pitane, 2nd c.BC)}}; -νῑκ- is also found in late documents, as {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“POxy.” 157.1 (6th c.AD).)}}

5384:
** φίλος**, η, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.93: [ῐ: but 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses the _vocative_ φίλε}} with ῑ at the beginning of a verse, see below].
**I** _passive_, **beloved, dear,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.20, etc.; παῖδε φίλω 7.279}}; frequently with _dative_, **dear** to one, μάλα οἱ φ. ἦεν {{Refs|1.381; φ. ἀθανάτοισι θεοῖσι 20.347, etc.: _vocative_, φίλε κασίγνητε (at the beginning of the line) 4.155, 5.359}}; with _neuter_ nouns, φίλε τέκνον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.363, 3.184, etc.; but φίλον τέκος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.162}}; also φίλος for φίλε (_Attic dialect_, _accusative_ to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 213.28), φίλος ὦ Μενέλαε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.189, compare 9.601, 21.106, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.76, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 545 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 277 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1168 (Lyric poetry)}}: _genitive_ added to the _vocative_, φίλ᾽ ἀνδρῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.74, 24.40; ὦ φίλα γυναικῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 460 (Lyric poetry): as}} _substantive_:
**I.a** φίλος, ὁ, **friend,** κουρίδιος φίλος, i.e. husband, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.22}}; φίλοι **friends, kith and kin,** νόσφιφίλων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.256; τῆλεφίλων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.333, compare 6.287}}; φ. μέγιστος my greatest **friend,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1331}}; φίλοι οἱ ἐγγυτάτω, οἱ ἔγγιστα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.41 codices, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.24.2; after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} frequently with a _genitive_, ὁ Διὸς φίλος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 306}}; τοὺς ἐμαυτοῦ φ., τοὺς τούτων φ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.47; φ. ἐμός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 421}}; τῶν ἐμε̄ν φ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 509; τοὺς σφετέρους φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.25: proverbial, ἔστιν ὁ φ. ἄλλος αὐτός}} **a friend** is another self, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1166a31; κοινὰ τὰ τῶν φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 279c, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1159b31; οὐθεὶς φ. ᾧ πολλοὶ φ. [prev. author] “EE” 1245b20}}; also of **friends** or **allies,** opposed to πολέμιοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.48; φ. καὶ σύμμαχος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.12, etc.}}; of **a lover,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.4 (in bad sense, “Lac.” 2.13)}}; φίλε **my friend,** as a form of courteous address, {{NT|NT.Luke.14.10, etc.}}; in relation to things, οἱ μουσικῆς φ. [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 580.3; ἀληθείας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 487a; τῶν εἰδῶν [prev. author] “Sph.” 248a; Χίους φ. ποιῆσαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.36, etc.; ποιεῖσθαι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 38; κτᾶσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.27, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40; φίλους τιθέντες τούς γε πολεμιωτάτους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 848; φίλῳ χρῆσθαί τινι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.63; ἡμᾶς ἔχειν φίλους 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.40; for 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.49, see at {φίλιος}.}}]
**I.b** φίλη, ἡ, **dear one, friend,** κλῦτε, φίλαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.722; λόγοις ἐγὼ φιλοῦσαν οὐ στέργω φίλην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 543}}; of a **wife,** φίλην τινὰ ἄγεσθαι take as one's **wife,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.146, 288}}; ἡ Ξέρξου φ., of his **mother,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 832}}; of a **mistress,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.23, 3.11.16; φίλην ποιήσασθαί τινα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.14.}}
**I.c** φίλον, τό, **an object of love,** τὸ φ. σέβεσθαι to reverence **what** the city **loves,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 187 (Lyric poetry)}}: addressed to persons, **darling,** φ. ἐμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 952 (Lyric poetry); so φίλτατον [prev. work] 970}}; τὰ φίλτατα **one's nearest and dearest, dear ones,** such as wife and children, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 851, “Eu.” 216, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 366, “OC” 1110, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 16}}: see at {φίλτατο; τἀμὰ φίλα, τὰ σὰ φ}., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ion” 523 (troch.), 613.}}
**I.d** οἱ πρῶτοι φίλοι, a title at the Ptolemaic court, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 99.3, “PTeb.” 11.4 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; or simply οἱ φ. τοῦ βασιλέως {{Refs|“OGI” 100.1; or οἱ φ. alone, [prev. work] 115.4; τῶν φ. και διοικητοῦ}} one of the king's **friends** and dioecetes, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 79.56 (2nd c.BC).}}
**I.2** of things, **pleasant, welcome,** δόσις ὀλίγη τε φ. τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.208, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.167: with _dative_ _person_, αἰεὶ γάρ τοι ἔρις τε φίλη 5.891, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.248, 13.295; οὐ φίλα τοι ἐρέω 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.104}}; δαίμοσιν πράσσειν φίλα their **pleasure,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 660, compare below 11.}}
**I.2.b** frequently as _predicate_, φίλον ἐστί or γίγνεταί μοι **pleases** me, it is **after** my own **heart,** εἴ πού τοι φίλον ἐστί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.320}}; μὴ φ. Διὶ πατρὶ γένοιτο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 316, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.387; εἰ τόδε πᾶσι φ. καὶ ἡδὺ γένοιτο 4.17; καί τοι φ. ἔπλετο θυμῷ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.145, etc.; τοῦτο μὲν ἴτω ὅπῃ τῷ θεῷ φίλον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 19a}}: less frequently with _infinitive_, οὐ μὲν Τυδέϊ γ᾽ ὧδε φίλον πτωσκαζέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.372; πεφιδέσθαι ἐνὶ φρεσὶ φίλτερον ἦεν Τρώων 21.101, compare 24.334, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.378; so ταῦτα δαίμονί κοω φίλον ἦν οὕτω γενέσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87, compare 108, 4.97: rarely with}}_participle_, εἰ τόδ᾽ αὐτῷ φιλον κεκλημένῳ if it **please** him to be so called, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 161 (Lyric poetry)}}: agreeing with _plural_, αἰεί τοι τὰ κάκ᾽ ἐστὶ φίλα φρεσὶ μαντεύεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.107, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.15; ἔνθα φίλ᾽ ὀπταλέα κρέα ἔδμεναι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.345; σοὶ δ᾽ ἔργα φίλ᾽ ἔστω μέτρια κοσμεῖν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 306.}}
**I.2.c** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and early Poets, **one's own**; frequently of limbs, life, etc., φίλον δ᾽ ἐξαίνυτο θυμόν he took away **dear** life, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.155, compare 22.58; κατεπλήγη φίλον ἦτορ 3.31; εἰς ὅ κε.. μοι φίλα γούνατ᾽ ὀρώρῃ 9.610; φίλον κατὰ λαιμόν 19.209}}; especially of one's nearest kin, πατὴρ φ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|22.408, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20 a. 11; ἄλοχος φ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.480: compare φίλτατος: as a standing epithet when no affection is implied, μητρὶ φίλῃ Ἀλθαίῃ χωόμενος κῆρ}} angry with **his own** mother, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.555}}: simply to denote possession, φίλα εἵματα {{Refs|2.261}}; φ. πόνος **their wonted** labour, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.20.}}
**I.2.d** applied to the numbers {{Refs 4th c.AD+|284 and 220, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “in Nicomachi arithmeticam introductionem” p.35P.}}
**II** less frequently (chiefly _poetry_) in _active_ sense, **loving, friendly,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.313, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.775: with _genitive_, φίλαν ξένων ἄρουραν}} **friendly** to strangers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.8, compare “P.” 3.5}}: of things, **kindly, pleasing,** φίλα φρεσὶ μήδεα εἰδώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.325}}; φίλα φρονέειν τινί feel **kindly,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.219; φ. ἐργάζεσθαί τινι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.210; φ. εἰδέναι τινί 3.277}}; φ. ποιέεσθαί τινι deal with one in **friendly** fashion, do one a **pleasure,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.152, 5.37.}}
**II.2** **fond of** a thing, **attached to,** ἄλλων νόμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 543; δειλίας φίλον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 604d.}}
**III** _adverb_ φίλως, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, φίλως χ᾽ ὁρόῳτε}} ye would **fain** see it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.347, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 45, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 247 (Lyric poetry), [1591], etc.}}; φ. ἐμοί **in a manner dear** or **pleasing** to me, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1581.}}
**III.2** **in a friendly, kindly spirit,** τήνδε τὴν πόλιν φ. εἰπών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 758; φ. δέχεσθαί τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 988c.}}
**IV** φίλος has several forms of comparison:
**IV.1** _comparative_ φιλίων [λῐ], ον, _genitive_ ονος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.351, 24.268}}: _superlative_ φίλιστος, η, ον, interpolated in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 842.}}
**IV.2** _comparative_ φίλτερος, _superlative_ φίλτατος, see entry.
**IV.3** _comparative_ φιλαίτερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.29, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 58}}: _superlative_ φιλαίτατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.3.8, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.98.}}
**IV.4** regular _comparative_ φιλώτερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.18 codices, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 146.}}
**IV.5** also as _comparative_, μᾶλλον φίλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 219, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 886; φ. μᾶλλον 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.1.4}}; _superlative_, μάλιστα φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.17.}}

5385:
** φῐλοσοφ-ία**, ἡ,
**love of knowledge, pursuit there of, speculation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.209, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61a, “Grg.” 484c, +others; ἡ φ. κτῆσις ἐπιστήμης [prev. author] “Euthd.” 288d}}; defined as ἄσκησις ἐπιτηδείου τέχνης, Stoic. in {{Refs|“Placita Philosophorum” 1 “Prooem.” 2.}}
**2** **systematic, methodical treatment** of a subject, ἐμπειρίᾳ μέτιθι καὶ φιλοσοφίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.35}}; ἡ περὶ τὰς ἔριδας φ. **scientific treatment** of argumentation, {{Refs|[prev. author] 10.6}}; ἡ περὶ τοὺς λόγους φ. the **study** of oratory, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.10: _plural_, οἱ ἐν ταῖς φ. πολὺν χρόνον διατρίψαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 172c; τέχναι καὶ φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.67.}}
**3** **philosophy,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 11.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 414b, etc.; ἱστορία φ. ἐστὶν ἐκ παραδειγμάτων 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 11.2:— 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator usually prefixes the _Article_, 2.51, 5.84, 7.45 (but compare 2.35 above)}}; sometimes also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus and 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, as 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 482a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 993b20, “EN” 1177a25}}, and so later, διὰ τῆς φ. καὶ κενῆς ἀπάτης {{NT|NT.Col.2.8}}; but more frequently without _Article_, τοῖς ἐν φιλοσοφίᾳ ζῶσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 68c, +others, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1341b28, +others (compare Πλάτων καὶ φ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.176d)}}; except when an _adjective_ or some qualifying word is added to ἡ θεία φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 239b; ἐκείνου τῇ φ. [prev. author] “Ly.” 213d; ἡ περὶ τὰ ἀνθρώπεια φ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1181b15; ἡ τῶν Ἰταλικῶν φ. [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 987a31 (and _plural_, αἱ εἰρημέναι φ. [prev. work] 29)}}; so later ἡ Ἰωνικὴ φ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.122; ἡ δογματική, Ἀκαδημαϊκή, σκεπτικὴ φ. 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.4, etc.; ὁ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς ἐν ἀρχῇ τῆς φ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.607c, etc.; especially ἡ πρώτη φ.}}**metaphysic,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1026a24, compare 18.}}

5386:
** φῐλόσοφος**, ὁ,
**lover of wisdom**; Pythagoras called himself φιλόσοφος, not σοφός, Cic {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Tusc.” 5.3.9, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius “Prooem.” 12; τὸν φ. σοφίας φήσομεν ἐπιθυμητὴν εἶναι πάσης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 475b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.271; ὁ ὡς ἀληθῶς φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 64e}} {φιλοσπῆλυγξ}; φ. φύσει, τὴν φύσιν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 376c}}; φ. τῇ ψυχῇ, opposed to φιλόπονος τῷ σώματι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.40}}: used of all **men of education and learning,** joined with φιλομαθής and φιλόλογος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 376c, 582e}}; opposed to σοφιστής, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 13.6, 9}}; later, **academician,** of the members of the Museum at Alexandria, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 712 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**2** **philosopher,** i. e. **one who speculates on truth and reality,** οἱ ἀληθινοὶ φ., defined as οἱ τῆς ἀληθείας φιλοθεάμονες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 475e}}; ὁ φιλόσοφος, of Aristotle, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.115b}}; ὁ σκηνικὸς φ., of Euripides, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.561a}}; as the butt of Comedy texts, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 71.1, 3rd c.BC: Bato Comicus 5.11, 4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 4, 3rd c.BC: Phoenicides Comicus 4.16.}}
**II** as _adjective_, **loving knowledge, philosophic,** ἄνδρες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 35; ἀνήρ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 64d; τὸ φ. γένος [prev. author] “R.” 501e; φ. φύσις [prev. work] 494a; ψυχή [prev. work] 486b}}; διάνοια{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 527b; πειθώ 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.269 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; σύνεσις [prev. work]p.211S. (_comparative_); οἱ φιλοσοφώτατοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 498a, compare “IG” 5(1).598 (from Sparta).}}
**II.2** of arguments, sciences, etc., **scientific, philosophic,** λόγοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 257b}}; λόγοι -ώτεροι, of **instructive** speeches, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.271; -ώτερον ποίησις ἱστορίας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1451b5}}; τὸ φ., opposed to τὸ θυμοειδές, as an element of the soul, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 411e, but ={φιλοσοφία}, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.355b.}}]
**II.3** **ingenious,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 571 (hexameters).}}
**III** _adverb_ -φως, διακεῖσθαι πρός τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.277; φ. ἔχειν περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 91a, compare 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 13.20.4, etc.}}; opposed to ῥητορικῶς, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.134 S}}.; _comparative_ **-ωτέρως**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Sensu” 436a20}}; **-ώτερον** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 7.8.3. [5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus [prev. cited] has the penultimate long, nowhere else found in poetry.]}}

5389:
** φῐλοτῑμ-έομαι**,
_future_ ήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 234a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.103}}; later, **-ηθήσομαι**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.18}} codices: _aorist_ ἐφιλοτιμήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 182b, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.44, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.42; later, ἐφιλοτιμησάμην 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 20.8.2, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 3.1 (written ἐφιλοτειμήσετο “Ephes.” 3 No.13)}}: _perfect_ πεφιλοτίμημαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.24, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 2.1.32}}:—_perfect_ in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.446 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}: (φιλότιμος):—**love** or **seek after honour,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.146a, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator [same place], 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.103, etc.}}: hence, **to be ambitious, emulous,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 281}}; φ. ὅτι.. **to be jealous** because.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.7, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.21}}; φ. πρὸς ἀλλήλους, πρὸς τοὺς ἄλλους, **vie emulously with, rival,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 178e, “Phdr.” 234a, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 29.14.}}
**2** the object of ambition, etc., is mostly added with a _preposition_, φ. ἐπί τινι **to place one's fame** in a thing, **glory** or **pride oneself** upon it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 553d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.12, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.42; ἐπὶ τοῖς πεπραγμένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.46, +others; ἔν τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 182b; ὑπὲρ τῆς δόξης 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.93}}; περὶ τῶν καλῶν **contend in rivalry** for, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.760c}}; περὶ τὴν θήραν, δεῖπνα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.18, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 9; ἀφ᾽ ἑτέρων ἀρετῶν [prev. author] 2.819c: with _neuter_ pronoun in _accusative_, πρὸς ἃ ἐγὼ φιλοτιμοῦμαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.5: with _accusative_ _cognate_, φ. φιλοτιμίας ἀκάρπους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.830e; τὴν ἀγαθὴν ἔριν 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.10.5}}; φ. πρὸς τὴν πόλιν **show patriotic zeal** for.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 140, compare “IG” 22.1176.26, etc.; εἰς τὴν αὔξησιν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.50, compare 25, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 4.44, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor [prev. cited]}}
**II** with _infinitive_, **strive eagerly to** do a thing, **endeavour earnestly, aspire,** οἳ πάνυ ἂν φιλοτιμηθεῖεν φίλῳ σοι χρῆσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.3, compare “Oec.” 21.6, “PPetr.” 3p.115 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 578.2 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; φιλοτιμούμενοι ἐπιδείκνυσθαιπρὸς ἅπαντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 232a}}: with _participle_, φ. ἐλέγχων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 336c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 9.6}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **to be anxious that**.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.25: with _accusative_, ἀεὶ ἕν γέ τι φιλοτιμούμενος [prev. author] “Oec.” 4.24: with ὅπως, καλῶς ἂν ποιήσαις φιλοτιμηθεὶς ὅπως ἂν παρὰ τοῦ Θεοδώρου λάβῃς τὰ ἐπιστόλια “PCair.Zen.” 41.19 (3rd c.BC),compare “PMich.Zen.” 6.3 (3rd c.BC).}}
**III** with _dative_ _of things_, **present with** a thing, χρήμασί τινας see reading in {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Gothico” 1.5}}: but with _accusative_ _of things_, **lavish upon,** τινί τι {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 1.1; πόλεμος.. νίκας ἀδίκους φ. 6th c.AD: Choricius Rhetor 35.71 p.410F.-R.}}

5394:
** φλογ-ίζω**,
_future_ φλογίσω [prev. author = 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus], but **-ιῶ**{{LXX|LXX.Psa.96(97).3}}:— ={φλέγω}, **set on fire, burn,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1199 (_anapaest meter_), LXX [same place], +others}}: **singe,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1233}}:—_passive_, **to be set on fire, blaze, flame,** φλογιζόμενον ἅλιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 95 (Lyric poetry)}}: **to be burnt up, consumed,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 397a29}}: _metaphorically_, of the tongue, {{NT|NT.Jam.3.6 (_active_ and _passive_).}}
**II** _intransitive_, **burn, blaze,** {{LXX|LXX.Exo.9.24.}}

5395:
** φλόξ**, ἡ,
_genitive_ φλογός: (φλέγω):— **flame** of fire, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.71, etc.; δεινὴ δὲ φλὸξ ὦρτο θεείου καιομένοιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.135; τῆς δὲ [νηὸς] κατ᾽ ἀσβέστη κέχυτο φλόξ 16.123; κατὰ πῦρ ἐκάη καὶ φλὸξ ἐμαράνθη 9.212; more fully, φλὸξ Ἡφαίστοιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.88, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” [same place]; πυρός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.225, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 8, “Heracl.” 914 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 83b, etc. (but also φλογὸς αἰθέριον πῦρ 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.56)}}; φλογὸς σπέρμα, of live charcoal, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.48}}; ἀναιθύσσειν, θύειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 344, “IT” 1331}}; ἐγείρειν, παρακαλεῖν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.24, “Cyr.” 7.5.23; ἐμβαλεῖν τινι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 4, “Rh.” 120; σβέσαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.77; φ. ἀπέσσυτο 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 859; ἀπορρέουσα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 67c; φλογὸς ἀποσβεσθείσης [prev. work] 58c}}: later in _plural_, **flames, meteors,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 341b2, “Mu.” 392b3, 400a30, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 178, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 74.48.}}
**2** **fire** as an element, φλογὸς αἶσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 12.2; φ. ἱλάειρα 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 85.}}
**3** of other kinds of **flame,** φ. κεραυνία, οὐρανία, of lightning, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1017, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 144 (_anapaest meter_)}}; of the **heat** of the sun, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 22, “Pers.” 505, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 696}}; **flash** of a miraculous cloud, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.206}}; of precious stones, ψυχρὰ φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 123.5}}; the **blade** of a sword, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Josh.3.22, 2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX, 2nd c.AD(?): Theodotion LXX.1Ki.17.7.}}
**4** in similes and metaphors, φλογὶ εἴκελος, ἶσος, of **fiery** warriors, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.330, 39}}; φ. οἴνου **the fiery strength** of wine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 758; φ. πήματος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 166 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **wallflower, Cheiranthus Cheiri,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.6.2.}}

5396:
** φλῠᾱρ-έω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **φλῠηρέω**:— **talk nonsense, play the fool,** ταῦτα λέγουσι φλυηρέοντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.131, compare 7.104, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 545 (_anapaest meter_), “V.” 85, “Pl.” 360, 575 (_anapaest meter_); παῦσαι φλυαρῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 213.1 (troch.); φλυαρεῖς πρός με 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 146; οὐ φλυαρῶ (parenthetically) 'no joke', 'seriously', [prev. work] 153}}: with _accusative_ _cognate_, φάσκοντα.. ἀεροβατεῖν καὶ ἄλλην πολλὴν φλυαρίαν φλυαροῦντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 19c; πολλὰ φλυηρέεις 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.103; ταῦτα φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.75; τοιαῦτα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 337b; ἄφες ἂ φλυαρεῖς ταῦτα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 313}}. with _participle_, οὐ μὴ φλυαρήσεις ἔχων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 202; φλυαρεῖς ἔχων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 490e; ἔχων φ. [prev. author] “Euthd.” 295c; Αἰσχύλος φ. φάσκων [prev. author] “Smp.” 180a; Δερκυλίδας φ. διατρίβων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.18.}}
**II** _transitive_, **prate against,** ἡμᾶς {{NT|NT.3John.10}}:—_passive_, φλυαρηθέντες καὶ κατακερτομηθέντες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.599}}; **to be made a fool of,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.173.}}

5397:
** φλύᾱρ-ος** [ῠ], ὁ,
**silly talk, foolery, nonsense,** τἄλλα πάντ᾽ ἐστὶ φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 365 (_anapaest meter_), compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 541.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365e, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 2, etc.}}: _plural_, **fooleries,** πολλῶν φλυάρων καὶ ταὧν ἀντάξια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 27.}}
**II** **tattler, babbler,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 369a, NT.1Tim.5.13, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.5, etc.}}: as _adjective_, ἡ φ. φιλοσοφία {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.4Mac.5.10; φ. λόγος 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 26; φ. γλῶττα 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.69}}: _comparative_ φλυαρότερος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.19.10.}} _adverb_ φλυάρως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 855.}}

5398:
** φοβερός**, ά, όν,
(φόβος) **fearful,** whether _active_ or _passive_:
**I** _active_, **causing fear, terrible,** χρηστήρια φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.139, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 127 (_anapaest meter_), “Th.” 78 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; ὅμιλος πλήθει -ώτατος **formidable** only from numbers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.98 (but τὰ τῷ πλήθει φ.}} things which are **fearful to** the multitude, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.7, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67e): with}}_infinitive_, φ. ἰδεῖν, φ. προσιδέσθαι, **fearful** to behold, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 27 (_anapaest meter_), 48 (_anapaest meter_); φ. εἰσιδεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 127 (Lyric poetry); φ. προσπολεμῆσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.22; φ. Πολυδεύκεα πὺξ ἐρεθίζειν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.2.}}
**I.2** **regarded with fear,** especially with respect to consequences, οὔτε ὅρκος φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.83}}; ἵππος φ. μὴ ἀνήκεστόν τι ποιήσῃ a horse **that makes one fear** he will do some mischief, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 6.15; σεμνότερος εἶναι καὶ φοβερώτερος δοκεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.18; φοβεροὶ ἦσαν μὴ ποιήσειαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.2; τοῖς πολεμίοις φοβερώτεροι [prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 4.11, compare “Ages.” 11.10 (_superlative_): τριήρης φοβερὸν πολεμίοις [prev. author] “Oec.” 8.8; τὸ πρὸ τῶν λυπηρῶν [προσδόκημα] φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 32c; φοβερώτατον ἐρημία 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.9; τὸ φ.}}**terror, danger,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lac.” 9.1}}; τῶν φοβερῶν ὄντων τῇ πόλει γενέσθαι the things which were **dreaded** as like ly to happen.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 1.4.17}}; φοβερόν [ἐστι] μὴ.. there is **reason to dread** that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hier.” 1.12, compare “Cyr.” 7.5.22}}; ἀγγέλλεσθαι ἐπὶ τὸ φοβερώτατον to be **fearfully exaggerated,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.57.}}
**I.3** Rhet., of style, **impressive, awe-inspiring,** τὸ κάλλος τὸ Θουκυδίδου φ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pomp.” 3; τὸ φ. [prev. author] “Lys.” 13; Ὅμηρος παίζων -ώτερος 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 130.}}
**II** _passive_, **afraid, timid,** ἐκτέταμαι φοβερὰν φρένα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 153 (Lyric poetry), compare 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 97, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 245; ὄμμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 620: opposed to θαρσαλέος, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3 19 (_comparative_)}}; φ. τὴν ψυχήν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 7.25}}; σκοπεῖν εἰ φοβεροί (i.e. οἱ πῶλοι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 413d; φ. ποιεῖν τινα [prev. author] “Lg.” 647c; φ. εἰς τὸ τολμᾶν [prev. work] 649d.}}
**II.2** **caused by fear, troubled, panic,** ἀναχώρησις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.128; φοβερὰ ὄσσοις ὁμίχλα προσῇξε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 144 (Lyric poetry)}}; φ. φροντίδες **anxious** thoughts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 127b.}}
**III** _adverb_ **-ρῶς** **threateningly, in a terrifying manner,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.15, cf. LXX.3Mac.5.45, etc.}}: _comparative_, -ώτερον φθέγγεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.10}}: _superlative_, -ώτατα ἰδεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.3.5.}}
**III.2** **timidly,** -ώτατα ἔχειν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 8.20.}}

5399:
** φοβέω**,
_3rd.pers._ _plural_ _imperative_ φοβεόντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.235}}: _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ φοβέεσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 162}}: _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 357 (Lyric poetry), (ἐκ-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126 (uncertain)}}: _aorist_ ἐφόβησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.15, etc.:}} —_passive_ and _middle_, _Ionic dialect_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2 singular φοβέαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.39}}; _Ionic dialect_ _imperative_ φοβεῦ or φοβέο, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.9, 7.52}}: _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _imperfect_ φοβέοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.41}}: _future_ φοβήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|22.250, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 649c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.23, etc.; φοβηθήσομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.30, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 40, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Zeux.” 9, variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 470a}}: _aorist_ _passive_ ἐφοβήθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.27, etc., _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐφόβηθεν or φόβηθεν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.326, 5.498}}; _aorist_ _middle_ ἐφοβησάμην only {{Refs|“Anacreontea” 31.11}}: _perfect_ πεφόβημαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.510, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐπεφόβηντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.32, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.50}}, _Epic dialect_ πεφοβήατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.206.}}
**A** _active_, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (never in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”)}} always in the sense **put to flight,** [ἴρηξ] ἐφόβησε κολοιούς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.583}}; [Ζεὺς] καὶ ἄλκιμον ἄνδρα φοβεῖ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 689; Τρώων οὓς ἐφόβησας 22.11; φοβῆσαίτε στίχας ἀνδρῶν 17.505; σὸς δόλος.. ἐφόβησε δὲ λαούς 15.15; σέ γέ φημι.. δουρὶ φοβῆσαι 20.187; once in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, φοβέεσκον ἐπὶ χθονὶ φῦλ᾽ ἀνθρώπων [prev. cited]}}
**II** **terrify, alarm,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.235, etc.; μὴ φίλους φόβει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 262; ᾧ μή 'στι δρῶντι τάρβος οὐδ᾽ ἔπος φοβεῖ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 296, compare 1013, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 572 (Lyric poetry); ἡ δύναμις τῶν νέων φοβοῦσά τινας 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.2; αἱ κάμηλοι ἐφόβουν τους ἵππους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.48; ρὸν Ἀλκιβιάδην ἐφόβουν, μὴ.. ἐπαγάγωνται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.45; with _dative_ modi, λόγοις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 215 (troch.); μεγαληγορίαισι φρένας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 357 (Lyric poetry)}}; τῷ μὲν Τισσαφέρνει τοὺς Ἀθηναίους φ., ἐκείνοις δὲ τὸν Τισσαφέρνην **to frighten** the Athenians with T., and T. with the Athenians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.82}}; with _participle_, λέγοντες φ. τινάς **by** saying, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 1.8; λέγοντες ὡς ἥξει βασιλεύς D 14.25: _absolutely_, πόνος ὁ μὴ φοβῶν κράτιστος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 864 (Lyric poetry), φοβήσαντες κατεστήσαντο τὴν πολιτείαν}}**by terror,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 551b.}}
**II.2** c._accusative_ _of things_, **threaten with,** φ. λιμόν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.51.}}
**B** _passive_ and _middle_, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always in the sense **to be put to flight** (compare {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia A 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.223, +others), once in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, κύνες.. διὰ σταθμοῖο φόβηθεν 16.163; frequently in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, ὑπέμειναν ἀολλέες οὐδὲ φόβηθεν 5.498; τοὶ δ᾽ ἐφόβηθεν.. θεσπεσίῳ ὁμάδῳ 16.294; κὰμ μέσσον πεδίον φοβέοντο βόες ὣς ἅς τε λέων ἐφόβησε 11.172}}; also _participle_, μὴ καὶ πεφοβημένος ἔλθης {{Refs|10.510, compare 15.4, 21.606; φοβηθεὶς δύσεθ᾽ ἁλὸς κατὰ κῦμα}}**in flight,** {{Refs|6.135; βῆ δὲ φοβηθείς 22.137: ὑπό τινος φοβέεσθαι}} **to flee** before him,{{Refs|8.149; ὑπό τινι 15.637; with _accusative_, οὔ σ᾽ ἔτι.. φοβήσομαι ὡς τὸ πάρος περ 22.250.}}
**B.II** **to be seized with fear, be affrighted,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.70, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1166, etc.}} —Constr.,
**B.II.1** _absolutely_, πεφόβημαι πτηνῆς ὡς ὄμμα πελείας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 139 (_anapaest meter_)}}; φοβηθέντες ᾤχοντο φεύγοντες flying **in terror,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.43}}; ἃ μὴ οἶδα.. οὐδέποτε φοβήσομαι οὐδὲ φεύξομαι, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 29b, etc.: with _dative_ instrum., μάστιγι φ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 37 (_anapaest meter_): with _accusative_ _cognate_, φ. αἰσχροὺς φόβους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 360b; ἐφοβήθησαν φόβον μέγαν NT.Mark.4.41; τὸν φόβον αὐτῶν μὴ φοβηθῆτε NT.1Pet.3.14.}}
**B.II.2** followed by Preps., φ. ἀπό τινος **to be afraid of** one (probably a Hebraism), {{LXX+NT|LXX.Lev.26.2, LXX.Jer.1.8, NT.Matt.10.28, NT.Luke.12.4}}; ἔκ τινος **from** some cause, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 671}}; εἴς τι **to be alarmed** at a thing, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “OT” 980; πρός τι [prev. author] “Tr.” 1211, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.Es” 4, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 50.18}}; ἐπί τινι **fear** for.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 14.4}}; but φ. ἀμφὶ γυναικί **fear** about.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.62; περὶ ἡμῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.35, etc.; περί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 275b (περὶ σφίσιν αὐτοῖς τὸ κατάδηλον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.123); περὶ χωρίῳ [prev. author] 2.90; ὑπὲρ τῶν μελλόντων 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.36; περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 404e; πρὸς ἀνδρὸς ἢ τέκνων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 150.}}
**B.II.3** followed by a _relative_ clause, φοβεῑσθαι μὴ.. **fear** lest a thing will be.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 770 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 606 (troch.), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.95, etc.}}; φ. ὅπως μὴ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.3}}; φ. μὴ οὐ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 16.6}}; frequently c._accusative_ followed by μή, ταῦτ᾽ οὖν φοβοῦμαι, μὴ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 550, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.2}}; φ. τοὺς οὐσίαν κεκτημένους, μὴ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 232c, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.88, etc.}}; φ. ὑπέρ τινος, μὴ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 387c}}; with _infinitive_ followed by μή, φοβοίμην ἂν τῷ ἡγεμόνι ἕπεσθαι, μὴ ἀγάγῃ κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 143e, “Grg.” 457e: also φ. ὅτι..}} ={φ. μὴ}.., in a more positive sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.1, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 52.26}}; φ. τόδε, ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.67 (but φ. τὸ κάεσθαι, ὅτι ἀλγεινόν because.., 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 479a)}}: διὰ τοῦτο φ. τινας, ὅτι.. Isocrates {{Refs|6.60}}; less frequently φ. ὡς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.12; φ. πῶς χρὴ.. [prev. work] 4.5.19; φ. εἰ δεήσει.. [prev. work] 6.1.17.}}
**B.II.4** with _infinitive_ with _Article_, φ. τὸ ἀποθνῄσκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 522e, etc.}}: more frequently with _infinitive_ alone, **fear to** do, **be afraid of** doing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 46 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 253 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 628, etc.}}: with _infinitive_ _future_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.105.}}
**B.II.5** with _accusative_ _person_, **stand in awe of, dread,** δαίμονας τοὺς ἐνθάδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 893; στρατὸν Ἀχαιῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1250; τοὺς ἄνω θεούς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 927b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.16, etc.; τὰς κύνας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.16, etc.}}
**B.II.6** with _accusative_ _of things_, **fear** or **fear about** a thing, βρόμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 476; τὸ προσέρπον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 227 (Lyric poetry); μέμψιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1057; τὸ τοιοῦτον σῶμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 239d; δουλείαν καὶ δεσμόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.24.}}
**B.II.7** with _genitive_, πεφοβημένος νυκτός, θαλάσσης, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 290, 766.}}
**B.II.8** with _participle_, προδιδοὺς φοβηθείς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 17.}}
